[
    {
        "review": "Anker Soundcore P30i buds.First and foremost I want to be clear that I am rating these buds purely on their own merit.  Without their software, without any Equalizer, just on how they perform straight out of the box.Baseus and Tozo and Jlabs are some of the names when we think of Budget Bass Buds.  And for those who are looking for good, clean bass... I'd compare these with Jlabs.  For bass, right out of the box, playing music with bass in it, they deliver a clear, audible thump, without the actual pounding vibration.  Right now, I'm listening to Cry Little Sister, from the Lost Boys soundtrack, at volume EIGHT.  (That's not even halfway up my volume bar from my phone).And the base tune soundstage is really, really good.  Especially for a bass tuned base.  The vocals, and background vocals are right up front.  Crisp, clear, and don't sound like they are being overwhelmed by anything.  And I can definitely hear every hit from the bass drums, as well as count the cords on the keyboard.Now I'm at 12, and those bass drums are starting to pound.  Other than it's significantly louder, I'm still hearing good clarity of the different ranges.  I can't say this for my Tozo T18 Crystal buds, without tuning.My Baseus BP1 pros would be creeping over the midrange at this volume on the base tune.Ok, now I'm at 18.  A lot louder than I usually listen to.  The highs are much more prominent over the lows.  Which is the opposite of how a lot of bass speakers work.Honestly, this kind of division of the ranges from a single driver speaker is kind of surprising, especially at 20 to 25 dollars.Battery life is good too.  I have a 10 hour day.  2 hours on the bus, 8 hour shift at work.  With the ANC turned on, they need a quick charge at lunch to carry me home.  With ANC turned off, yeah, I can get back in the door before they need to be charged.  Sometimes just barely, if I've had them cranked up.ANC- on the bus they do pretty good.  Cuts down a lot of the engine and sound noise from the bus.  I can still hear people talking next to me, but at about half what it would be without the buds in.At work?  Well it's so noisy at work even full blown ear muffs aren't making it good.Feel- ok this is subjective, because my right ear canal is different from my left.  In my right, I can feel a bit of pressure when I put them in.  But once in, they're quite comfortable, and I can fall asleep with them in, as a side-sleeper.Bluetooth is good.  Stays connected as long as I'm in range, and range is definitely acceptable.  Definitely better than the chap 17 dollar Onn buds I have.  Easy to pair also.My only real complaint is no front panel to show the charge on the batteries.All said the bass isn't as deep on the base soundstage as Tozo delivers, but it's cleaner.  Tozo also offers much better options in their software, as does Baseus, in some ways.I DO like a lot that SoundCore went the route of making the stems wider, instead of longer.  Having long hair, it's less likely to get caught on the stems, and pull them out.Speaking of pulling them out.  I decided to do some headbanging.  My glasses flew off but these buds didn't budge.Overall though I have no regrets at all, buying these very good sounding set of inexpensive buds."
    },
    {
        "review": "After spending $1000 in headphones/earbuds in the last 3 years (1 Sony $390, 1 Samsung $350, 1 Google $199), and being disappointed with \"cost/benefit\" on all 3, I decided to give a shot at this absolutely inexpensive option.But before pressing \"buy now\" and just purchase it, I decided to do very same work I did when buying the other 3 premium devices, and took my time, reading reviews, educating myself about the company and it's commitment with customers/users, and finally, I went deep into the specs of the hardware, software, and the mobile app (this last one definitely a big plus).At this point you might say: \"it's a freaking $30 Bluetooth earbud\" just buy, use it, and if doesn't deliver it's promises, then just return it and add another \"zero\" to the original $30 and buy a premium device.Well, not so fast... I was intrigued by how good it all sounded and I want to use this whole experience to test an assumption I've been carrying for a short while, which is that we've been all scammed and abused with the so called premium priced devices, in other words , if you want the goods, you have to pay much higher price for it. So, I looked into this as an opportunity to unmask my theory and for that I had to put the \"works\" in place.After blending and filtering all the information gathered (the process even included \"unclutter suspicious\" reviews), the conclusion was obvious: despite all looking very standard, there was a positively intriguing desire to give it a try, and the result couldn't be more satisfying.Design looks average initially, but it actually reflects a distinct personality with a touch of elegance, specially with the colors available (green was my option and I was not disappointed).The hardware is absolutely underestimated for the price tag, meaning, it has the ability to deliver a sound quality that matches my Sony WH1000 (I listen to audio-music/books/podcasts an average of 6 hours a day) with crystal clear sound nuances, including a sharp treble and a solid bass, and yesssss, a battery life that will get you listening music and talking on the phone for a few days just by a one full charge on the charging case.Call quality... isn't this feature one of the most important components that will drive us towards or far away from a device...Yes!!!! So here it is: Shockingly amazing call quality. They built this right, and the balance between the software and the hardware, is in perfect harmony, 4 mics and AI, cleaning all the \"distractions\" away from phone conversations, and music listening without any disappointment.And here is the \"cherry in the cake: THE MOBILE APP. Again, another well built component of the whole experience. Better than my Sony, Google, and yes, heartbreaking, my Samsung (I'm a big fan of Samsung products).Summarizing, it's not Bluetooth 6.0, not 14mm sound shell, but it's absolutely a shocker of a quality device that only cost me $30, and it gives me peace of mind with call and music-sound quality. Noise Canceling absolutely under your control, through an app designed with everything you need to make it \"your way\", and satisfy your desire to listen music like a music producer ;)Ps: no one paid me to write this review, and despite the fact that I don't ever right a review, here is one I hope it will add value to your decision."
    },
    {
        "review": "I’ve bought a lot of headphones. These are by far the best. They’re a great fit. They’re comfortable. They sound as good as the AirPods my daughter lost. I haven’t had any complaints from anyone I talked to on the phone. And I can definitively say that the noise cancellation is better than any earbuds I’ve ever used. And then there’s the app. I’ve actually never had any earbuds with an app like this. I personally think it’s phenomenal. You can set the controls in app. There’s so much customization. And then there’s everything from meditations to bedtime stories. I’m listening to one as I wrote this. It’s literally a professional reader. It’s amazing. It makes me feel like a kid again. I started listening to see if my child would like it. But it was so good I kept listening. Just the app alone is worth the price, which is wild. I’ve seen some reviews saying the opposite of everything I’ve written. I don’t know what to say. These are just amazing inexpensive headphones. Someone would have to have some interesting karma not to enjoy these headphones. These are so good in combination with the app that I hope they last for years and years to come."
    },
    {
        "review": "The title says it all. I have about 7 different earbuds and 5 over ear headphones varying in quality from okay to really good. This pair ranks up among my best. Balancing between comfort and sound quality these would be a good value at $50, at under 30 they were exceptional.The sound quality is rich and the range of sounds these guys produce is comparable to my $100 Beats.Where these really excel is in their fit. My “ear holes” as my kids call them are pretty big so not a lot of earbuds fit well AND stay in. These are the only ones I own that will stay in while sleeping, working out or running I can wear them for long periods of time comfortably and without the frequent adjustment even my Bose pair require.Noise cancellation is okay, but even at just okay, it’s still more than adequate for most applications.For the combination of all the elements, and the price, these are an excellent value."
    },
    {
        "review": "Bought these and figured I’d give them a try after I saw lots of positive reviews at a super affordable price. I was immediately impressed with these. I guess I’ve always bought the cheap ear buds over the years, and even still rock a wired 3.5mm pair of skull candy buds I had for about 12 years, because these blew me away with how much better sounding they were. These have incredible bass and tone and even have the ability to customize an equalizer to your liking in the app! (No you don’t need the app to make these work.) The app even lets you customize the tap controls which is awesome. They work dual and single, if you want to keep one in the case. Battery life seems decent so far, of course that fades on anything over time. The feature that impressed me the most was the auto noise canceling you can do with these. You can customize this control with custom taps too! When noise canceling is off, the built in microphones allow “pass through” noise which is stereo and directional. While the audio quality of this part isn’t great, it allows you to hear and have conversation while the ear buds are in, while also still being able to play music as normal if you want. What’s wild is when you turn the noise canceling on, they shut all the mics off and it’s COMPLETELY quiet. I’ve never had ear buds that are THIS quiet and noise canceling. The first time I used this feature was in the gym while music inside was blaring. When I turned noise canceling on, I looked up and thought someone instantly cut the music off in the gym entirely cause I couldn’t hear any of it. It was like the power in the whole building went out it was so quiet. For the price of these, I’d say you can’t beat them."
    },
    {
        "review": "As someone who has owned several pairs of Apple earbuds, I was impressed with what you get for around $23-30. I bought a pair of these as a back up pair for a big vacation I was taking. The most amazing thing about these is the battery life. They almost lasted the entirety of my cross ocean flight without recharging them in the case yet. The noise cancellation is pretty good. The sound quality is also pretty good. I like how there’s a built-in stand for your phone too. These earbuds are not as good as the ones made by the fruit company, but they also cost around $30. I’m happy with them. I even got a second pair as a back up for work.The other cool thing about them, which I didn’t realize until I was playing around with them some more is that you can customize them with an app on your phone. I didn’t know there were all these sound profile options. There is also a surround sound option. It’s not spatial audio, but still pretty good. They pair really easily with all my devices. They connect pretty seamlessly with my iPhone. I don’t even have to choose to connect. They just do it on their own. I also like how you can customize the options for the taps on them. I found it really easy to set up taps for pausing and alerting Siri.In summary, these are pretty darn good earbuds, especially considering the price point. I’m impressed by all their features. The noise cancellation works well. And the battery life is killer. That is by far the best part of them."
    },
    {
        "review": "An absolute beast of a daily driver. These things have great sound quality, battery life, and I love the little EQ details and controls they have on the app. My favorite feature is the noise cancellation it’s very very good. These are way better than most $100+ earphones you can get on the market for a banger of a price 💸 🤑"
    },
    {
        "review": "I like these earbuds quite a bit. The sound quality is great, the voice quality is good for ear buds, and the color is cute. I like how easy the charging case is to manage, with them slotting in easily. The reason for four stars is two-fold based on that. One, I've had them about a year and they've started to lose charge more rapidly, not lasting a full work day - 8 hours - when they're supposed to last longer. The other reason is that they fall out of my ears a lot. I tried switching between the cushion sizes and it helped a little, but they'll often slide out while I am eating just from my jaw moving. I was stretching my back by bending forward the other day and had to catch one as it fell, which I've had happen a number of times.These are all fairly minor issues overall, and they are quite reliable otherwise. The noise cancelation is also pretty good. For under $30 now, and just over when I got them, a pretty solid deal all around. If you use them for exercise, though, I might recommend getting some foam earbud cushions or something else to help keep them in, or you could lose them."
    },
    {
        "review": "If you want the raw truth on the Anker Soundcore P30i by Noise Cancelling Earbuds — they’re honestly one of the better cheap earbuds you can buy right now, but they are absolutely not “premium killer” earbuds like TikTok sometimes claims.They’re good because they give you:surprisingly solid ANC,massive battery life,strong bass,decent app support,and useful featuresfor around £30–£50.But they also have clear weaknesses you notice if you’ve used:AirPods Pro,Sony WF series,Galaxy Buds,or higher-end Soundcore models.Best overallAnker Soundcore P30i by Noise Cancelling EarbudsThe good — what’s genuinely impressive1. ANC is surprisingly good for the priceThis is probably the biggest strength.The adaptive ANC cuts:bus rumble,train noise,engine hum,fans,gym background noiseway better than most cheap earbuds. Multiple reviews say the ANC feels genuinely useful rather than fake “marketing ANC.”+2Raw truth:It’s nowhere near Bose or Sony flagship level.But for £35–£50? It punches above its price.For commuting:very respectable. For flights:decent. For voices and sharp noises:average.Human voices still come through quite a bit. +12. Battery life is excellentThis part is genuinely strong.Real-world:around 6 hours with ANC,around 9–10 without ANC,huge total case battery.That’s honestly better than some expensive earbuds.+1Fast charging is also genuinely useful:10 mins ≈ about 2 hours playback.+13. Bass is BIGIf you like:rap,drill,EDM,gym music,bass-heavy Spotify playlists,you’ll probably enjoy them immediately.The 10mm drivers and BassUp tuning hit hard for cheap earbuds.+1These are tuned for fun, not studio accuracy.4. App support is actually very goodThis matters more than people think.it gives:EQ presets,custom EQ,touch control changes,ANC controls,firmware updates.And honestly: you NEED the EQ.Out of the box they’re too bassy for many people. Reddit users repeatedly say EQ tweaking improves them massively.+15. Value for money is excellentThis is where they shine.You’re getting:ANC,multipoint,app support,IP54,long battery,decent call quality,Bluetooth 5.4,for budget pricing.+1That’s hard to beat.The bad — the real weaknesses1. Sound quality is NOT audiophile levelThis is the biggest reality check.People who say:“These destroy AirPods Pro”are exaggerating heavily.The P30i sound:warm,bass-heavy,compressed sometimes,not super detailed.Vocals and instruments can sound muddy or recessed.+2If you listen to:acoustic,jazz,classical,detailed rock mixes,you’ll notice limitations quickly.2. Fit is inconsistentThis is the most common complaint.If the seal breaks:ANC gets worse,bass disappears,sound becomes thin.People with smaller ears especially complain about this.+1For some people:perfect fit. For others:constant adjustment.That’s the gamble with budget earbuds.3. Transparency mode is mediocreTransparency mode works… but sounds artificial compared to Apple/Sony.You can hear surroundings, but it doesn’t feel “natural.”Review-Rating4. Build quality feels cheapNot terrible. Just clearly plastic.The buds are lightweight and comfortable, but they do not feel premium in-hand.Review-RatingThat said: Soundcore products are usually pretty durable overall.5. Some QC inconsistency existsThis is the uncomfortable truth with cheaper earbuds.Some Reddit users reported:hiss,imbalance,ANC weirdness,random distortion,inconsistent sound between buds. +1But others say theirs are flawless.That suggests occasional quality-control inconsistency rather than a universally bad product.The weird phone-stand caseThe charging case doubles as a phone stand.Honestly? It sounds gimmicky……but people actually end up liking it.+1Useful for:trains,flights,watching TikTok/YouTube.Not revolutionary though.Who these are BEST forThese are GREAT if you:want cheap ANC,love bass,commute,go gym,watch videos,want long battery,want features without spending £100+.They are especially strong for:students,casual listeners,everyday use.Who should avoid themSkip them if you:care about ultra-clear detailed sound,hate bass-heavy tuning,have very small ears,want premium transparency mode,want elite call quality outdoors.Final raw verdictThe Anker Soundcore P30i by Noise Cancelling Earbuds are not magical flagship killers.But they are one of the best “normal person” budget earbuds around.The raw truth:ANC = good for the moneybattery = excellentbass = powerfulapp = excellentsound = fun but not refinedfit = hit or missbuild = budget plasticvalue = extremely strongIf you buy them expecting:“cheap AirPods Pro competitors”you may be disappointed.If you buy them expecting:“feature-packed £40 earbuds that do most things well”you’ll probably be impressed.AttributeAnker Soundcore P30i by Noise Cancelling EarbudsAnker Soundcore P40i by Noise Cancelling Wireless Earbudssoundcore Life P3i Hybrid Active Noise Cancelling EarbudsAnker R50i NC Noise Cancelling In Ear EarbudsSound qualityFun, bass-heavyMore refinedSimilar bassy soundDecent budget soundANCGood for priceBetter overallBasic-goodGood budget ANCBattery lifeExcellentExcellent+Very goodVery goodComfortMixed fitBetter fit for manyComfortableComfortableApp/EQExcellentExcellentExcellentGoodBuild qualityBudget plasticBetter feelBasicBasicTransparency modeAverageBetterAverageBest forEveryday budget ANCBest overall Soundcore valueCheap bass-heavy optionCheapest solid ANCPrice/valueExtremely strongBetter if discounted close to P30iCheapCheapest pickIf your budget is around:£30–£45: P30i is a very good buy.£50–£70: the Anker Soundcore P40i by Noise Cancelling Wireless Earbuds are honestly the smarter upgrade overall.Good for noise canceling if you use both earbuds or if your the person who uses 1 earbud in1 ear you can hear everhthing around you irl- good transparencyI got gotta give these S tier for inexpensive Non budget earbudsComes in many diff colourseasy to useeasy to.connect and adjustability to a TI reccomendand ir comes with a phone stand"
    },
    {
        "review": "I was torn between buying the p30i or the p31i, seeing as the prices were similar and the 31 is a newer model. Initially I liked the p30i appearance though, as the stem is not as long, as well as the case being slightly smaller and also featuring a worthwhile phone stand.I decided to buy the p30i as there was a good lightning deal, and I'm very happy with it so far. Although I have not used the p31i, I can say that the sound quality and volume capability of this p30i is amazing. I'm coming from using the Earfun Audio Pro 3, and the sound quality and volume on these are much better.One clarification I wanted to make is that the description indicates there is no multi-point connectivity. Perhaps this was introduced in a later firmware update, but there is definitely a feature called \"dual connectivity\" which allows you to connect two devices at the same time.The app is also well designed as there are a number of EQ customizations. The battery life is also excellent so far. I put these on for the first time tonight and have been listening to music for a couple of hours and just finished a phone call that lasted close to 90 minutes, and both earbuds still show 100% battery (I didn't use any noise cancellation during this time). The noise cancellation itself works very well, and for the price of these you really can't go wrong.I was tempted to get the p31i as I saw a great lightning deal that only put it a few dollars more, but I think I'll just keep these for now. The noise control and other functions work perfectly fine, and I'm able to crank the volume pretty loud on these and the bass sounds very good. Like I said, the app provides each you customizations allowing you to select from a lot of presets or customize your own sound, and this is extremely useful and helps enhance the sound in many different ways.Another thing that's nice about these is that the touch sensitivity is very accurate, and much better than other earbuds I have used."
    },
    {
        "review": "Wow it's just so goodDefinitely worth the valueSo easy to control I love how modern it is with the controlsSound clarity absolutely amazing.💯"
    },
    {
        "review": "The change from the iPhone 13 to 17 and the elimination of Lightning made new headphones necessary. This time it was allowed to be wireless. I already own the Soundcore Space One, which I really like but is far too big as a casual listener for on the go. That's why the P30i seemed interesting to me after a lot of research and at the price. Yes, they are bass-heavy, but I generally like that. That's why I used Koss Porta Pro for ages, but they were used less and less due to the constant elimination of jack connections. And wireless is a luxury that you won't want to miss at some point. I've always used the standard EarPods on the iPhone (jack and Lightning) and was satisfied. The P30i sound relatively terrible out of the box. The bass was too dominant even for me. Instead of turning down the bass, increasing the treble turned out to be the most suitable setting for me. Fortunately, a wide variety of adjustments are possible via the app. With the Space One, I had a guided tour in the app to adjust the headphones to my hearing. I didn't have that here. But this isn't a problem. For sporting activities, I've had old Sony in-ears with cables that fit perfectly in my ears, which doesn't work with all headphones. Unfortunately, this only works partially here too. The medium attachments are too loose, the large ones that I have in now fit, but over time they push out of the ears, which requires constant pressure in order not to lose them. Reminder to myself: I have to test putting the Sony attachments on the Soundcore P30i. The noise canceling is o.k. but, as so often described, not the best. The Space One, for example, can do this much better, but they are also much larger. What I recently noticed on a longer walk is that steps taken when running hard can be transferred to the headphones. It's not that pleasant, but it probably depends on the way you run. On the way back the effect was no longer disturbing, which could have been due to the slower running and the alcohol level. :DConclusion: In terms of sound, I'm very satisfied with the listeners. The running time is also convincing. So far I haven't managed to hear the battery empty before it ends up in the charging cradle. The fit in the ear is just fine for me. They're comfortable, but they're not as tight as I would like, but that's more to do with my ears. I've had problems here before and the only headphones with which I didn't have the problem were my old Sony InEars and the EarPods from Apple. It's enough for normal running (with regular pressure), but unfortunately the support isn't enough for me for sports and I'd rather choose my old combination of Sony InEars and iPod Mini. I still have to get used to the control options by tapping on the headphones, but they work anyway. Would I recommend them? Definitely! The price/performance ratio is great and the limitations are more personal. I would only generally question the hold for sporting activities. There may be something better, like my old Sony."
    },
    {
        "review": "Best"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "1) The sound of the WF-C510 is really good right out of the box without EQ. I listen to a lot of music, various genres, and everything is balanced and in control. Punchy bass, treble that's crisp and doesn't make my ears bleed, and decent mids. I definitely trust Sony when it comes to audio products.2) Fit and comfort are amazing! I can leave these in all day. No joke, after 5+ hours of use, I feel zero discomfort. Not only are these earbuds the most comfortable earbuds of all time, but getting a good in-ear seal is effortless. Just set them and forget them, zero hassle. Just to note, there's no ANC but I don't mind; I use these at home where it's already quiet. Further, there's a feature called Ambient Sound Control, kind of the opposite of ANC, that lets outside noises in (press button on left earbud). Obviously, the volume has to be pretty low for this feature to be useful.3) I rarely ever use playback controls on a pair of earbuds, as I just use my phone for that stuff. However, the physical buttons on the WF-C510 work like they should and it's always nice to have actual buttons over touch controls, mainly because there's less chance for accidental inputs, a common occurrence on my other earbuds.4) The battery life seems to jive with Sony's claims of 11 hours in the buds and another 11 in the case. I'm getting a 50% battery level after 5+ hours at a volume level above half with the \"Sony Sound Connect\" app installed on my phone. I'll admit, the 11 hour battery claim was the #1 factor for my purchase.5) The app gives you a ton of stuff to play around with. There's a bunch of EQs, 360 audio (more app downloads and payment required, I think), information regarding battery levels, and more.6) A couple of oddities: The charging case confuses me. Does the orange light turn off when charging is complete or does it stay lit? I've experienced both scenarios and both times the app said the earbuds and case were at 100%. The instruction manual was no help. Also, for some reason I had to re-pair the earbuds to my phone's Bluetooth after shutting it off, which was super annoying. I was testing to see if an older device (without the app) would pair, so to avoid interference I turned off my phone's Bluetooth. The older device paired with the earbuds just fine without the app, so it isn't required. I plan on deleting it; it's probably a major battery hog anyway.7) Minor issues aside, the Sony WF-C510 delivers premium comfort and pleasing sound. and they're super affordable. Get yours today! lolUpdate 3/25 -- Checking in to mention that the orange charging light on the case does in fact shut off to indicate that charging is complete and I'm still very happy with my purchase."
    },
    {
        "review": "I don't understand the logic behind other earbuds that claim water resistance with capacitive controls. I have tried almost a dozen across various brands and NONE of them work properly when wet. It's not that the water damages them (it doesn't), it's that the capacitive buttons can't tell a water drop from a finger, so they're constantly registering false touches, jumping tracks, changing volume. CAPACITIVE EARBUDS ARE USELESS WHEN WET. (Fine once they dry off.)This WF-C510 pair from Sony, with physical buttons instead of capacitive touch, is the FIRST SET EARBUDS I'VE FOUND THAT ACTULLY WORK IN THE SHOWER or heavy rain.If you're just going for a jog in light mist or some drops of sweat run down your ears, those others do OK, but in a heavy rain or if you want to listen in the shower, go for these. I use earbuds in the shower so I don't wake up my spouse or kids if I get up early.  I used to use a suction cup Bluetooth speaker, but that's loud and disturbs the family.Another problem with many capacitive earbuds: if you don't have any audio playing for a few minutes, they'll turn off to save battery... but because the capacitive touch requires power to work, there's often no way to turn them back on without putting them in the case and then removing them. This means if you don't have the case with you, you can't use them at all. I have actually bought extra cases just to leave in the places I often get stuck with powered-off earbuds, but what a waste of money that is!But note that where JBL, for example, sells just extra cases for their capacitive buds, I don't see that Sony does that. While you don't really need an extra case for these because you can turn them on and off via buttons, in the event you do want an extra case, be aware that your only option might be used on eBay.Also, these Sony WFD-C510 earbuds are small (compact, in the best possible way). These are some of the few earbuds I've found that I can wear under ear protection when mowing or doing other loud equipment work. Most other earbuds are bulky in comparison and ear protection pushes against them directly, really digging into the ear. These are so slim, you barely feel them even while wearing ear protection over them.On sound quality, these are great. They're not the best overall, but I think they are the best per dollar (and no earbuds are as good as real headphones if sound quality is the primary goal). They sound amazing for $50 buds.So, yes, overall, I would highly recommend them to anyone, but especially if you care about using them when very wet or under ear protection. Best I've found for those scenarios by a wide margin. To address both issues with a single pair of earbuds is incredible. One of my favorite bits of tech ever."
    },
    {
        "review": "Bought these to replace my favorite earbuds of the past few years, the KZ SKS and was a little disappointed at first. That was until I tweaked the EQ in the SONY app, which these responded wonderfully to. The bass is very lacking out of the box, but it makes for balanced tone. I like a lot of bass and bright highs, so I moved the slider to 7 on bass and adjusted the others accordingly, with no loss in sound quality. The biggest change though, was the exceptional battery life and amazing passthrough mode that I did not expect to like as much as I do. It allows conversations to be carried out while on the phone or listening to music. Even though these are roughly double the price of my $30 KZs, I would actually say they're an even better value with the features and battery life. They're also super light and relatively small, so they won't fall out of your ears. For a 6mm driver (which is supposedly shared with some of their more expensive models), these have an excellent soundstage and really can thump. The ONLY drawback (if you want to call it that), is the buttons are not capacitive, and must be physically clicked. This can be helpful in preventing accidental presses though, which is a plus. Build quality is what you'd expect from Sony. Very good. Will update if these have any drawbacks in the future."
    },
    {
        "review": "I purchased the Sony WF-C510 for around $50 or less, and considering that price point, I am generally satisfied with the product. Below is my experience after a few weeks of use.Sound:For the price, the sound profile is good and well balanced. The bass is present, though not very powerful compared to more expensive models. As a user who has owned higher-end earbuds, I do notice a lack of depth in the low frequencies, but at this price range, that is acceptable. You cannot expect premium bass performance from a budget model, and within those limits, the sound is decent.Comfort:The earbuds are small and lightweight, and after some time you barely notice you are wearing them. They are comfortable enough to use while lying in bed, and if they had touch controls, you could even listen while resting on one side without discomfort. That said, the fit is not perfect for me. They do not feel 100% secure at all times, and I occasionally need to readjust them manually. It is not frequent, but it is an area that could be improved.Buttons:For the price, I did not expect touch controls, but the physical buttons can be inconvenient at times. Pressing them often causes the earbud to move out of position, which then requires readjustment.Microphone:Based on feedback from my wife during phone calls, there was noticeable background noise on my end. I cannot provide a very detailed review of the microphone, but this was the main comment I received during the three weeks of use.Would I recommend them?It depends on the use case. I would not recommend them for intense workouts. If you are very demanding about sound quality or expect a high-end audio profile, these will likely disappoint you. However, for walking, office use, or casual listening—and if you are not very demanding about sound quality—they are a good option for the price.Note:I have used the Jabra Elite 75t for about two years (originally $199) and have been using the Sony WH-1000XM3 for four years. Obviously, this is not a fair comparison, as the difference in sound quality is significant. Still, considering the price of the WF-C510, they are worth it if you are not overly demanding about audio performance.============================Update after a few months of use:============================After a couple of months with the Sony WF-C510, my overall opinion remains mostly the same, but I can now add a few clearer conclusions.The biggest downside is definitely the microphone. Call quality has consistently been poor. On several occasions during meetings, I had to disconnect them and switch to other headphones to continue the call. Background noise isolation for the mic is weak, and for professional use, they are simply not reliable.The sound profile is still good for the price. It remains balanced and acceptable within its budget category. However, if you are used to earbuds in the $150+ range, these will likely feel underpowered and lacking depth. The difference in presence, clarity, and overall audio richness becomes more noticeable over time.Would I buy them again? Probably not. While they are not bad for the price, I personally prefer stronger audio performance and more depth, even if that means paying more.Overall, my experience is neutral — not bad, not exceptional. They deliver what you would expect from a $48 pair of earbuds, but they do not meet my personal expectations, which are influenced by years of using higher-end models."
    },
    {
        "review": "These are amazing. The sound quality is very good. They don't fall out of my ears (all other earbuds i've had, lots of them, don't stay in my ears. Even my AirPods Pro 2 fall out of my ears, but not these. Although they lack noise cancelation, they do significantly reduce environmental noise. The buds form a tight seal, and turning off the Ambient Noise feature makes them effectively noise canceling. BUT, the most impressive feature is the water resistance.I, ummmmmmm, left them in my pants pocket, forgot they were there, and put the pants in the washing machine. Yeah, very much bad not good. The were in there for about 15 minutes total, and 5 minutes into the Wash cycle. So, I remembered that I'd left them in my pants pocket, and that they were in the wash. I fished them out (they'd fallen out of my pants pocket and were on the bottom of the tub, under all the T-shirts, socks, other pants .  .  .  ), and set them on my desk to dry. That night I put them in a UV dryer and cycled it several times. The next morning I tested them AND THEY WORKED. Holy Moly Batman! These are tough buds."
    },
    {
        "review": "I purchased these a little under a year ago, and up until about a month ago, I absolutely loved them. Honestly, I would have easily given these 4-5 stars if it weren’t for the charging case issue.Unfortunately, the case has taken a pretty sharp nosedive lately. I now have to charge it almost daily just to use one earbud at a time... which kind of defeats the whole “wireless earbuds” concept.For the price, I expected a bit more longevity. I usually buy cheaper earbuds because I have a talent for leaving them in pockets or sacrificing a single earbud to the outdoors forever, but I decided to splurge for better sound and call quality - and to be fair, these definitely delivered on that front.I also have smaller ears, and these have been some of the most comfortable earbuds I’ve used. They’re lightweight, not made of super hard plastic, and don’t feel enormous or awkward after wearing them for a while.So overall: excellent sound, calls, and comfort... but the charging case aging this badly in under a year is really disappointing."
    },
    {
        "review": "The most important feature for me is fit; I wear earbuds in the gym, and can't have them falling out! These earbuds feel secure in my ears, deliver excellent sound quality, have all the features I want, and none I don't. Pairing was easy too. I'm very happy with this purchase!"
    },
    {
        "review": "The box was ripped open right where the earbuds were placed in the package. I don’t know if it was someone being curious, or just a shipping machine rip. Either way, I took the earbuds out of package, charged them fully and put them on for a trail.I would put a “mind blown” emoji here. The sound right out of the box was good. Then I downloaded the “Sound Connect” app and I was lost for a good hour and a half, just having fun and listening to music.I was able to quickly use “short cut” way to find what kind of sound I like, as the app just walked me through. I now know, ( by this apps definition) that I like clear bass. There is an equalizer where anyone whom has the knowledge and the skill to set their music to sound just like they want to hear. I have an eclectic range of music. Billy Joel ( older version cd) that didn’t give me the strong clear bass I like, yet it was quite balanced. I listened to Tchaikovsky‘s Swan Lake and that just made me smile. Enter P!nk with “Truth About Love” cd and I could hear parts of the music that I have not heard before. Amazing!! And to end the night with a bang  I switched to “Disturbed” Down with the Sickness and I was banging. The sound never got distorted. Everything was clear and I actually had to turn the sound down, as I like it loud!! Mind you, I am not a audiophile, but I do love music, so take my opinion as you choose.The buttons on the buds are easy to use. I used with Alexa, phone call, next song, previous song, and it was seamlessly easy. Functionality is A+.Instructions have a QR Code to take you to a users manual. The buds come with a one year warranty. Just a quick scan tells me I need to keep the box it came in and I am sure there is more that is needed for the warranty. Just read to be safe. The package comes with different size buds inside the package.Now, I can not say enough about how comfortable these buds feel. I have purchased sooooooo many types of earbuds and have NEVER felt buds this comfortable. Every pair of buds, I will not name them, but seriously a LOT, has been uncomfortable. These are just so comfortable and even though I have no noise cancellation, they are sealed so well in my ear that it doesn’t feel like I am missing anything.Long story short, get these!!!!! Sony has always come through and these are no different. Be well."
    },
    {
        "review": "I have the Sony WH-1000XM4 (with a headband) and the Xiaomi Redmi Buds 3 Lite, so these will be my point of comparison. I have read opinions that these Sonys do not have a very high volume but for me the volume is enjoyable. The bass sounds very good for the price, nothing to ask for from the low frequencies of the 1000XM4 (which to be fair, the bass of these headphones, the 1000XM4, does not stand out much even with the equalizer). The audio in general is cleaner and of better quality compared to the Xiaomi ones. The material does feel cheap, both in the headphones and the case but nothing to worry about, they are cheap but the construction is good and comfortable. They feel very light and do not bother you at all after hours. The battery of the headphones is very good, it takes a long time for it to drop below 100% (unless you activate the ambient mode) and although they do not have noise cancellation, I personally feel that in this type of headphones it is not really necessary because the rubber closes the ear very well. The case on the other hand... doesn't last long. In 2-3 days it had already dropped to 50%, but I will continue checking (I clarify that I use them for a long time a day, when I don't use them much the battery in the case does not drop as quickly). If you see them for $800 MXN or less, they seem like a good option to me, especially because of how light and comfortable they are and the audio quality in general. If you see them as more expensive, I would look for other options.Finally, two pieces of advice:1. Download their app, 'Sound Connect' (from Sony) and use the equalizer. The audio that comes from the factory does feel very flat (maybe that's why they complain about the volume), so using the equalizer substantially improves the sound quality. If you don't want to rack your brains too much, just open the app, register the headphones and in the equalizer section choose the 'Excited' profile (or some other profile that has at least +7 Clear Bass).2. I've read on some forums that some people had a problem with the case: if you let the battery in the case reach 0%, it won't charge again (the headphones do, but the case doesn't). In the same app you can monitor the battery of the headphones and the case. Check it from time to time and try to ensure that the battery in the case does not drop below 20-30%. I haven't had any problems and it doesn't happen to everyone, but it's better to prevent anything. Update: after two months of use, the case has completely discharged more than once and it continues to work, don't be scared about that issue, but yes, its battery lasts three to four days, sometimes a little less."
    },
    {
        "review": "I thought I needed noise-cancelling earbuds for the gym, but these do the trick at a fraction of the price. Since they fit snugly with the silicone tips (and I have small ear canals), they block out a lot of noise passively. Noise-cancelling probably wouldn't do much better, because it's not optimized for voices and other higher frequencies. If you want earbuds and don't want to pay $300, then these are a great value. Note that I bought them on sale for $68; the current price of $100 is pushing it.The sound is clear and balanced, no excessive treble or bass. No connectivity issues using a Samsung phone and MacBook. Battery life shouldn't be an issue for most people, they last forever.If you haven't used this type of earbud before, just note that when you first put them in, they might not seal well, but just give the silicone a minute to warm up and it will sit better (provided you have the correct tip size installed)."
