        "review": "Great for my son to help with his schooling, the lights are amazing- great quality too"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "I am a user, and fond lover, of the G502 and G600. When I saw V3 Pro and the feature set, I really wanted to take a dive into the Razer ecosystem and give it a whirl. This one mouse had the features and potential to replace both the G600 and G502 on my desk.While using and trying the mouse, I can say that it does replace both and works as expected. The form may cause other pause, but it comes down to preference and usability. The G502 is a great mouse but was causing wrist issues despite the perfect for. The Naga V3 Pro is slightly smaller than the V2, but it has given me the form of the G600 which tends to be usable longer for me, with the features of the G600 and G502."
    },
    {
        "review": "I was really, really contemplating rating this less because of Razer Synapse 4.  I couldn't get button remapping to work in any game at the start, it would only work after I closed Synapse entirely.  Profile switching also didn't work.  The software took so much finagling and trial and error manually deleting Synapse and re-installing more than once--I even tried deleting Synapse data online from my account via the Privacy Center to no avail.  Midway through, I almost just resorted to keeping things on onboard memory and manually switching between games.  It was brutal.Thankfully profile switching started working and button remaps followed suit.  I don't know how I got it to work, but it works!  It handles so well that the pain setting up the software can be forgiven.  This is everything I wanted out of my old Logitech G600s, of which I've had several and daily drove for the better part of a decade.  I'd always try but ultimately return other MMO mice because I'd always miss ring-finger button.  I know I'm not the only one that felt this!It seems like Razer heard our cries and implemented it on this new Naga V3 Pro, even adding the two additional buttons by the left-click button like on the V2 Hyperspeed.  My only gripes are that the side buttons aren't as pronounced and contoured as the G600, and the left- and right-mouse clicks are just obnoxiously loud.  Travel distance and weight per click as well I'd say aren't great either--maybe better for older MMOs where each click needed to be more deliberate, but a lighter switch would be better for modern MMOs.  I guess I'll get used to them.Despite all of that, the fact that in addition to the extra buttons this is wireless AND I can swap plates for other non-MMO games?  I can learn to live with this.Logitech really missed out."
    },
    {
        "review": "I’ve been really impressed with the Razer Naga V3 Pro. My favorite feature is the removable side panels—it’s great being able to switch the button layout depending on what I’m doing. The mouse feels very solid and well-built, and the weight is just right for me. It feels comfortable and controlled without being too light.My only issue has been the 2.4 GHz wireless connection. I haven’t been able to get it to run as smoothly as I’d like, so I normally toggle it over to Bluetooth, which has worked much better for me. Other than that, it’s a solid mouse and I really enjoy using it. Definitely one of my favorite mice I’ve owned."
    },
    {
        "review": "Works great with 2, 6, or 12 side buttons. Razer Synapse customizes it with up to twice as many functions. Button on ring finger rest acts as trigger for second set on all Razer devices. Replaced my V2 with it."
    },
    {
        "review": "Synapse 4 currently does not support Razer Naga V3 Pro for MAC. This is ridiculous, and should be specified for buyers."
    },
    {
        "review": "The buttons on the 12 button MMO panel wobble and creak. They are not sturdy. The buttons on the bottom right of the panel are impossible to use without fat fingering an extra key.I like the scroll wheel and the third click. The scroll wheel is three extra buttons on its own.  The third click should probably be used infrequently as it is an unnatural hand gesture.I’m coming from an old razer naga x with keys that don’t jiggle, more solid construction, all for a lower price. Doesn’t seem like there’s an equivalent to it these days. I wish they would make another dedicated 12 button and stop giving the mmo players this junk three panel nonsense."
    },
    {
        "review": "Top notch. Does about everything correct. Great feel, light, responsive, easy to customize. Lots  of options."
    },
    {
        "review": "Side buttons are too close to reasonable expect to select the correct one every time. Only one side panel is RGB. Mouse is too bulbous for smaller hands. Packaging is nonsense.The HyperShift ring finger button is the only nice thing about the mouse. Or you'd think so.... Actually using it caused serious hand cramps during gaming. Retuned after 6 hours of use- back to the Basilisk"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "Surprisingly reliable and long lasting for a razer product.No issues experienced with connectivity, and bluetooth mode does its job although its not a very good one, think of it as a power saving mode.I like that I can change out the batteries as well so theres the security of it not becoming e waste if it's built in battery dies.And overall its very comfortable apart from the there not being any support for your thumb to rest on under the area with the numbers, some MMO mice have that support rest area but not this one, not a deal breaker though, great mouse and my favorite so far"
    },
    {
        "review": "After my Logitech G604 at work decided to start having switch troubles, it was time to look for another mouse. I was hoping to get another Logitech G604 or similar since I utilize the side buttons for work and for gaming. Unfortunately, at least as of the last time I searched, Logitech discontinued the G604 and didn't plan on making another similar. I'm glad I started looking at Razer mice as this one caught my eye and I was intrigued.I've had this mouse now for 9 months and it was the best decision I've made in any of my PC purchases, minus the PC of course. The mouse feels good in your hand and is very light compared to the G604. The feet slide extremely easy on mouse pads for flawless movement. The buttons are very responsive and I enjoy the extra 6 that I get compared to the 604; more commands/shortcuts at a thumb press. Some have had the button paint start to wear off but I have not, at least yet, so I'm not sure if they just have super acidic hand sweat or eating Cheetos while gaming. Anywho, the paint has not worn off in any way and I can still read all the numbers on the side buttons. As far as connectivity, I don't 'hardcore' game so I probably won't ever use the HyperSpeed setting on it but the bluetooth works wonderfully and is very quick to connect on start up. The mouse also has a timeout function which you can adjust via the software. The software I don't hear much good about but I haven't had trouble with it I guess. It allows me to program the buttons as needed and that's about all I use it for since this is the only Razer product I have.I'd recommend this mouse to anyone who doesn't like searching for keys on their keyboard for functions. It's not only a great mouse for gaming but for work when you need button combos that are a pain to do on the keyboard. I liked it so much that I ended up buying another one for home and replaced my G604 at home a few months later."
    },
    {
        "review": "TERRIBLE !The Synapse software doesnt work, profiles do not load when running associated applications.Contacted Razer support. They told me uninstall Synapse3 then run their DriverRemovalTool that support linked me and then run the Synapse4 installer. Uninstalled the Synapse3 software, ran the DriverRemovalTool, rebooted, ran the Synapse4 installer and it tells me... Synapse3 is INSTALLED and that it needs removed to instll Synpase4 !! Did that, Synapse4 installed.. profiles still do not load. SO THAT DOESNT WORK !Support then told to me install then run their RazerLogCollector tool, installed it and ran it, \"failed to upload, please check your internet connection\". SO THAT DOESNT WORK !Support then told me to download the FirmwareUpdateTool, the FirmwareUpdateTool, requires the mouse be put into \"Bluetooth\" mode, mouse fails to pair with Bluetooth ! Mouse only works when the switch on mouse is on \"Wireless\" Mode, so cannot update the firmware if their is an update. SO THAT DOESNT WORK !Support then told me to login to their SynapseTestAccount through the Synapse software and gave me a username password to use and they could see whats going on, opened Synapse and clicked login,.. no user/password window comes up ! SO THAT DOESNT WORK !NOTHING \"RAZER\" WORKS, THIS IS ABSOLUTE GARBAGE AND SO IS THEIR SUPPORT !AND ON TOP OF ALL THAT NOT WORKING.. THERE ARE MORE THAN A DOZEN RAZER PROCESSES RUNNING AT ALL TIME< AND THEY ARE EATING UP 1.6GB OF MEMORY AND EVERY PROCESS MUST STAY RUNNING !NEVER AGAIN RAZER AND THEY CHARGE THE PRICES THEY DO !"
    },
    {
        "review": "have to admit admit that while i dont love the razer brand, this mouse is without a doubt the best - as in the most capable/powerful mouse - on the market. scroll wheel side buttons? yes, they work perfectly and are clicky in a good way, not in a bad way. all the buttons are tactile and responsive. i have average size man hands and the mouse is a lot more comfortable for long sessions than other mice ive used, so i'd say the size/comfort/ergonomics all just kind of work if you have medium and small hands. the synapse software is easier and better than the logitech g hub software. it is definitely more powerful, in terms of just what you can actually do with the mouse and all of the buttons. you can have a crazy amount of macros and software launchers and all kinds of stuff. if you go really overboard and use 3rd party software to add another layer of commands and controls you basically have infinite controls/macros. the only flaw i think ive really noticed is that the design of the buttons near the thumb naturally sits - this only happens while gaming - sometimes your thumb may accidently press one of those side thumb g buttons so you need to take that into account or train yourself out of doing that."
    },
    {
        "review": "DOES NOT LIGHT UP.... as all the other Razer Nagas do. I have always owned a Razer Naga but they are hard to find. The reason I buy Razers are for the lights 1st over everything. Other than what I am extrememly disappointed in, the mouse feels nice. Has bluetooth or USB connections. As soon as connect it works. Buttons are smooth using. It is Batteried, which if you get Snyapse program it tells you the charge. Appearance other than no lights is nice/slick... The description doesn't say anything about non lighting that I seen. Razer failed on this brand mouse. Yes I would buy another one if needed as it does again, feel real smooth. Sensitivity can raise very high, higher than any other mouse ive had. (400-6400)"
    },
    {
        "review": "I used to despise Razer, but nowadays, the MMO mouse from Razer is pretty decent hardware. Their biggest problem has always been their software, however, and I must admit their support for software integrations is lacking. There's no OBS integration and no Discord integration. I'm getting by without setting up macros for hotkey combos by simply using my Logitech keyboard's macro keys for the integrations I want, and the only reason I didn't get another Logitech mouse was Logitech didn't make a new mouse model and discontinued their old, wired G600 model, making it cost too much to replace. I went with the Razer Naga V2 because it was somewhat cheap by comparison and wireless and I felt like giving other brands a try. I ended up really loving the build quality of the Naga V2, but Razer's software was still pretty terrible.The hardware design is pretty good, but there are some ergonomics issues. The top left mouse click buttons (not left click) are hard to find by feel and also a little difficult to reach. There's no palm rest for a big hand to rest on. I think this mouse could benefit greatly from a 3d printable palm extension, but that would increase its weight, and the mouse is very lightweight. I ended up using a wrist rest that helped a little with mouse fatigue.The battery is somewhat easy to swap out, and you don't have to swap often. It uses a single AA battery, and I opted to use a rechargeable lithium battery in it that adds some extra capacity.The LEDs on the buttons are very dim by comparison to my other MMO mice, and they don't have the same flair I get from some other brands. The colors are customizable, but there's no wave effect on the mouse, which is weird because Razer's keyboards have the wave effect.DPI is easy to change on-the-fly. I see no noticeable lag from being wireless. I also don't see any connectivity issues as long as I keep the mouse within 8 feet of its dongle."
    },
    {
        "review": "Let me start by saying that this mouse is not a bad mouse in general, but if you are a gamer and you are here because you are looking for a replacement for your Logitech G600, you will probably not love this mouse.I have been a G600 user for many years, and my last one recently started having some issues that made finding a replacement necessary. The V2 is *slightly* smaller overall than the G600, so it may not feel as good in your hand. It does have a bit of a lip on the right side where your ring finger rests, similar to the G600's shift button, so it seems like Razer could have implemented this feature, but did not.The scroll wheel on top seems sluggish to me, in that it takes quite a few scrolls to move down a page, and I have not found any setting in the Razer Synapse app to change this. There is a button underneath the scroll wheel that \"unlocks\" the wheel and allows the wheel to spin freely instead of \"clicking,\" however that is all this button does, it is not a functional button like the G7 and G8 buttons on the G600.The V2 has two buttons in the top left corner of the left mouse button. By default these buttons are for turning the DPI up or down \"on the fly,\" a feature which I have found to be entirely useless. I have never met anyone who adjusted their DPI once they found one they liked, much less needed to constantly change it. Even if you did need or want to do this, the buttons are pretty far away and not very easy to hit. You can re-map these buttons in the Synapse app, but again I have found they are too hard to reach for them to be useful.By far the worst feature is the 12 side buttons. It has the same number of side buttons as the G600, however they are considerably smaller than the buttons on the G600. The buttons on the V2 are also not as beveled as the ones on the G600, are placed closer together, and do not have the home row \"nubs\" on the middle buttons which help you know where your thumb is. Additionally, where the G600 buttons are angled towards each other, with row 1 and 2 angled down towards each other, and rows 3 and 4 angled towards each other, the angling on the V2 side buttons is kind of all over the place, making it more difficult to move seamlessly from button to button.As far as connectivity goes, I had no problem using the dongle to connect using the 2.4ghz option, and so far the mouse seems to track well. You will need to download the Razer Synapse app to customize keys and DPI settings. It does not seem to allow you to customize the polling rate, other than to choose from 125, 500 or 1000.All things totaled, this mouse does not stack up for me and I will probably be returning it. I find that not having the G7 and G8 buttons on top, and all of the shortcomings with the side buttons make this very difficult to use while gaming. I find myself having to pay far too much attention to what my thumb is doing and trying to find the right buttons.TL;DR - If you are a gamer, especially one who is accustomed to the G600, this mouse will disappoint you."
    },
    {
        "review": "This mouse has been great, the first couple days i had an issue with it lagging or stuttering, but its software was updated and it has been great since, ive been using it for a while and had zero issues. Multiple DPI settings and being able to click a button on my mouse and change dpi if needed based on my settings is great too."
    },
    {
        "review": "It was definitely an excellent purchase. I'm not a gamer, I'm a programmer. I bought it mainly because of the number of buttons it has since I require shortcuts and macros to speed up writing code and other things. Programming is practically copy and paste haha. I have customized buttons for Ctrl + C, Ctrl + V, Enter, F5, forward, backward, volume up and down, etc. The horizontal scroll also helps me when I have to navigate through long files or Excel sheets; Likewise, free vertical scrolling is a big help when I have files that are thousands of lines of code or long web pages. It has helped me a lot to not have to make combinations with the keyboard since I simply press a button with my thumb. It took me a bit at first to learn the location of the buttons, but as muscle memory develops my productivity and speed have increased. I still have two jobs so I work with two different PCs and with the switch located on the bottom it makes it easy to switch between computers in a matter of seconds; I use one device with Bluetooth and the other with the 2.4 GHz dongle. Of course, it is limited to only two teams. I would have liked it to support one more device. I had previously purchased the Logitech mx master 3s, but it was a very large and heavy mouse for my small hand so it was uncomfortable for me to work with. Then I acquired the Logitech M720 Triathlon, this was much more comfortable for my hand, but over time I felt that it required more buttons and I decided to buy this Razer. This one is also a little big and heavy, but not as much as the mx master 3s. At the moment, in my opinion, I consider this Razer much better than the previous two from Logitech. The Razer Synapse software offers a world of configurations that are mainly used for games, but I have known how to take advantage of it for productivity. For example, you can create different profiles to save button settings. This is very convenient if you have one configuration for one game and a different configuration for another game and not having to configure the buttons every time you change games. Settings are saved on the mouse, not on the computer like other brands, so there's no need to reconfigure the mouse when you connect it to a new computer. So far only one device has not detected the saved settings, but that is the only case. Regarding the battery, I thought if it would only last a couple of days because I use it for the entire day, but I have had it for a month and it is only at 60%. The battery it brought is an Energizer alkaline, that also helps with long life so I will continue buying alkaline batteries from the same brand or Duracell, but it doesn't hurt to buy rechargeable batteries to help the environment hehe. That has been my experience during the first month. I will continue to update my review as I continue to use it."
    },
    {
        "review": "Work properly"
    },
    {
        "review": "In games, when using abilities while moving, you can not only copy and paste, but also customize the buttons on the left side, and by registering shortcut keys, you can operate without looking at the keyboard. What's especially nice is that you can separate templates on a dedicated customization screen. The good thing is that you don't have to customize the buttons for each task and game."
    },
    {
        "review": "Quick delivery. Only problem i could see was damave to the packaging. Great deal"
    },
    {
        "review": "Many times cannot connect to the Razer software for the 9 programmable buttons to work. Dunno why."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "now for my background, i play r6 and csgo, and occasionally roblox shooters. i went from originally a 60g razer orochi v2 using a fingertip grip, and i went to this mouse with a traditional claw. i’ve had this mouse for a few months and i can say that this is a very good and universal shape for all sorts of grips. i have a medium size hand and i can comfortably fingertip and claw this mouse.the coating is lovely and the best i’ve personally felt on a mouse, and i tried the grips but they eventually wear out over time so i don’t recommend using those (the actual mouse texture is better than the grips anyway). now i know this sounds a little ridiculous but moving from a 60g small mouse not even including the batteries, i played a LOT better with this bigger one after adjusting and i’m honestly happy with my purchase (even though it cost quite a bit). it feels light in the hand for the size and the weight is flawlessly distributed. the skates are solid for being stock but you can always upgrade, the scroll wheel is pretty normal and has distinctive bumps per scroll but personally i like more smoother ones (i am probably just being nitpicky). the main clicks are lovely to hear and the little dongle included doesn’t take up too much space, i’d say only the actual cable itself does but this isn’t actually an issue. the dongle can also support up to 8k hz which isn’t necessary but it’s a “nice to have” feature and i use 2k hz myself to save battery life;speaking of battery life it’s pretty good and i suggest not using 8k since it drains it pretty fast but it lasts up to a week or two for me with regular use not charging (just remember to plug it in after use) the side buttons are a DEFINITE improvement from my old mouse and that was a perfect feature on the mouse. now, razer’s synapse software can be a little finicky sometimes but i suggest just configuring your settings and uninstalling it since it can take up some of your ram and overall performance.in conclusion, this is a really good option for gaming mice and it can fit a LOT of playstyles. this has all of the latest mouse tech (might be a little outdated for me to be saying that in the big 25 now) and each part of the mouse is very pleasant to use. i suggest this mouse if you have the money (it is pretty ehh value for money but as long as you have the funds it’s a s tier mouse)"
    },
    {
        "review": "(Review from my 15 year old son)Honestly this mouse felt SO much lighter than I thought it would. I just upgraded from the Glorious Model O- Wireless Gaming Mouse, and let me say... the upgrade was WORTH it for sure. I pretty much only play Fortnite on Keyboard and Mouse, and I was having issues with my side buttons on my old mouse. I decided it was time to upgrade since I can't have those issues happening mid-game. The Razer Viper V3 Pro, is definitely a solid upgrade. The side buttons are so much more solid than the side buttons on my old mouse. Not to mention, the response time of the clicks was faster, and the accuracy of my aim with the better sensor was so much better. The scroll wheel is also very responsive and fast which is important for Fortnite. (I was able to easily quad edit in Fortnite when using this new mouse)The wireless connectivity isn't even noticeable at all. The response time is insane for a wireless mouse, and it surprised me a lot. I don't have a lot to say about the battery life yet, as this is my first day using it. I will say though, I charged the mouse to 68%, used it for an hour in Fortnite, and the battery was only 67% afterwards.Here are the few cons I can think of:When switching from whatever mouse you currently use, it will take a few hours or even a day to adjust to the lightness of the mouse. (mainly if you played on heavier mice before) It's not anything with the mouse itself, but just a matter of adjusting to the feel of the new mouse.The mouse comes with some grip tape you can put on the mouse, which honestly looks really cool in my opinion, and it really helps with making sure your aim is as good as it can be, since your hand won't slip off the mouse. (See image) (Note: mouse is charging)Razer also has a good software which allows you to adjust the mouse to match the feel of your old one which is really helpful to make sure you don't have to COMPLETELY start over with learning a sensitivity.Overall a really good mouse, no complaints to be honest."
    },
    {
        "review": "The Razer Viper V3 has been an excellent mouse for both gaming and everyday use. It feels very lightweight and comfortable in hand, making long sessions easy with no fatigue. The shape is simple but very ergonomic, and it works well for both claw and fingertip grip styles.Performance wise, the sensor is extremely accurate and responsive. Tracking is smooth, clicks feel crisp, and there is no noticeable delay. The wireless connection is stable and reliable, with no lag or dropouts. Battery life has also been great, lasting several days of heavy use without needing frequent charging.Build quality feels solid, and the mouse buttons and scroll wheel all feel well made. Overall, the Viper V3 delivers excellent performance, comfort, and reliability. It is a great choice for anyone looking for a high quality lightweight gaming mouse."
    },
    {
        "review": "I had a Logitech superlight and moved to the Viper v3 pro. It was the best decision that I have ever made. The mouse sensor in my opinion is more accurate the Logitech mice. The feel and texture and material of the mouse feels very nice especially the white coating option is phenomenal and has really good grip for a very low amount of weight gain. The size definitely favors mid-big hands and excels in the Claw grip. It isn't the lightest mouse on the market but it still in my opinion is more than light enough for most. The scroll wheel actually feels quite nice and i prefer it over the Logitech one. Though this mouse supports 8k polling I rarely find myself using it as it drains battery too fast, I would just use 4000/2000 if you want a high enough speed with a very long battery life. If you are looking for a high quality claw grip mouse then this is the mouse for you, if you don't have a claw grip I would recommend looking at other mice that might be for you."
    },
    {
        "review": "As someone who loves the Superlight and has been jamming out with version 2 of it along with a Logitech Pro X TKL paired with it, it's been nice to swap back to my Black Widow V4 along with the Viper Pro 3.Right out of the box, and I am someone with medium to large hands, this mouse is just a pure delight to hold; it's not often I can say that my hands feel like they disappear when using a peripheral, but this mouse and the Superlight from Logi are the two mice that achieve that for me. Razer knew what they were doing here and it shows in the product; this will be my go-to mouse and I will alternate my Razer setup back to the Logitech set up as the mood strikes me to change things up week by week.When it comes time for the Wooting 80HE to arrive, I will most likely be pairing the Viper Pro 3 with that board due to just how consistently good it feels; I am 3 hours deep into a play session and the battery is down to 98% and I'm using the 4K polling rate; not going to bother with 8K just yet.It's hard to put into words here just how good the slight ergo shape of this mouse feels; I really have tried a number of different mice and this could be \"the one\"; I don't say this in a hyperbolic fashion either. It's rare that you use a gaming device where you just forget what you're using because it seamlessly blends into the play session.For anyone looking to try this out, the asking price is steep. For a low frills mouse in the aesthetic department it really isn't going to be winning wards for it's design change to be not as sharp as it's predecessor. I for one really dig the smoother design and the light hump towards the back of the mouse, the finger ridges, or whatever you want to call them, on the L + R mouse clicks, all come together to suit whatever combination of grip you want to employ here. I am using a light palm grip and find that going claw and then relaxing my fingers feel natural.The side buttons deserve a special award here; they are separated ever so slightly and their clicks are immediate / responsive. Truly the only gripe I have with my Superlight are the side buttons; Logitech really needs to take the oatmeal mush out of those and it will be a near perfect mouse for me.Really curious to see what kind of competition this will spur with other manufacturers; that said, and for now, this is the best of the best for something that is here, on Amazon, that you can order and have by tomorrow while it's in stock. To whoever nabs one, enjoy; curious to see other reviews pop up on here."
    },
    {
        "review": "TLDR:Good mouse, great quality for price, beautiful designs, 10/10The Razer Viper V3 Pro is probably the best gaming mouse hands down. It’s ultra lightweight, feels amazing to move around, and extremely responsive.The clicks are a little on the louder side, the material is smooth and not particularly grippy, so if you prefer grippier mice, there are those grip stickers that you can put on.I have an average adult male sized hand. In terms of what type of grip you use (claw, palm, etc..) this mouse is definitely a claw or fingertip type of mouse. Palm grip doesn’t feel amazing on this mouse since it’s a little small in my opinion. There’s also no “ergonomic” shape like the razer deathadder, so if you’re a claw or fingertip type grip person, this is probably the best mouse on the market you can get. (Please do check measurements to compare with your own hand size)I got the faker edition due to his iconic style, and it’s a beautiful red with a stunning demon art design.Overall it’s a high end mouse with high end performance, the mouse is definitely equal value to the price.For league players out there: I ranked up from Platinum to Emerald after I got this mouse (not saying it does wonders lol)"
    },
    {
        "review": "I have had this mouse for about a year now and use it as my daily driver, and I love it.It is really light and glides across the mouse pad. The mouse wheel is not noisy at all and neither is the clicking of the mouse buttons imo. The battery life is good lasting more than a few all day play sessions, and a couple of weeks of light use.It comes with some little adhesive pads that you can place on the mouse which I would recommend using as the mouse is very smooth.My complaints are that the mouse gets clammy feeling after a while. Not a huge deal, but I have thought of buying another adhesive pad to go over the emblem because it gets to feel gross. I do clean it, but this is the only mouse I have ever had this issue with. Also the dongle is kind of a pain if you have OCD, it is hard to get it to lay flat down.Overall I love the mouse and think it is worth the money. I would just recommend buying an adhesive patch (I'm not sure what they are really called) to go over the razer emblem as that part can start to feel gross."
    },
    {
        "review": "So far so good. I was impressed on how light this mouse felt when I used it for the first time. You can definitely feel the extra accuracy when you aim, by that I mean every small movement you make on your mousepad translates exactly as you would expect on the screen. My router and modem are right under my desk and I don't get any interference.  I don't open the program that comes with it (synapse) because I used a wired headset with it (razer blackshark v2 X) and I don't want synapse messing the audio settings of my headset since the default option is pretty good. The battery works for a long time; I used it for like 3 consecutive days and when I open synapse to check battery is about 70% (I use 2k polling rate). Clicking sounds satisfying as you would expect from an expensive mouse. Your forearm gets considerably less tired. The side buttons and click buttons have not collected any dust or grime from my hands, unlike my old mouse did."
    },
    {
        "review": "Very nice"
    },
    {
        "review": "Original product! New! Product arrived more than 15 days ahead of schedule!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Light and feel nice"
    },
    {
        "review": "Extremely light and with a long-lasting battery, very good mouse… super recommended"
    },
    {
        "review": "Great mouse but higher polling rates affect fps depending on your pc"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "The Anker USB-C Hub, 7-in-1 Multi-Port USB Adapter has been an excellent addition to my setup. It instantly expanded the connectivity of my laptop and has worked flawlessly from day one. Everything was truly plug-and-play with no complicated setup required.The variety of ports is exactly what I needed. Whether I’m connecting USB accessories, transferring files from an SD card, using HDMI for an external display, or charging through the USB-C pass-through port, the hub handles everything smoothly and efficiently.The build quality is outstanding, which is what I’ve come to expect from Anker. It feels solid, the cable is durable, and the compact design makes it easy to carry in my laptop bag without adding extra bulk.Performance has been consistently reliable with fast data transfers, stable connections, and no overheating, even during extended use. If you’re looking for a high-quality USB-C hub that combines functionality, portability, and dependable performance, this 7-in-1 adapter is an excellent investment. I highly recommend it."
    },
    {
        "review": "I’ve been really happy with this hub. It has a nice balance of ports without going overboard. Some hubs don’t have enough connections, while others are packed with ports I’ll never use. This one hits a sweet spot for everyday use.Everything has worked reliably so far. Connections have been stable, devices are recognized quickly, and it has become a permanent part of my desk setup.The build quality also feels solid. The housing has a nice premium feel, and it doesn’t feel flimsy despite being compact. It’s small enough to travel with if needed but works equally well as a desktop hub.One thing to keep in mind is power. Make sure your laptop charger has enough wattage to power both your laptop and any connected accessories. That’s not a fault of this hub, just something people should be aware of when setting up a dock or multi-port adapter.ProsGreat selection of ports without being excessiveReliable performanceCompact but well builtPremium-feeling materialsWorks well for both travel and desktop setupsConsRequires an adequately powered USB-C charger for the best experiencePort selection may not fit every specialized setupFinal ThoughtsThis has been a great USB-C hub. It offers the right mix of ports, performs reliably, and feels well made. As long as you pair it with a charger that can provide enough power for your setup, it’s an easy recommendation.Who It’s ForPerfect for MacBook and laptop users who want a dependable USB-C hub with a practical selection of ports without carrying around a much larger docking station."
