[
    {
        "review": "I have a  LITE-ON USB 2.0 External CD/DVD Writer Model eUAU108, manufactured in 2013, with an attached cable that is slightly less than 4 inches long.  This model was an improvement over prior models, in that it requires only a single USB 2.0 connection, whereas previous models employed a Y-cable to enable connecting to 2 USB ports simultaneously for additional power.  I have always felt that this drive was marginally capable of running on the 500 mA specification for USB 2.0 ports.  I think this is the main reason for the ultra short cable - to minimize the voltage drop.The short cable worked OK when I was using the drive with a laptop, but attempting to use it with a computer attached to the back of my flat screen monitor is a major problem.  The USB ports are about a foot above the desktop, making it necessary to support the drive in some manner.  This has always been problematic.  I tried using the only USB extension cable that I had, a cheap dollar store cable, and it did not work at all.My next thought was that I should purchase a higher quality, and obviously more expensive, USB cable, but I still had reservations.  Would a longer cable, even of a higher quality, work?  Why did LITE-ON force a short cable onto the consumer?  They could have provided a USB port instead of an attached cable.  I then thought of using a powered hub, which could solve the voltage/power issue, but I still wondered if there might be other signal quality issues that a longer cable might cause.  I believe I was starting to overthink the matter at this point!I started looking at the market of powered hubs and discovered that there was quite a variety, from no-name cheapies to surprisingly expensive higher end units. It was easy to eliminate the no-name cheapies because there were brand names I was familiar with that were very affordable. Then I discovered that some of the hubs were rated at 10 Gbps!  Gotta have that feature, right?  Even though I don't have a single device faster than 5 Gbps, I justified this feature on the basis of future-proofing.  I figured it would only be a matter of time before I had devices of that speed.  Later on I discovered that all the USB ports on the computer attached to the back of my monitor are 10 Gbps capable – five are USB 3.2 Gen2 (10 Gbps) and one is USB4 (40 Gbps!) - additional justification for going with a 10 Gbps hub.As I looked at the 10 Gbps offerings I quickly discovered that many, if not most of them, were from RSHTECH/Rosonway.  They have quite a variety of well designed, useful devices.  So many options that I found it helpful to generate a table of features to more easily compare them.  I finally decided to go with the RSH-H7C4 for the following reasons:• 10 Gbps on every port• both USB-A and USB-C ports• push button switch (vs. rocker type), with an indicator light, for each port• all ports support USB Battery Charging 1.2 protocol (BC1.2), delivering a maximum of 1.5A at 5V (7.5W) per port [standard USB ports limit current to 0.5A (USB 2.0) or 0.9A (USB 3.2)]• 36W (12V/3A) DC power adapter• detachable 3.3 foot USB-C host cable (when I need to connect to a USB-A host I use a cheap adapter I purchased at a dollar store; some of the Rosonway units include a dual-headed cable with both A and C plugs!)• two year warrantyIt's a beautifully designed unit with a solid, weighty feel to it.  Rounded edges and a thin accent band of non-anodized aluminum around each end give this hub a modern, classy appearance.  With the case being narrow and slightly rounded on the bottom, one might have concern about its stability against the pushing and pulling of the connected USB cables.  While it might technically be more stable with a completely flat bottom, it is surprisingly stable as is due to its weight and mass. Not at all like some of the lighter weight plastic cased devices that I have.  It would take a deliberate effort to tip it over; I don't think that will be an issue.  I actually do tip it over to accommodate one of my situations: the cable on my DVD drive is so short that the drive cannot lay flat when the cable is inserted into the port on the hub from above.  I have to lay the hub on its side (see video).The included USB cable is heavy gauge, very thick. With my limited experience in the world of USB cables I have made two observations: 1)there is a tremendous range of cost, quality, and capabilities among the many cables on the market; 2)an extra USB cable frequently comes in handy.  It would make sense to me that RSHTECH would provide a suitably high-grade cable with their hub in order to support the 10 Gbps data rate and the BC1.2 current draw capability. The fact that the USB cable is detachable is a bonus!  It can be used elsewhere.  I could even change it into an extension cable by the use of a gender-changing adapter.The indicator lights are a beautiful blue, quite small, but tastefull in my opinion; apparently too small for some reviewers, but I have no complaint.  I would rather have them understated than overstated.The first testing I did was to connect the aforementioned LITE-ON CD/DVD writer to the computer hanging on the back of my monitor using the RSH-H7C4 hub and its included USB cable.  (When the LITE-ON drive is directly attached to this computer I never have problems because the power requirement of the LITE-ON drive is easily satisfied.)  I briefly tried the hub in “bus-powered” mode (without the power supply attached). I was able to read files on the DVD writer with no problem. (This was just a brief, not comprehensive, test because my main concern was testing the more power intensive task of  burning a disk.)The next testing was in “self-powered” mode (with the power supply attached).  I successfully burned and verified an ISO image. I then disconnected the power supply and tried to burn the image again in bus-powered mode.  The burning went without any error messages, but the verification process failed.  Another attempt in bus-powered mode failed during the burning process.  Returning to self-powered mode (with the power supply attached), I was again able to successfully burn and verify the ISO image.It appears that when the DVD writer is attached through the hub and cable, burning a disk requires additional power beyond what the computer port is supplying (in order to power the circuitry in the hub and to compensate for loss over the length of the three foot cable).  USB Battery Charging 1.2 protocol (BC1.2) seems to do the job here.  I am pleased with that!  I have solved the short cable issue; I can now lay my DVD drive flat on the desk and have dependable operation.Additional testing with a much older computer, a Dell Inspiron Mini 1012 (vintage 2010), revealed an unexpected limitation: the DVD writer was unable to be recognized as a boot device by the Inspiron Mini when connected to it through the hub.  As far as I can tell, this is a limitation of the older BIOS in the Inspiron. There is no way to make this system recognize an optical drive connected via a USB hub at boot up.  After the system is booted the drive is able to be used with no problem.  It is my understanding that the more modern BIOSes of today handle this situation in a more sophisticated manner and can detect hub-connected optical drives at bootup.  I verified this by bootup tests on the computer attached to my monitor and on a modern netbook.  Both of those systems were able to recognize the DVD writer at bootup with no problem when it was connected via the hub.The Dell Inspiron Mini 1012 has USB 2.0 ports that theoretically should supply enough power for the LITE-ON CD/DVD writer, but I have always felt that it was marginal.  I would like to be able to supplement the power. The RSH-H7C4 is only a partial solution here.  It can be used with the Inspiron for burning disks since that is a process that occurs after bootup, but in the case where I need to boot from this DVD writer I might have to first transfer the DVD image to a bootable USB thumb drive and use that instead.While not a comprehensive evaluation of the RSH-H7C4, my tests have assured me that some of my problems have been solved.  I can comfortably lay the LITE-ON CD/DVD writer on the desk instead of trying to figure out how to suspend it a foot up in the air in order to attach it to the computer hanging behind my flat screen monitor.  I can also use the hub for more dependable disk burning using my Inspiron Mini 1012.I am quite impressed with the RSH-H7C4 hub and RSHTECH/Rosonway.  I've always been very excited by little “boxes” and devices that solve computer interconnection (and other) problems. The companies that come up with these solutions are frequently very clever.  RSHTECH will now be one of the first companies I look to for such solutions.  A brief look at their lineup shows devices such as:• USB switches (to share USB ports between two computers)• powered and unpowered USB hubs, some with card readers and HDMI ports• powered USB extension cables• unpowered USB extension cables with built-in signal booster chips• USB HDD/SSD enclosures• docking and cloning stations• 10 Gbps USB connected M.2 NVME/SATA SSD enclosures• 10 Gbps USB connected Magnetic M.2 NVME SSD enclosure with 100W PDRSHTECH puts an effort into including enough hard facts and specifications in their product descriptions to enable consumers to easily understand a device’s capabilities and limitations.  This fact, and my favorable impression of the RSH-H7C4 hub, give me confidence to expect good performance and value with any of their products."
    },
    {
        "review": "Review of Rosonway 7 Ports USB 3.2 Hub 36WI purchased the Rosonway 7 Ports USB 3.2 Hub 36W and tested it in two separate configurations. First with my Surface PC laptop operating Windows and second with my Mac Mini 4 operating Sequoia. The Hub, which is powered with a 36W(12V/3A) DC power adapter, comes with 7 Ports, 4 USB-C 3.2 ports and 3 USB-A 3.2 ports. It claims speeds up to 10Gbps on every port.The Hub is small and sturdy aluminum device with independent electronic switches on each port and a blue LED indicator to display power status. This enables power management over all connected devices. When I connected it to my Surface laptop PC it worked seamlessly. I was able to connect to my external mouse and keyboard, 4 thumb drives and an external backup drive. The computer recognized all the drives and could rapidly access the external backup even with all these peripherals attached and running, the hub did not get hot (only warm as would be expected).I then put the hub through an extremely tough challenge. I connected it to my Mac Mini 4 through one of the rear-facing thunderbolt/USB C ports. I then connected all my peripherals to the hub. I did not use any of the other available Mac Mini ports, only the Ronsonway Hub. I connected the following peripherals: external Kensington Trackball mouse, a Seagate Portable 4TB External Hard Drive, two thumb drives,  external speakers, a Xencelabs Quick Keys, and a backup UPS device and a second hub (7-Port USB C Hub Splitter 10Gbps with 3 USB-3.2, 4 USB-3.0 Ports) – this hub was connected to a Logitech camera.All of the peripherals worked perfectly. I could open and read all the external drives (the Seagate and the 2 thumb drives) the webcam, mouse and Quick Keys all worked without problems. Even with all these peripherals including the second hub attached and running, the hub did not get hot (only warm as would be expected).I couldn’t be happier with the performance of this compact, but powerful, hub. I made a brief video if you want to see the hub and the attachments I made to the hub.One final thought, I believe this hub is a terrific economical option for expanding a laptop or desktop to accommodate an amazing array of peripherals. This hub demonstrates that a single USB C connection to a Thunderbolt 4 port on my Mac Mini, can simultaneously and seamlessly accommodate a wide array of desktop components."
    },
    {
        "review": "This device is the perfect accessory for someone needing to connect multiple devices using a single interface. Excellent for traveling light, I'm looking forward to my next trip with the need for only this charging / data hub and my laptop. Not only does this hub provide connectivity for both USB A and USB C connections, allowing me to pass data seamlessly between all of my devices without a need to repeated disconnect / reconnect for access to ports, it also CHARGES all of them simultaneously using the included 36W power supply.It was incredibly easy to decide which devices to activate with individual power switches for each port and indicators to show which ones are active. I did NOT need to deactivate any of the ports, however, as there was ample power to charge all of my equipment at one time. That means a lot when I'm traveling with early morning departures and late arrivals each day, giving me a single place to charge everything at once while I sleep and wake up ready for the next adventure.The only thing that could have made this better would have been enough power to charge my (giant) laptop, or the ability to connect a different external (USB C) power supply to make more power available. It was still a huge improvement, though, having to carry just my laptop power brick plus the adapter for this USB hub and having all the connectivity and power I need for an extended trip away from home. My backpack (and my back) really appreciate this excellently designed device.Pros:- Has connections for both USB A and USB C adapters- 36 watts of power was enough for all my devices (phone, watch, AirPods, big camera, small camera) at one time- Provides data connectivity at the same time as powering devices- Compact shape and light weight saves room in limited travel luggage- Individual switches to selectively power outlets as need changesCONS:- Could not support high-powered external pass-through power adapter- Not enough power for a large laptop (although it does charge smaller laptops and tablets)"
    },
    {
        "review": "I have been in IT long enough to know that a USB hub is one of those products that sounds simple on paper but has a surprisingly wide range of quality in practice. I have also been burned enough times by bad ones to approach any new hub with a healthy dose of skepticism. So when I say that the Rosonway powered USB hub has genuinely impressed me, I want you to understand that it is coming from someone who does not hand out compliments to peripheral hardware easily.I have had this hub for 13 days now. That might not sound like a long time, but in the context of my daily IT work, I have put it through what I would consider a serious run of testing. This is not a hub that has been sitting on a desk powering a wireless mouse dongle and a phone charger. It has been put through a ringer of a minimum of 12-14 hours 5-6 days a week. And it was used every day. With a rotating cast of demanding devices that would expose any weakness in a lesser product.Why I Came Back to RosonwayThis is actually my second Rosonway hub. My first one is still running, still reliable, and was purchased specifically because I had grown so frustrated with other brands that I needed something I could actually count on. Before finding Rosonway the first time, I dealt with pretty much every problem a USB hub can throw at you — and often all at once. Devices dropping connection randomly. Transfer speeds that had no business being as slow as they were, even on a nominally high-speed hub. Units that died within weeks of purchase. Build quality that felt like it might give out at any moment.When you work in IT, unreliable hardware is not just an annoyance — it is a productivity problem and, at times, a professional one. The last thing I need in the middle of a task is a hub that decides to drop a device or throttle my speeds without warning. After going through several different brands and experiencing those issues repeatedly, I finally landed on Rosonway. My first unit solved all of those problems and has kept solving them ever since.So when I needed more ports, the decision to go back to Rosonway was not really a decision at all. It was the obvious move based on earned trust. Sure you could go with more notorious brands, but the bang for my buck on this brand has been optimal.Daily Use in an IT ContextMy use case is not casual. I run this hub daily as part of an IT work setup that includes both a PC and a Mac, and I switch between the two regularly. Cross-platform reliability is something I specifically look for and specifically test, because a hub that works beautifully on Windows but causes headaches on macOS is only solving half my problem.This one works correctly on both. No configuration differences, no quirks on one platform that do not exist on the other. It simply works the same way on both machines, which is exactly what you want.The range of peripherals I have connected through this hub over the past 13 days is wide. I have been running a wired USB-C to 3.5mm DAC for my Corsair headset with microphone — and I want to flag this one specifically, because audio devices are often where cheaper hubs start to show their weaknesses. A DAC demands consistent, clean power delivery. Any instability shows up immediately as audio dropout, static, or microphone signal issues. This hub has delivered clean, uninterrupted audio and microphone performance every single time.Beyond that, I have put external hard drives, thumb drives, and an SSD dock through this hub. External drives and SSD docks are about the most demanding things you can connect to a USB hub from a power and bandwidth standpoint. Drives pulling sustained power while transferring data will expose any inadequacy in a hub's power management quickly. I have had no issues — transfers run at expected speeds, the SSD dock behaves as if it is connected directly to the machine, and nothing has dropped or stuttered.I have connected all of these devices in various combinations, not just one at a time. Running multiple demanding peripherals simultaneously is where a lot of hubs start to fall apart, and this one has handled every configuration I have thrown at it without complaint.No Drivers, No NonsenseOne of the things I appreciated immediately was the plug-and-play experience. There were no extra drivers to hunt down and install. On both the PC and the Mac, I connected the hub and everything worked. That is the experience it should always be, but it is not always the experience you get — especially when you are working across two different operating systems.Anyone who has spent time troubleshooting driver issues for what should be a simple USB hub knows how much time that can waste. The fact that this product required none of that was noticed and appreciated from day one.The Physical SetupThe cable length on this hub is one of the practical details that does not always get mentioned in reviews but genuinely matters in a real workspace. It is long enough to allow for flexible positioning without being so long that it becomes a cable management problem. I was able to position the hub where it actually needed to be on my desk rather than compromising based on where the cable could reach. Small thing, but it adds up.The hub is powered, which is worth emphasizing for anyone who has tried to run demanding devices off a bus-powered hub and wondered why things were not working right. Having dedicated power through the included adapter means each port has what it needs to perform, even with multiple devices pulling from it simultaneously. For the kind of peripherals I am running — drives, an SSD dock, a DAC — powered is not optional. It is required. This hub delivers that without any hesitation.Thirteen Days and CountingI want to be honest about what 13 days of heavy daily use tells you and what it does not. It does not give you years of long-term reliability data. What it does give you is a solid read on whether a product is going to perform under real working conditions without hand-holding. I have not been gentle with this hub, in terms of use. I have been plugging and unplugging devices frequently, running demanding transfers, switching between operating systems, and connecting combinations of peripherals that test the power distribution. In all of that, I have not encountered a single failure, dropout, connection issue, or performance problem.That tracks with my experience with my first Rosonway hub, which is still running well after a year and half. The consistency between the two units tells me something about how Rosonway approaches quality control. It is one thing for one product to perform well. It is another for two separate purchases, at different times, to both deliver the same reliable experience.Who This Hub Is ForIf you are running a setup with demanding peripherals — external drives, audio devices, docking solutions — and you need something that will perform reliably across PC and Mac without driver headaches or connection instability, this hub is worth serious consideration. If you have been through the frustrating cycle of buying cheaper hubs, dealing with the problems they inevitably bring, and then replacing them, I understand that cycle because I lived it for longer than I should have.The Rosonway powered USB hub is not the flashiest product in the world. It does not need to be. It has been dependable so far. It does exactly what it is supposed to do, every time, without requiring you to babysit it or troubleshoot it or work around its limitations. In the world of peripheral hardware, that kind of consistency is a huge bonus in my line of work.Two for two on Rosonway products. I will be back if I need more ports. I am satisfied with my purchase."
    },
    {
        "review": "TLDR: This is a fast, powerful, versatile 7-port USB 3.2 Gbps hub that gives you lots of ports which is especially useful for modern laptops which often only have one port.This connects to a laptop/PC via a USB-C cable. It also needs a power cable to function so make sure you are close to a wall outlet or even better, a surge protector.You get 7 ports - 4 USB-C ports and 3 classic USB-A ports. They are all version 3.2 ports with a speeds up to 10Gbps. You can toggle each one on/off with the buttons on the side. The ports have a light that comes on with enabled.It's got sturdy aluminum body so it's safe to transport and won't get damaged in your backpack.Performance‑wise, it’s handled everything I’ve thrown at it. I’ve had multiple drives, a mouse, a keyboard, and a couple of random accessories plugged in at the same time, and it didn’t choke or overheat — just got slightly warm, which is normal. The 10Gbps ports definitely move files quickly, and the external power supply keeps everything stable. It’s not meant to fast‑charge big devices, but for data and powering smaller gear, it’s been rock‑steady.If you need a dependable, powered hub with a mix of USB‑A and USB‑C ports and don’t want to spend a fortune, this one’s easy to recommend.The company: I'm a big fan of this brand. I own other products from them, and they are all great quality."
