[
    {
        "review": "This keyboard offers a truly premium feel at an excellent price. The build quality is solid, the keys are responsive and comfortable, and typing on it is a pleasure. Everything feels well made and reliable, making it great for both work and everyday use."
    },
    {
        "review": "The keyboard feels solid and sounds much better than I expected for the price. The keys are smooth, the compact 75% layout saves desk space, and it still keeps the function row and arrow keys. The wireless connection was easy to set up, and switching between devices has been convenient.The RGB lighting looks good without being too bright, and the battery has lasted well with the lights turned down. The software could be a little easier to use, but I did not need it for basic settings. Overall, it is a comfortable and good-looking keyboard for gaming, work, and everyday use."
    },
    {
        "review": "I bought this keyboard after spending some time looking for a wireless mechanical keyboard that I could use for both work and gaming, and I’m really happy with my purchase.What I like most is the feel of the keys when typing and the sound. It has that characteristic mechanical keyboard sound that is very satisfying without becoming annoying when I’m working for several hours. I also think the 75% size is perfect: it takes up much less space on my desk without sacrificing the keys I actually use.I also really like the way it looks. It feels stylish, solid, and much more polished than I expected. The wireless connection has worked very well, and being able to switch between devices is really convenient.I mainly use it for work and do a lot of typing, so it was important to me that it be comfortable as well as nice-looking, and so far it has completely met my expectations.When I bought it, I didn’t realize that the letters were printed on the front of the keycaps rather than on top. It only took me a few hours to get used to it, though, and now I actually like it because I think the lettering may wear off more slowly.The only thing I didn’t like is that it doesn’t have a dedicated “Home” key. It has an “End” key, but not “Home.” That said, the software makes it incredibly easy to remap keys and customize their functions, so it works perfectly for me anyway.I also like that you can choose from several colorful lighting effects, although I usually keep the lights turned off because I find them a little distracting while working. The keyboard is somewhat heavy, so I would consider it more of a desk keyboard than something you would want to carry around, which is completely fine for my use."
    },
    {
        "review": "The AULA F75 Pro isn’t just a keyboard — it’s a life upgrade. From the moment I unboxed it, I knew this thing was built different. The build quality feels premium in a way that makes everything else on my desk feel insecure. The weight, the finish, the layout — chef’s kiss.But the sound? Oh man… pure mechanical ASMR. Every keystroke is a buttery, creamy “thock” that makes typing feel less like a task and more like a treat. It’s so smooth and satisfying I found myself typing things I didn’t even need to type — emails, notes, grocery lists, lorem ipsum paragraphs — just to hear it sing.Key layout & switch orientation: One thing I absolutely love is how the keys are south-facing, sitting slightly inward instead of pointing outward toward you. It gives the whole board a cleaner, sleeker look and makes the RGB shine through in a softer, more premium way.Wireless performance: flawless. Zero lag, zero hiccups, zero drama. It connects instantly and stays rock-solid whether I’m gaming, working, or just vibing on YouTube comments.Battery life: absurdly good. I swear this thing runs on wizard energy because I keep checking the percentage and it keeps refusing to go down.RGB: The lighting is vibrant without being over-the-top, and the multi-function knob makes switching effects, brightness, and colors stupidly satisfying. One smooth twist and you can go from a calm ambient glow to full-on gamer neon. It’s intuitive, responsive, and makes controlling the RGB feel extra premium without the need of a software which is a plus.Overall? This keyboard is elite. Legendary. A masterpiece of clicky, thocky engineering. If you want something that feels incredible, sounds incredible, and looks incredible, the AULA F75 Pro is the one. I absolutely, genuinely loved this product."
    },
    {
        "review": "Just, WOW!This thing is HEAVY but feels like it was made out of butter. The exterior casing is made out of what feels like dense '90s style plastic which doesn't show fingerprints. If I didn't know better I thought it was metal covered in some kind of coating.The keys on this thing are the smoothest I have ever felt in my life. Just pure buttery smoothness, but a really nice clickety clack when you start typing. There isn't a spring sound like a lot of old keyboards from the '80s up to the mid '90s, but the clacking sound is still very satisfying and retro in its own way.The lighting patterns are just out of this world. I expected it to just light up, but it has quite a few different patterns like rippling, pulsing, and my favorite is some kind of mode that makes the colors jump around. The fact that the lettering is on the front of the key instead of the top is really cool.So far the Bluetooth and 2.4 GHz connections have both been really good, I haven't tried it plugged in but I'm sure it would be good. Seriously, the quality on this thing is unreal and exceeds my expectations by several miles. I thought I was just getting a fairly decent plastic keyboard that would weigh about the same as most others and have springy sounding keys. This thing reminds me of some kind of professional music gear. If you aren't sure, just get one, this is seriously one of the highest quality items I have ever received from Amazon from a non-top-tier brand."
    },
    {
        "review": "I'm impressed. Coming from a 4 year old Corsair mechanical keyboard with cherry mx switches, I had high hopes that this keyboard would get the job done. It lacks dedicated media keys, but you still have media keys by using the function key.The battery is excellent to start; I hope it lasts just as long as my Corsair did. The weight is a little beefy despite it being mostly plastic and thicc. The size is perfect if you intend to use it on your lap or TV tray. There are many different LED modes as well, which is nice, as well as the fact that you can set these modes without having to install some sort of app to control them.While I primarily use the 2.4 connection, I do like the fact that it supports 3 bt devices. The response rate is very good and on par with the Corsair. You will have to get used to the arrow key layout with end, pg up, pg dn, and delete being in one single vertical row.Probably the most intriguing thing that made me want to even look at it was the front etched keys, which were perfect for my use case. I usually rest the keyboard on a shelf slightly higher than my shoulder when I sit so the keyboard is closer to my eye level than my hands. These keys make it easier to quickly see and press some custom commands I have instead of having to pick up the keyboard, sit it on my lap or tray, and then use my commands.The key presses are also very muted from the cherry mx switches, which is a plus and slightly more satisfying.Overall, I'm Impressed.(update as of 3-15-26)Nearly a year later, the battery's lifespan has decreased to about 50% of its original capacity."
    },
    {
        "review": "I’m honestly really impressed with the AULA F75 Pro Wireless Mechanical Keyboard. Right out of the box, it feels like a premium product. The build quality is solid and has a nice weight to it, so it stays firmly in place and doesn’t slide around during use.One of my favorite things about this keyboard is the sound. The clicking is super satisfying without being overly loud—it just has that perfect mechanical feel that makes typing enjoyable.The RGB lighting is another standout feature. At first, I didn’t realize there was software available, but once I found the link at the bottom of the product page and installed it, the level of customization blew me away. You can really dial in the exact colors and effects you want.Overall, this keyboard exceeded my expectations. Great feel, great sound, and excellent customization options. Definitely a solid buy."
    },
    {
        "review": "I bought this keyboard for my husband and switched the keycaps to fit his aesthetic as well as so he can read a bit better. (He’s new to the computer club used to be console and he’s got bad eyesight from the military.) so in the picture the keycaps are changed but not the switches! The original keycaps it comes with is a grey with the letters, symbols, and words on the side.Even though I swapped them out I want to state they are absolutely excellent quality, super soft matte feel, and low profile. I kept the keycaps for future use instead of tossing them or offering them up to my family.Which is what I usually do with manufactured keycaps. So, that’s how good they are! That and they honestly are an absolutely gorgeous color in person despite just being greytones.Now onto more of the benefits this keyboards the forth of fifth I’ve customized (I just customize as a hobby for friends and family)This one though is the first of this brand and oh my gosh do I regret not buying this brand sooner! Mainly for myself because of all the bonuses with this keyboard. Haha.Firstly, the keyboard came in awesome packaging and when I realized it can be used wirelessly too! Absolutely fantastic! I didn’t check for that before purchasing so it was a super awesome surprise!And the quality too my husband has absolutely loved getting to use his new keyboard and he got so giddy with the RGB too.I feel like building wise too this is a great keyboard for beginners. If you want a nice keyboard that has easy hot-swappable capabilities this is definitely where to start!And the volume button is such a bonus too. It makes accessibility count with this keyboard. That along with how compact it is too it takes such little space. To put into retrospect his desk is only I think 38 inches width and he still has plenty of room on each side of his desk for his snacks and drinks because of how little space the keyboard is.Literally no issues have come from the keyboard, it even with his wonky usb ports (a custom built computer composed of old/used parts.) it works well and doesn’t have any connectivity issues.So, to put all the perks together:-compact and comfortable-easy to swap keycaps around and switches—switches it came with are the perfect click for those who don’t like it to be an overbearing sound.—perfect for beginners of customizing keyboards.-Volume nob to control volume with easy access rather than fighting around your desk to get to the speaker control.-has wired, Bluetooth, and dongle capabilities which cleans up wire management.— Battery life is great so charging doesn’t happen as often and it lasts even longer with rgb turned off.- I almost forgot: also, has amazing presets of rgb colors that are fun and is perfect for zoning out to when you are having writers or artist block.Now, for the jealousy caption. I need to save up to get me one too! Because, this keyboard is everythingggg and I am definitely in need of an upgrade from my wired keyboard as well as all the perks of this keyboard.Definitely, looking forward to customizing one for myself down the line! This company is for sure my go too for keyboards. I highly recommend!For now I’ll just enjoy seeing the smile on my husband’s face every time he looks at his keyboard while we game together! The best Christmas purchase I’ve ever made!"
    },
    {
        "review": "It’s awesome sounds good just very loud, and takes a bite to get used to"
    },
    {
        "review": "A legendary keyboard in its shape. Its price is excellent and its sound is awesome. Its presence, to be honest, feels heavy in it and feels tired. I strongly recommend it."
    },
    {
        "review": "First of all, if you don't have much knowledge of mechanical keyboards and want to start with this keyboard, it is a very good option. The quality is felt from the moment you take it out of the box, it is a heavy keyboard and that does not mean it is bad, it feels very good to type on it, plus the sound is quite different from a keyboard that normally comes with a new computer or that you simply bought a cheap one at a Walmart or on the internet, it sounds firm and good when typing. Compared to cheaper keyboards, these mechanical switches are very sensitive, I am not referring to their duration, but to the force necessary to press them since the force needed is minimal, which makes them less burdensome for long days at the computer. It has several RGB lighting modes, for my part I only have it in white and at a moderate brightness, because yes, the brightness of the backlight is quite a bit. Another thing I liked is that you can check the keyboard battery with the key combination: FN + B. Which is very useful since it does not have another battery indicator. Just to conclude, it is an excellent keyboard that is very worth it, and if you find it at a discount, do not hesitate to purchase it."
    },
    {
        "review": "As a developer, it is very important to have a good keyboard. It just arrived and so far the weight, feel, connectivity are excellent. I hope it lasts long enough."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "Best keyboard I've ever owned. Sturdy case, sleek profile, the layout makes this keyboard incredibly easy to clean without the need to dismantle the keys in the process. The White LEDs are bright and easy to see in the dark, unlike many backlit keyboards that can make it harder to read the keys. The responsiveness both in typing and in the tactile feel of every key clacking on release is a delight. I was skeptical when the sale brought the price below $35, but I could not be more impressed with the quality upon arrival. As a friend has been using a slightly different G4 model for 5 years, I have every expectation that I will be using this keyboard for years to come. Very happy customer."
    },
    {
        "review": "I ordered one of these after my place of employment got tired of keyboards failing constantly. After several months of outright abusive use by my co-workers, they still performed flawlessly with a lovely sense of tactile feedback, soda spills aside. One failed only after half a thermos of coffee was spilled into it, but that's to be expected from anything not waterproof rated.After ordering one for myself to use at home, I LOVE typing on it. It's my first true mechanical keyboard, and it's pleasant to just tap away, typing whatever I feel like. Each key feels like it's got two levels of depression (almost like me): First a clear 'catch' when the key activates and types, and then another more firm one when you reach the bottom of the key well. I've yet to have it double-type anything even when my clumsy fingers wanted to, although it hasn't saved me from butterfingering my keystrokes in games.As far as gaming goes, it's not PERFECT. It does make an audible noise when typing, so when you are frantically spamming the strafe or jump keys, it's going to sound like clicking the mouse over and over, but it's really no worse than your average mouse. If you're wearing headphones and blasting your speakers, you won't notice it. If you're in a silent small room, you can't avoid hearing it. Find your medium.Its clearly more meant for office work, but I find it good for gaming, and it's sturdy and heavy enough it doesn't move around at all or feel like it'll die if I drop my phone on it."
    },
    {
        "review": "Satisfying but quiet clicks, decent lighting, solid construction, and amazing responsiveness give this keyboard a 5 ⭐ from me. I game pretty much everyday and Logitech is the only keyboard/mouse company I find worthwhile AND affordable. As a major plus, it's also very ergonomic and easy to use for long periods of time."
    },
    {
        "review": "Just got it so not a complete review. But so far, I really like it. I'm not a gamer, but just needed a keyboard upgrade and this is perfect. So far :)Note: True, there are no painted letters/numbers on the keys. Once usb plugged in, everything is seen with the backlight on the keys. I figured out the reason: painted letters and numbers wear off after a while. This see-through letters/numbers (presumably) will always remain. You don't use the keys unless plugged in anyhoo! So, I see no problem!It's very nicely weighted and has a nice feel on the keys. Great for the $35 price I paid!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I can't tell you how well this works for gaming, but if you're looking to buy this as a Mac keyboard for standard daily use, definitely read on.This keyboard is far better for a PC.  It doesn't have matching keys for a Mac keyboard, so you'll have reduced functionality with key commands.  These are differences I've come across so far:- Instead of a CMD key on both sides of the space bar, this keyboard has only one on the left of the spacebar that functions that way, and it's not in the position of the usual Mac CMD key.  (see my pic)- There's no volume control on this keyboard, so you'll have to have your Mac keyboard available to change volume unless you keep volume control visible on your menu bar.- Screen dimming requires holding this keyboard's fn key and then using F1 or F2.- Some special function keys on this keyboard don't do anything on a Mac.Here's what I do like about the keyboard:- Satisfying click.  The keys are like old school keyboards with large keys that actually had feel to them.  The volume of the click is kind of loud though, so if you need to maintain a quieter environment this keyboard won't do.- The attached 10 key is nice to have.- It's corded so no batteries required.- The keyboard is sturdy feeling with just-right weight to it- The key backlighting is adjustable- No quick trigger keys means no accidental double lettering- Because the keyboard rises high off your desktop compared to modern keyboards, make sure you have a seating position that lets you keep your hands lower than normal.Overall this keyboard is probably great for gaming or for PC users who don't have to keep it down while using it.  If you're a Mac user, especially one who needs a quiet environment, you should look at different models."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a mechanical keyboard. That means it is noisy where it has the traditional keyboard sound. I personally love it! The key are far more responsive than the soft-touch keyboards that are mostly used today. The clicking of the keys could be distracting to someone sharing an office, however, if you work in a private office, this keyboard is great. Also, the keyboard is backlit which makes it that much more useful when working late nights in a darker environment. It is constructed of metal and has a slip resistant base that stays put on my keyboard slide."
    },
    {
        "review": "I've been very happy with this keyboard for everyday work use. The mechanical keys have a great feel and make typing much more enjoyable, especially during long periods of writing and data entry. The build quality feels solid, and the keyboard has a clean, professional look that fits well in an office setup."
    },
    {
        "review": "I bought this keyboard after returning another one from a major retailer that the letters were wearing off of the keys after less than a weeks worth of use.  I love how it lights up making the keys easy to see.  This lighting feature can also be turned off for dimmed.  It looks a little different from standard keyboards and it seems like the keys are just slightly closer together than a standard keyboard.  I also like that it is not as wide as a standard Microsoft keyboard saving me a couple of inches on my desktop."
    },
    {
        "review": "Very good"
    },
    {
        "review": "I am using this keyboard right now to type this review. I got one of these for work, and I like it so much I bought another one for home. The brown switches have tactile feedback, but no audible click. The feel and sound of these keys while typing is very satisfying. This keyboard is a bit noisy, but I haven't heard any complaints about that so far. I much prefer this mechanical keyboard to my old membrane keyboards. I am still getting used to it, so I make a few typos, but the frequency of them is diminishing. Build quality is excellent, the chassis is aluminum. There is no pointless, annoying RGB backlighting. Only the keys light up, which makes it easy to use in the dark. I would recommend this keyboard. It's more expensive than membrane keyboards, but I think it's worth the extra money."
    },
    {
        "review": "Excellent, smooth when pressing the keys. Some noise, but I like the sound. It does not have programmable keys. It feels durable."
    },
    {
        "review": "I wanted to wait a couple months before writing this review, give myself time to decide my opinion.Well, I would say it is excellent, one can usually rely on Logitech to provide a good product and this is a prime example.The noise level is not the loudest I have heard from mechanical keyboards. Besides, if you want the responsiveness of a mechanical keyboard, you have to expect and accept a certain level of click-clack.I use it mostly to play games and secondly to write stories, as such it works well. While I do not play professionally, or online, nor care to, during my usage I have not noticed any lag or ghosting.In particular, I like the while lighting, it is not strong, but not weak either, allowing me to see the keys in any lighting situation. Which I appreciate, as the popular RGB keyboards are a bit too \"loud\" for me.It is also very tight and small for a mechanical, and fits comfortably on a small desk.I highly recommend this keyboard!"
    },
    {
        "review": "The person who gave it to him loves the Logitech brand and was very happy so I think it's very good"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "It's taking a little getting used to the slightly different arrangement of some keys but man... overall, this keyboard KICKS TAIL!!!  I love how it lights up, I'm always working in low light or the dark and my eyes aren't what they used to be so I just can't tell you how much of a difference it makes.  I also feel like the key symbols won't/can't wear out because they're backlit.  Typical keyboards have letter and words wear off over time, but I don't see how that's possible with this keyboard.  It's not a \"gamer keyboard\" it's just a great basic keyboard with all the functions you need.  Keystrokes feel amazing!  Not sure how to describe but when you're typing it just feels really nice.  A real quality built product and worth every penny.  I'm considering buying another before they don't make them anymore... LOL  Oh, the battery lasts forever on this thing too.  You can turn it OFF when you're done for the day and you can set the keyboard to pair/work with multiple computers (even have keys configured differently if you like).  The backlight shuts off when you remove your hands from the keyboard or stop typing for about 5 seconds, but its got this really cool \"motion detection\" feature that once you put your hands over the keys again or start typing it lights right back up!  I'm telling you if you're in the market for a new BLUETOOTH keyboard and are willing to pay a little more for a NICE one... this is the board to have!!!  I really love it.  I'll give you the 1 thing I didn't like about it as I began using it...  The FUNCTION ROW of keys has no space above the NUMBERS or Number pad, etc.  So it's super easy to hit the wrong keys sometimes.  I'll often go to use the \"home\" key and end up hitting the key to switch between machines, or the biggest one is the \"lock\" key the farthest top right key over the number pad's \"minus/dash\" key.  I often go to hit the \"minus/dash\" key and end up locking my windows (having to sign in again).  HOWEVER, using the Logitech software that is used to configure the keyboard I was able to just disable that lock key.  I don't need a KEY for that anyway.  When I want to lock my Windows session (log out) without shutting down, I'll just do it the normal way with my mouse (Start > Power > Lock).  No big, been doing that forever anyway.  Anyway, this keyboard is awesome.  Especially, once you get used to it.  Most times I get a new keyboard and have to get used to the key arrangements and different spacing anyway.  Might as well do it with a great keyboard.  😊👍🏻  P.S.  I have always been a Microsoft keyboard/mouse kinda guy.  I didn't think much of Logitech until I got the MX Master 2 Mouse, which is now also my favorite ever.  This keyboard goes with that mouse it's sold in bundles they're of the same product set."
    },
    {
        "review": "I love this keyboard. I use the same monitor for my gaming PC and for my work Surface Pro. I had a wired keyboard which I had to unplug from the gaming PC and plug into the docking station every morning and then do the reverse after my work hours. It became tedious after a while, so I decided to upgrade to a wireless keyboard that can switch between multiple devices without having me crawl under my desk to do so.This one seemed not too expensive, had full width, and reviews were decent. I'm very pleased. Switching between devices is handled with 3 dedicated buttons for this specific purpose, no crawling required. I used the dongle to pair it with the gaming PC, and Bluetooth to pair it with my work computer. Now I can work from the kitchen or the yard if I want to.It's slightly louder than I expected, but is by no means clickety like some others I've used. Typing is now completely satisfying. The texture of the buttons is soft and the feedback is addictive and makes me want to type forever.As an unexpected bonus, I was able to connect the keyboard to my phone and type in mobile apps with a full keyboard!! What a novel and refreshing experience.The only complaint from my daughter is that it doesn't have colored backlight. It's just plain white."
    },
    {
        "review": "If you need a keyboard that fits in a crowded desktop or you just like a clean and neat desk looks, and if you work with applications that require precision on what you type, coding, CAD, the feel and precision of a mechanical keyboard is what you need. Love it. I've heard it's great for games too, but I don't play games at all, so I can't tell, but I bet durability required for games is similar to software coding, tons of strokes per minute. Games are certainly more rough, due to adrenaline, that can be hard on a keyboard. Not my thing, I need precision, and compact format."
    },
    {
        "review": "So I just received and began to use this keyboard today. I am pretty blown away by how nice it looks, how good it feels, and just the experience overall. Compared to my previous keyboard, I already like this a fair amount more. For reference, I was using the Corsair k57 RGB wireless. Also a quite nice keyboard, but this has a much more premium feel. It's also much more professional looking, which is why I chose it as I plan to use this in an office environment. Some thoughts below.-The clicks are super nice, I've never used a \"clicky\" switch with feedback like this before and I quite enjoy it. Probably my favorite feedback I've had from just typing on a keyboard. The various corsair boards I've used in the past I typically chose a quiet and/or a linear switch.-Overall a very nice, professional look. I wouldn't expect any weird looks with a keyboard like this in a professional environment.-Pairing was easy, plugged in the receiver and it instantly connected; no other input needed. Even the sister product to this never did that for me, the MX master 3S mouse. I had a bit of a struggle trying to get that to pair using the included receiver, as I recall. (Otherwise one of my favorite mice, also coming from a mostly corsair brand background). There is also bluetooth pairing, if preferred. (or if the receiver refuses to work, like my MX master 3S.)One minor negative is the backlight. The first problem was even getting the keyboard to light up at all. The keys for the keyboard backlight simply did not seem to function at all, no matter if I had the FN key held or not. Puzzled, I installed logitech's software and looked for options there. No luck. A bit annoyed at this point. Luckily, their software offered me access to an upcoming beta version of their software, \"Logitech Options\", so I downloaded that to poke around and see if there were more settings there. As it turns out there were. Oddly, they didn't work though. The keyboard refused to light up, the keys meant to dim or brighten it doing nothing at all. As I began a search to see if this was a common issue, the backlight finally came on. No idea what changed. Pretty standard white LED backlights. Lots of pissed off customers on forums about it though. Apparently, Logitech has been pretty silent about the issue. I'd be pissed too, if my $200 keyboard didn't have a functioning backlight due to software issues.With that rambling anecdote, the backlight itself is just fine, about what I expected for a professional keyboard like this. Just clean white LEDs.In summary, a very nice looking, nice feeling premium keyboard. Pretty unassuming in a professional environment. Easy setup. Quite happy with it.Edit: The backlight does not seem to remember brightness settings between use sessions, so if you will get super annoyed or angry about having to hit brightness up each time you use, that's something to consider. Most annoyingly, if the room is deemed \"too bright\" by the sensor, the backlight will not turn on at all. Understandable, but I'd still like the option."
    },
    {
        "review": "I replaced a Logitech 860 with the MX, the quality and feel of the MX is very good. I love the feel of the keys. The key setup is more tighter than the 860, but after a couple days I’ve became accustomed to it and I like it. The difference in the feel and quality is significantly better than the 860."
    },
    {
        "review": "I was very disappointed when I started using this overpriced keyboard.First of all, the build quality is inexcusable for a $150+ keyboard, especially from a brand like Logitech. The space bar and the backspace keys squeak horribly every time I press them. I understand that can be fixed by applying some white silicone grease, but I shouldn't have to do that with an expensive keyboard right out of the box. In addition, the rubber foot across the back of the keyboard has already started to peel off.I purchased the tactile (quiet typing) version, and while it isn't the loudest keyboard I have ever typed on, \"quiet\" is not at all how I would describe it. Someone in the next room or cubicle would definitely be able to hear you typing on this. I wonder how loud the \"clicky\" version is...And now, to address the backlight issue: Several reviews have mentioned problems with the backlight. It seems to function \"as intended\", but the design choices really make me scratch my head. The backlight will turn off just a few seconds after you stop typing, and doesn't turn on again until you press a key. That means that if you aren't constantly typing, the backlight will be turning on and off in a way that is very visually distracting at the bottom of your vision. If you take your hands away from the keyboard, you will not have the keys illuminated to help orient your hands when you start typing again. In a dark room that means you can't see anything on the keys. Even the timing of the backlight is inconsistent. Sometimes it turns off while I am still typing, sometimes it stays on for a minute or two with no input, like it got stuck.At first I thought I just had to adjust the timeout settings on the Logi Options+ app. Nope! No options! Just a couple of annoying, distracting backlight animations (like, random keys illuminating, why would anyone want that?!). I'm not sure who signed-off on the design for this thing or what they were thinking.Furthermore, the caps lock key has a distracting blinking whenever it is on, especially annoying if you are gaming and caps lock is set to something like toggle run/walk.The keyboard is Bluetooth only, which seems to work fine. But I would have liked to have the option to plug it in like a normal USB keyboard. As far as I know the USB plug is only for charging, but also leaves an always illuminated and fairly bright \"charging\" LED, very obnoxious if your computer is in your bedroom.In short: Don't waste your money on this. I will be returning mine."
    },
    {
        "review": "I just finished my first long work session with this keyboard. I'm glad I bought it. It's very easy to type on. It's my first mechanical keyboard of any kind. I went with the quieter version. You still get the clicking sounds, just not super loud. I finally understand what people mean when they say typing on a mechanical keyboard feels more satisfying compared to non-mechanical ones. Highly recommend this keyboard if you're looking to get into mechanical keyboards. Definitely won't be my last."
    },
    {
        "review": "Original review: It's a keyboard an it worksAfter hearing people rave about mechanical keyboards, I decided to get myself one. I am not sure how representative of mechanical keyboards this one is, but I find it to be very similar to everything I have used before. The keys don't feel \"clicky\" at all. In fact, I am constantly running into an issue of resting my hands on the keyboard, and \"mushing down\" a key by mistake (in other words, \"wwwwwwwwwwwwwwwww\" because my finger pressed a key without me even noticing).I think I can get used to pulling my hands away from the keyboard to keep that from happening, but I find it weird that I now have to do this. I assumed the keys would be harder to press, not mushier than the cheap-o keyboards I have had.Another issue I have had is that the keyboard is not flat. I have tested it on multiple surfaces, including a glass table. I ended having to prop one side up with some paper. Not too happy about that.For whatever reason, this does not have a print screen key. Luckily you can remap it using the Logi Options+ software.Honestly, at this price, I am not sure I would recommend this keyboard. Ok, so why 4 stars? It does have some positive points. First, it looks pretty sharp. Second, aside from not sitting flat, it feels solid / well-built / quality. It is small, yet it is heavier than most keyboards. I am used to keyboards lasting \"forever\", so I will update here if/when I run into issues. Third, switching between different machines works flawlessly. Fourth, it allows me to bring my settings to all computers (e.g. remapping the screen capture key). Fifth, it has a rechargeable battery and it lasts long enough that I never have to worry. I do have a charging cable right on my desk, so every once in a while I just leave it plugged in, so I have never seen it go below 85%. What else can I say, it is a keyboard and it works.-----Updated review: beware the USB dongles.Logitech mouses and keyboards (e.g. \"MX Mechanical\", \"MX Master 3s\", \"MX Anywhere 3\") connect either via Bluetooth, or via a USB dongle. However, the USB dongles are not all the same. For example, the \"MX mechanical\" keyboard only connects to a \"bolt\" dongle; and the \"MX Anywhere 3\" mouse only connects to a \"unifying\" dongle. Unfortunately, there is no clear guide / summary for which devices go together. You just have to check each device before buying to see what dongle it uses. Devices in the same generation are *not* guaranteed to use the same type of dongle.Here is my experience: I setup for myself a MX mechanical keyboard + MX Master 3s mouse and everything works fine. Both devices connect to a \"bolt\" dongle, so I have a \"bolt\" dongle on my laptop, and another \"bolt\" dongle on a desktop computer. I can switch between laptop and desktop with a press of a button. The keyboard/mouse itself is fine, the review can be seen above.My wife saw my setup and wanted the same, but she didn't want the MX Master 3s mouse (\"it is too big\"), so I got her a this same \"MX Mechanical\" keyboard and a pink \"MX Anywhere 3\". As it turns out, the mouse does not come with a \"bolt\" dongle, it comes with a \"unifying\" dongle. So now I have one \"bolt\" dongle which connects only to the keyboard, and one \"unifying\" dongle which connects only to the mouse. Thus, to \"fix\" this situation, I either need to use Bluetooth for everything (which I was trying to avoid to begin with), or I need to purchase 1 extra bolt dongle and 1 extra unifying dongle, so that each computer has both a \"unifying\" and a \"bolt\" dongle. Let's hope I have enough USB ports, or I will also need a USB hub.Even if you are aware that different dongles exist, you still need to be careful. If you buy a \"MX Anywhere 3\", you get a \"unifying\" dongle, but if you buy a \"MX Anywhere 3 for business\", then it has a \"bolt\" dongle.  This is, by the way, how I ended up with a \"unifying\" dongle - while browsing the products, I opened up two tabs, one with a black MX Anywhere 3, and one with a pink MX Anywhere 3, except one of them was \"for business\" and the other was not.You might think this mess would warrant giant disclaimers (\"DOES NOT HAVE A BOLT CONNECTOR\"), or some kind of guide for which devices go together, but nope. For extra hilarity, Amazon bundles a \"MX mechanical mini\" (bolt) with a \"MX Anywhere 3\" (unifying). The whole thing is confusing, misleading, and just terrible user/customer experience. If I could start over, I would just get something else."
    },
    {
        "review": "Product is excellent, but be aware this is a US layout keyboard. If you want a UK layout (or European) you need to look for this specifically on Amazon."
    },
    {
        "review": "This keyboard is pretty amazing. I own a HHKB, and a Pok3r. I build and customize my own keyboards. I bought this mainly because of the Logitech Flow feature. But first, I wanted to talk about the keyboard itself and why I think this is the best office keyboard to date.The build quality is amazing. It's sturdy, and the internals are very tight, which means, no rattling, no added noise or reverbiation. As a result, this is a very quiet mechanical keyboard (I can only speak for the brown switch version).The keycaps are made out of recycled plastic, but they don't feel cheap. They are quite smooth and easy to type with. The typing experience is very consistent and smooth (see type test photo). My typing speed is consistent with my other keyboards, but for me, it's definitely feels much better to type with tactile silent switches compared to my other keyboards.Even though this keyboard is a low profile keyboard, there are flip up feet at the bottom of the keyboard to lift it up a little more to get a better angle if that's what you prefer.The lights are nice, but what makes this keyboard really stand out is when it doesn't use the lights at all. Without the backlights on, this keyboard can do 10 months on 1 charge, which is completely unheard of for a mechanical full size wireless keyboard. I've had this keyboard for a couple of days on the whole time, and by now, my other wireless 60% would have died and required a recharge.There is only one issue with this keyboard which is customization. For Macros, only some of the keys (F Keys and Cursor Targeting Group) can be customized vs what I am used to, which is being able to customize every single key. For customizing the physical look itself such as changing the switches or the keycaps, this keyboard cannot be customized like that. The spacebar doesn't use standard stabilizer stem widths. You can replace every key cap except for the spacebar. You also cannot change the switches unless you know some soldering which is not recommended.As for the reason why I bough the keyboard. I absolutely love it! The logitech flow option allows me to move from my multiple computer setups with only one keyboard and mouse. I often multitask, I have a computer running 24/7 as my \"smart tv\" as well as my personal laptop, and my work computer. Considering how I can flow through all those computers from one set of mouse and keyboard without having to physically switch the mouse to use that specific computer is absolutely game changing. I feel like I am using one computer, even though they are all completely seperated.Whenever a product can make someone's life easier, is when you know you have something truly revolutionary. For work and every day use, this is absolutely the best office keyboard to date."
    },
    {
        "review": "I really like this keyboard!!!In fact, I would say that this is a good/reasonable successor to the magical, venerable K800 Wireless and WiredI (like most) have an old wired K800 (attached to my work laptop), and was  sooooo sad to see that I couldn't get another one.  I took the leap and purchased the K800 wireless as a backup (what I writing this on..) for my personal laptop, and it truly is such a wonderful easy feeling keyboard.Again, logitech has discontinued the wireless one - enter to MX Keys (full size)I think that this one has that old school IBM-style feel, yet waay more robust, heavy (surprisingly), and modular (regarding key caps and triggers)I think, if past is prologue (as it invulnerably i..) this product to will either disappear, or get worse - I will by a 1 or 2 for safe keeping"
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a US layout keyboard on sale in the UK... but no mention of that on the description."
    },
    {
        "review": "This keyboard is a solid little keyboard. It’s very well built and has a satisfying feel and noise while typing. I really like how easy it is to switch between different computers. I have it set to use both my desktop PC and my MacBook Air when I dock it on my desk. It knows which is which and changes the keyboard layout accordingly. Everything just works. The battery life is excellent and it charges up really quickly over usb c. The Logitech Options app is really easy to use with everything laid out in such a way to just make sense. Also; the back lighting is simple but still nice.Ok onto my complaints. First I wish the key caps were a better material. They get glossy really quick from the slightly dirtiest fingers. I dunno if that’s due to the dark grey colour or the material itself. But for the price it should have better key caps. Second; I wish this thing had full rgb backlighting. The white is nice; but full colour would be better. Ok that’s it.So overall this is a great desktop keyboard that you could also take with you if you wanted to use your laptop or iPad on the go with it. It’s great for multi device use and the typing experience is really nice!! If you’re coming from the keyboard that came with your Mac or PC… or want something small and portable; this is definitely for you!"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "Bought this for my wife’s WFH setup. She fell in love with the Creamy Switch craze on TikTok and this was a reasonably priced keyboard that fit the bill. I found a cute sakura themed keycap set to replace these, but she said she’d have been happy with the blues and purples that came preinstalled.It looks great, feels and sounds fantastic, and does everything you’d want from a keyboard. She loves the size, specifically that it includes the number pad. Not enough decent keyboards do these days. People want minimalism in everything, but the functionality of a dedicated number pad is hard to beat.Connecting is super easy. We’re using the hardwired USB-C option, but I was able to use Bluetooth on my laptop at work while I was testing and installing the other keycaps prior to giving this as a birthday gift. She loves that it’s such a lightweight feel of typing, even noticeably so to someone without the heavy handed typing of myself, having grown up on IBM and Alps switches which allow for some decent banging.I’m not a keyboard snob or really a deep enthusiast, but some of my friends and coworkers that care more than I do really loved it as well. It’s a crowd pleaser, and that wonderful little subdued THOK on each tap is soothing as foretold."
    },
    {
        "review": "I LOVE this keyboard!! After spending two weeks researching I decided on the Aula F99 and I am so happy with my decision. I’m not a gamer but wanted a full keyboard for work and this checked all the boxes. The sound is so creamy and reminds me of marbles or smooth stones. It’s so fun to type on and the RGB is kinda fun too. Bluetooth connected with ease, so did wired. Haven’t tried the dongle yet but so far it’s been plug and play play with no issues. Feels sturdy not cheap. Sounds great out of the box. Ordered the Gatreon Milky Yellow switches but I may not even need them cuz I’m kinda digging this sound already. It’s soooo satisfying. Great value!!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Perfect right out of the box. Set up was effortless. I love the simple and minimalist look and layout of it. I decided to swap my old HP wireless keyboard that I use for work out for this one and while it's a tad bit louder than I expected for snall office settings, I still love the sound and feel of it. It is definitely \"stockier\" than expected for plastic. Bit on the heavyside in comparison to a standard HP keyboard. Feels good and solid though. I've been using it all day and have no complaints so far.Small side note: if you're a dingus like me, the ÷ key on the number pad is the same as /. Took me a minute to figure that out because I'm so used to / + - * 🤣"
    },
    {
        "review": "First I want to say that I initially bought this for my husband as he had bought a walmart clicky brand keyboard and was finally growing tired of the clicky sound lol. Well as the keyboard enthusiast I suggested this one as it was simple and a few of the review videos had sounded promising.When it arrived the first thing I noticed was the heft, this was made with durable material and not light weight at all, which put some ease to my mind that it wasn't just made of cheap plastic. The size was just perfect, I couldn't be happier, and when I typed for the first time the creamy and thocky sound just itched the right place in my brain. I could not get enough of it.I love that there are 3 types of way that you can connect, BT; Dongle or wired. I always prefer wired.The keycaps are nice, although I prefer rounded keycaps to the sharp square ones, I did replace the factory keycaps and I just fell in love with this more. The switches sound nice and deep and honestly, this is almost perfect for me, I'll be playing around with this keyboard especially since it's hot swappable and I might try other switches.For those just starting out with interest in the keyboard area, this is a great first start with a not too expensive price point! I did end up having to buy another keyboard for my husband since I stole this one, it was definitely worth it though! Can't wait to mod this one a little more!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Coming up on almost a year of owning this keyboard. It was just what I needed. I love the feel of the keys, the sound, the 3 different options to connect, the weight and the number pad. The battery life wireless is okay. I wish there was a power button. Sometimes you accidentally touch a key after turning off the computer and the keyboard feels like it stays on forever lol. I use it for my WFH job and light gaming. I bought it last April and now it’s February and I’m starting to notice connection issues (non responsive) and sticky keys. It’s been acting a bit possessed lately. I work with a lot of VDIs so that could also play apart in the weirdness but it’s been more and more frequent lately. I use the Bluetooth option, I will try to hardwire to see if there is a difference. Will update my review if it poops out 🤔🤔"
    },
    {
        "review": "I’ve had this keyboard for almost 2 months now and wanted to wait to write a review until I had used it for a while. I’ve got to say this keyboard is awesome. recently got a WFH after only ever working in an office so I knew I wanted to invest in a nice keyboard. I spent at least a week researching and reading reviews on keyboards and didn’t realize how hard it would be to find nice keyboard with a num pad but I kept coming back to this one because of the amazing reviews and of course I love the way it sounds! For the average person yes this is a little bit on the pricier side but to me it was totally worth it, I like that it has 3 forms of connection (Wired, Bluetooth, and USB), there are a million different RGB options and it sounds amazing and it comes with super easy instructions and you can change the key caps if you want nicer keys. I will say, as you can see in the video I have two keys where the lighting under them doesn’t work/ change color, but I still have it 5 stars because it doesn’t affect the function of the key board. Another slight inconvenience is that you do have to charge it with the wire after a few days if you’re using Bluetooth or USB and it doesn’t give a low power warning but besides that it’s perfect. I also want to note that I have seen that this key board had gone a little viral on TikTok and I did purchase before it was trending so I would say if you are coming here from TikTok no, it’s not a scam it’s actually a great keyboard!"
    },
    {
        "review": "The Aula F99 is a \"96%\" mechanical keyboard that comes in around the $90 price point normally, but can get as low as $60 on sale. That form factor means that it does have a numpad and arrow keys as well as home, delete and page up/down keys, but those keys are moved up in-line with the function keys and the numpad/arrow keys are squished up closer to the main keys with the arrow keys set a little further down to make space. Having a numpad was a must for me because I like to play games that often use the numpad for important functions, and I also prefer typing numbers on a numpad when the occasion arises. At this price point, this is probably one of the best options for people who want a numpad and/or arrow keys but still want a keyboard that looks and sounds more like a custom keyboard you see in YouTube shorts of people doing sound tests.It has linear switches meaning there's no \"bump\" or clickiness to them, I'm not 100% sure what exact switches they are because there is some conflicting information online. I think mine has \"Star Vector\" switches? Regardless, the switches are quite light on force and very quiet, which brings us to the sound. This keyboard sounds great for the price. It's a little on the clacky side, but the switches have no scratchiness to them and are obviously pre-lubed, and there appears to be some kind of sound-deadening material inside the case as there is none of that hollow pinginess you get from something like a cheap Logitech mechanical. Overall it is a massive improvement from the Logitech keyboard I had previously to this one.The case is plastic but seems to be fairly high quality. The keyboard is surprisingly heavy which is very confidence inspiring and gives you a very stable feeling typing experience. It also has RGB lighting on all of the keys but the keycaps are not shine-through meaning the lettering doesn't light up. If that's a big deal to you, I'm sure you could swap them out for something else later as it does come with a keycap and switch puller, which also means you can change the switches if you want. It also has 3 different connection modes: \"Wired\" which uses a USB-C port on the board for connection (and it does come with a USB-C to A cable in the box), \"2.4 GHz\" which uses a little wireless dongle that sits in a little slot on the top edge of the board when not in use, and it's also able to connect via bluetooth. I haven't tested the battery life as I use this keyboard at my desktop computer with the wired connection, but having the different modes and a physical switch to change between them means you could have this board connected to multiple different devices and switch between them whenver you want which is cool and useful if you need that.I'm fairly new to the world of mechanical keyboards, this is only the second one I've ever owned, the first being a Logitech board that didn't sound or feel nearly as good as this one. As I type this review out on this very keyboard, I find myself liking the sound and feeling more and more. I'm sure there are things about it that someone with more knowledge and experience could pick apart and criticize, but overall I am very happy with this keyboard and would definitely recommend it to anyone who's not an expert and is looking for something better than the offerings from companies like Logitech and Razer."
    },
    {
        "review": "This keyboard is absolutely amazing! It has such a wonderful creamy clicking sound that isn't overly loud but still satisfying to use. Though I did instantly replace the keycaps with some very cute cats ones, they do feel very nice and have a wonderful texture. I love that you can adjust it to be in a more ergonomic position! Also the battery lasts for such a long time I am legit shocked every time it tells me I need to charge it lol. I use my computer and keyboard a lot as I am both a gamer and do all my work from my computer, so it gets a lot of use. And yet there has been no sticking, no issues, and is a lot nicer to use for longer period of times.Something else of note is that this keyboard was practically made for us who have small hands. My hands are very small and my last keyboard was sometimes annoying to use because I was unable to easily reach as many keys. This one is perfectly compact to where it does not feel crowded but also is super easy to use all while having the numkey pad! Also I love the customizations you can do with the keyboard colors and lighting!This was actually recommended to me by a friend. I went to her house and used it for a minute and was so stunned by how nice it felt I ordered this right there and then. I literally cannot recommend it enough!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Even though the order was placed on Sunday, I received it the same day. My first impressions of the keyboard are that it is really nice. He has a very soft voice. And the application works successfully. I recommend this product, you will definitely be satisfied with it."
    },
    {
        "review": "Incredible keyboard, with soft keys and pleasant sound, excellent construction, with good materials, very robust and heavy. I had keyboards like the razor blackwidow and it is superior. And the best part, it comes standard abnt2!"
    },
    {
        "review": "The best in class Keyboard I have ever used! The Typing Experience just feels awesome! Earlier, I was using Dell Wireless!Saw some Video on Social Media, and Man'O'Man! FELL IN LOVE WITH THIS KEYBOARD!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I have had this keyboard for a month now, and wow! It is by far the best keyboard in my collection. I love a nice creamy typing experience, and this more than delivers. I had been searching for a full keyboard that was versatile and easy to use. I bring it back and forth from work, and with my wedding planning, I've put it through it recently. There are 2 different heights that you can set the keyboard to for wrist support, and thanks to the larger keys, I can have a full set of nails and have 0 issues typing.My only complaint is that the light that indicates the battery is always red, even when fully charged. I'm not too sure why this happens, but it doesn't bother me.Great brand. Great quality. Beautiful design."
    },
    {
        "review": "Love the smooth creamy amazing typing experience. Yet to check bluetooth response. Wireless dongle works great with PC.  RGB is not shine through so not much use for backlight."
