[
    {
        "review": "I can’t recommend the kisnt KN85 enough. The RGB is bright (although the software isn’t great), It’s a good weight for a plastic keyboard, the colors it comes in are attractive, and the price is amazing. The most important part (to me) is the switches. They are Bsun purple switches, a smooth, creamy linear switch that sounds amazing. I genuinely use these in my other boards. It is made of plastic, but the plastic is sturdy. It is bluetooth, 2.4 ghz, and wired. Overall, this keyboard is worth it. I love this keyboard, i’ve bought two of them!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Great board especially at this price point. This really raises the bar for entry level products and makes it much more accessible. This thing will impress most seasoned enthusiast. I could see this thing attaining a similar status to the Aula F75.You get really good acoustics, great switches (lubed BSUN Purples), gasket mounting AND hotswap.I've been typing on it for a few days and the sound tests you see online are very accurate, board sounds very creamy and poppy, which makes for a very satisfying typing experience.The RGB on this board is really bright and has some cool preset animations.Definitely NOT office friendly, I took this thing to the office one day and had to swap it for a normal keyboard because it is quite loud. I enjoy the sound but I don't think my coworkers did. lolUnfortunately I did notice that the space bar seems to make a really loud thunk which doesn't match the sound of the rest of the board, but you can fix this pretty easily with a rubber O-Ring or some foam inside the space bar.And sadly I did not get any spare keycaps with my order, as I've seen other buyers receive in their reviews :(Overall though, really good board and significantly raises the bar for entry level products. This thing will outperform boards that cost twice the price. I am very happy with my purchase and will be buying one for my gf too"
    },
    {
        "review": "I'm shocked at the quality of this keyboard for the price. The main case is plastic but a is very good quality plastic. Obviously, it's not going to be aluminum at this price. The quality of the keycaps is also excellent. They are the more premium PBT plastic rather than the cheaper ABS plastic and they feel great. It even has more features than my other higher end mechanical keyboard as this one not only has wired USB (obviously), but also 2.4 Ghz dongle (which secures nicely in a little cutout of the bottom of the keyboard and held in magnetically), and a whooping 3 Bluetooth channels so you can pair this keyboard with many different devices at the same time.I love the layout as well. It's a little smaller than a TKL layout keyboard, but without any compromises. Typically, I don't like 75% or less because you lose too many useful keys or they have them in a weird place, but this keyboard's layout is perfectly done. You still have all of your Home, End, PgUp, PgDn, Insert, Delete, and Print Screen and Pause/Break keys. The only key missing is the Scroll Lock key which is never used anyway so this is perfect.It has nice RGB that lights up under and around the keys. Also comes with some extra switches and even a decent keycap and switch puller tool.  Even comes a thin, cheap, plastic dust cover which I appreciate.My only complaint whatsoever about this keyboard is that I'm not too crazy about the switches, but that's really a personal preference thing. They're certainly not terrible and I can definitely see other people with different preferences liking these, as actually a lot of other reviewers did really like the switches. But I'll probably be swapping them out, which is another great thing about this keyboard is that they are hot swappable.For those wondering about the sound - the stock keyboard is clacky rather than thocky."
    },
    {
        "review": "Chances are, you've found this keyboard after reading a number of Reddit threads or watching a review or 2 on Youtube, and I can confirm they're all very correct to recommend this keyboard.It's kind of obvious that it's not trying to be high-end, as evidenced by the all plastic build, basic and other creature-comforts that are left out, like volume knobs and little near-useless LED screens, but it is still a solid, well built keyboard with a decent-enough feature set.But, it's a keyboard, so if we're being real, its the tactile feel of typing on this thing along with it's really nice, satisfying \"creamy\" sound (at least that's what I've heard it called, though I still don't quite get why) that makes you want mechanical keyboard like this and this one does the thing real well while also looking pretty dang nice at the same time (I got the gray gradient version with side-printed keycaps. The battery life using the included USB 2.4G wireless dongle is exceptionally good and it also has RGB backlighting with 20 preprogrammed lighting modes, if you're into that thing, but I've got it set to a static color and it looks good enough for me.All of that at this price point makes this a really, really, really good option and one that I can absolutely recommend."
    },
    {
        "review": "It's a truly amazing keyboard, it feels great, looks great, and sounds great. I have only one issue with it, it's loud, but that's only an issue at night. The ergonomics feel pretty good, and the switches are very nice and responsive. Everything feels high quality, especially for this price. Very worth it and affordable.The backlight is a nice touch, it can be disabled and enabled, along with changing the color and speed.Note:\"Mom if you're seeing this, I wrote this review\" -your son."
    },
    {
        "review": "This keyboard has been an absolute dream, and the price cannot be beat. It uses bsun switches (I'm honestly not sure which, I've done some digging but can't find them), of which I got the linear version. These switches are nice, there is no noticeable scratch, a crisp bottom out, and the stabilizers are actually silent with no rattle or ticking. There is a variety of foam included, but they leave a bit of room in the case which keeps the board from sounding muted. There are NO SCREWS, so modding this thing is a breeze (might need a small flathead tho) and you have a lot of options straight out of the box. It also comes with a hotswap board, so you can lube the switches (already lubed, but do what you want) or change them out entirely. There are also FLIPPY FEET (2 STAGE!!) so you can adjust your typing angle. I have heard people complain about the software that comes with the board, but it turns out it is fully compatible with the Signal RGB app. Now I can assign any pattern I want, even outside of the presets! The lighting is very prominent since the LEDs are south-facing. 3 of the lights do not work in the software, but they are on the edge of the board with the modifier keys so they are not noticeable.My ONLY gripe is that the keycaps are not very durable. I have been using mine for a few months now and the tops of the WASD keys are starting to become shiny and smoother. These are likely not PBT, but they are a very thick ABS so the sound stays deeper and fuller. However, a PBT set from a name brand manufacturer costs more than this entire keyboard, so this is an incredibly minor complaint."
    },
    {
        "review": "This keyboard sounds even better than the reviews claim. Before buying, I came across a few comments suggesting that some of the internal padding had been removed or that the overall quality had declined as the keyboard became more popular. After using it myself, I can confidently say that those concerns were unfounded.The build quality is excellent, the typing experience is incredibly satisfying, and the switches feel fantastic. Every keypress sounds and feels premium, making it a joy to use for both work and gaming.So far, I’ve only tested the 2.4GHz wireless mode, so I can’t comment on the wired or Bluetooth performance. However, based on my experience, I have no hesitation recommending it.For the price, this keyboard offers outstanding value. It was absolutely worth the wait, especially considering I ordered it from the US and had it shipped to South Africa."
    },
    {
        "review": "Set up was a breeze, simply plug in. It charges in a few hours.This keyboard is really nice. I like the “creamy” sound other viewers have described. A VERY light touch is all you need to type. I won’t say it’s sensitive, it just requires a light touch of each key. That is something I need to get used to.I have a laptop set up on a riser, and needed a keyboard that will fit in the small space beneath it. This was my choice after reviewing MANY keyboards. I also wanted a laptop with raised keys, a mechanical keyboard. I like the “old school” feel of it.Weight: it’s heavy. I won’t be traveling with it, so that doesn’t matter to me. But I love the heaviness of it. Feels secure in its spot.Ergonomics: there are two levels if you want to change the angle. Three is you count not using the little feet at the back. I also have a padded hand/wrist rest for extra comfort (NOT INCLUDED) but the keyboard itself is comfortable to use.Color: I would have preferred black to match my desk accessories and laptop, or blue my favorite color. I went for the least expensive option as the color doesn’t change the effectiveness of the device.Lights: it’s advertised as a gaming keyboard. I’m not a gamer, nor will I ever be. You CAN turn off the lights. Simply read the paperwork that comes in the package to know how."
    },
    {
        "review": "The keyboard exceeded my expectations in the essentials: very pleasant “thock” sound, firm keystrokes and three connection modes that work without problem. The construction feels solid; The plastic is thick and clean to the touch, of better quality than in other models in its range. Points in favor: Excellent feel and linear response. Bass-creamy sound from the factory, without the need for mods. Very good value for the price: it is close to “custom” performance of higher ranges. Points for improvement: The administration software was useless on two PCs. The page is confusing, and although the keyboard was recognized, it did not offer real configuration options. The back uses a simpler plastic, and the non-slip rubbers are glued with double-sided tape that began to peel off from the first day. I am worried that they will be lost or will need to be replaced soon. Does not include a numpad: ideal for gaming and writing, but can be limiting for those who use numeric input a lot. Conclusion: An excellent gateway to the “custom” world for its sound, touch and connectivity, as long as you do not depend on the software or need a full keyboard. Separate note (does not influence the product rating): tracking took four days to update; The package arrived without damage, but the logistics process, communication and professionalism on the part of the seller can improve."
    },
    {
        "review": "Great Product"
    },
    {
        "review": "Honestly, I am absolutely blown away with the quality of this kisnt KN85, it's hands down the best keyboard I have ever owned. First of all, the sound quality of the key click is insane, this thing is so thocky with the bsun switches, PBT double shot key caps, gasket mounts and multiple layers of dampening foam/silicone, genuinely such a pleasure to type with. It is even hot-swappable if you want to change or upgrade your switches. The battery life is amazing, first use never charged out of the box lasted over a full week of heavy daily use.I find it very hard to find any fault with this keyboard whatsoever. One note is that the LED's are south-facing, so shine-through backlit keycaps won't work, I like double-shot caps for the thock, so this doesn't bother me one bit. But seriously, I have never been so happy to be typing at my desk. It has changed my whole experience. I watch reviews of £200-£500 keyboards, and I still think this is better.All I will say is get it fast, as prices are constantly rising due to demand."
    },
    {
        "review": "looks and feels amazing, setup was easy and battery is lasting along time which is great.  but the sound of the keyboard is so creamy, it really does sound like a premium keyboard. I also love the beige color scheme its very clean yet retro and the letters are bolded and of course its not a lousy 60% so you can enjoy all the key functions without having to press FN all the time"
    },
    {
        "review": "Awesome keyboard that punches above its weight. Stock switches are louder than I'd like, but hey it's a custom keyboard, so just swap them out easily for anything you like. For the price it's great keyboard. ONLY major issue is I like separate numpad controllers (I put these on the right of my mouse so it's quicker) but Kisnt only make these seperate numpads for the two other colour schemes that exist. The beige and green scheme is missing."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "I've only had this keyboard for a few days, but so far I'm very happy with it. One of the main reasons I chose it was the color scheme, light blue, navy blue, and white, which matches my home office perfectly. The RGB lighting is a nice bonus, and I especially love that I was able to set it to blue to complement my workspace.The keyboard connected without any issues and has been very responsive while typing. The keys were a little clickier than I prefer out of the box. While they weren't excessively loud, I wanted a softer typing experience, so I switched out the switches. Now the keyboard is much quieter (soft touch), which is exactly what I was looking for.Another thing I noticed right away is the weight. This keyboard feels solid and substantial, and it stays in place on my desk while typing. I got the unit with 108 keys because I type often and needed a full-size keyboard. As for the price, it was reasonable for the color options because I've seen some keyboards that were over $199 in basic black.I'm usually a Logitech customer, and my last Logitech keyboard lasted six years, so I was hesitant to try a different brand. However, this keyboard had the colors I wanted and seemed to come from a reputable company. So far, it has exceeded my expectations. If it holds up as well as my Logitech did, I'll be extremely pleased.Based on my experience so far, I would definitely recommend it."
    },
    {
        "review": "aside from the cable being dinky it is a very nice keyboard. love the feel of the keys and ease of use. wish it had media controls but it is worth losing for this form factor. lighting is pretty cool and i see myself leaving it on default."
    },
    {
        "review": "Disclaimer: I'm not a gamer! I wanted a durable keyboard for work that had a backlit option, swappable keys, and a fairly quiet profile. I tried the Redragon K668 first, but its clicking sounds were too pronounced for my shared workspace. This one still has clicking noises, but the layers of sound dampening make a noticeable difference. I love the lighting options and that I can easily modify them, and the key feel is smooth and very responsive. If you're looking for a quieter-but-not-silent keyboard, I recommend this one!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Best part about the Redragon keyboard is the rather silent typing...no one likes loud/clicking keys when you're using it in a living room where others are present. The lighting effects are nice...defaults to a pulsating RGB type of lighting but is configurable. Has several programmable keys which is a bonus. The biggest problem I've had with these keyboards is the paint wearing off after months of use. We'll see how it holds up long-term...I'll be back to review longevity once I've used the keyboard for several months. Great buy overall for silence of keys, comfort, and feel. Would buy another."
    },
    {
        "review": "I am extremely happy with this purchase. The product worked exactly as expected and exceeded my expectations in both quality and performance. It was easy to use, well-made, and delivered excellent results. Everything arrived in great condition, and it has performed flawlessly since I started using it. I would definitely recommend this product to others and would not hesitate to purchase it again"
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a very good full keyboard, with inverted T arrow navigation keys, numeric keypad, home/end/pgup/pgdown/delete/insert grouping, and 12 function keys. It's also backlit with a good variety of lighting modes. So far I'm really happy with it. Could it be better? Sure; it lacks a wrist rest, ergonomic layout like a Microsoft Natural, and doesn't have dedicated media control keys (although this last point isn't a big deal for me since this keyboard is for use with a computer that won't be used for multimedia).However, keyboards are a very personal thing and a big deal these days, so here are some more details to help you understand this keyboard's features.Weight: It's not as heavy as the dedicated gaming mechanical keyboards that seem popular now with gamers and programmers. But it's not flimsy either. Light enough to not be much hassle if you're going to be traveling with it.Key feel: This keyboard is not one of the loud clicky ones. It does feel more tactile than a typical membrane keyboard, and the keys have a nice travel when you press them, but the click sound is very soft. These are regular-style keys, not chicklet keys.Lighting: I wasn't too worried about have top RGB features, but sometimes if it's darker in the room I do like some backlighting. This keyboard is a nice compromise between keyboards that have only white backlight, and keyboards that have a full software control app that let you customize everything. There is no software with this keyboard, it has several modes all configured using the function keys. Here are the lighting modes:- Lighting off/on- Neon mode: multiple colors light up three static zones on the keyboard. There are a few combinations you can select. The colors stay the same in the zones and don't change.- Spectrum mode: 7 colors rotate and fill the entire keyboard continuously. So it'll be lit red, then fade into purple, then blue, then green, etc.- Static mode: You cycle through and pick the color you want of the 7 supported colors, and it will stay lit continuously with just that one color.There are brightness and breathing effects that you can configure for each of the modes.I only use static mode, but gamers might like the other ones. I saw more expensive boards that only did spectrum or only had one color.Key visibility: The keys are not painted so they're a little hard to read in poor lighting if you don't use the backlight. However, I did like that the symbol keys had the correct placement for lower/upper case characters. I have seen many expensive keyboards from Razer, Corsair, Alienware, etc., that often invert the upper/lower case labeling for symbol keys and number keys. If you're an experienced typist, you'll work around that, but it bugs me when I see it. I found out later that this is an intentional design decision so that the more commonly used lower case symbol is more visible when backlit (the lighting doesn't cover the entire key and ends up emphasizing whatever character is in the upper case position). But you won't have to worry about that with the ReDragon.Key layout: After looking at the huge variety of keyboards on Amazon, you'll notice that some smaller keyboards don't have the numeric keypad or the six-key navigation group. Or that some have that ANSI style keyboard with the bigger Enter key but then put the slash key somewhere else. Or some that have compressed distance between keys or even smaller keys to make it more compact.This keyboard has an ISO keyboard layout with 104 full-sized keys. The key spacing is about a quarter-inch, which is similar to other full-size keyboards I've used.For the price, I think this is a great peripheral. Longevity would be my only concern, and I'll update this review later if I run across any issues."
    },
    {
        "review": "I love the font of the letters on the keys.  You pretty mush HAVE to have the lights on to see the lettering at all but it is very plain and easy when the lights are on.  Keys feel nice, somewhere between a reed style key and an full mechanical.  not nearly as loud as full mechanical keys though."
    },
    {
        "review": "When I purchased the first Redragon K509 keyboard, I also purchased a Redragon S107 keyboard and mouse combo at the same time.  The keyboard in that S107 combo looks identical to this K509 keyboard.  It even has the exact same instructions, looks identical, and behaves exactly the same.  This K509 keyboard appeared to be a good value.  I felt the quality was good for the price.  The LED's were not fixed colors (which is good), and the LED's could be set in many colors, in \"breathing mode\" and constant brightness.  However, when set to constant brightness, the lights dim a significant amount.  I could live with that.I would give this K509 model a fairly high rating, but after 6 weeks, the first keyboard stopped working.  Windows would no longer recognize the device.  I tried plugging it into different USB ports, but that made no difference.  I tried using the keyboard on 2 other PC's, and they had the same trouble recognizing the device.  I figured I just had bad luck, and ordered a second, exact same keyboard.  After 5 weeks, this second keyboard started having the exact same trouble.  Windows would no longer recognize the device.  It couldn't even tell it was a keyboard.  Again, I tried this keyboard different USB ports and on two other PC's.  It would not work no matter which port or PC I plugged it into.  Other devices, including other keyboards, work on each of the USB ports I tried on the 3 different PC's.  I also have a Redragon S107 Keyboard that is part of the Redragon S107 Keyboard/Mouse combo.  That keyboard still works great, and looks and behaves identical to the K509, except for the label underneath the keyboard.  The S107 keyboard (not this KB) works just fine on all 3 PC's, and I still use it without trouble, to this day. I have contacted the manufacturer and attempted to get support for this product, multiple times, and they said that it was a problem with the USB port, and to plug into another port.  I had tried this, as well as trying the keyboard on multiple USB ports on two other PC's.  The manufacturer would not admit that the problem was with the keyboard.  I was given instructions how to remove the unrecognized device in the Windows \"Device Manager\", and let windows recognize the device again.  This had no effect.  After the problems began, each time I plug in the keyboard, it would light up for a few seconds, the PC would make the typical \"USB device attached\" sound, then the light would turn off. Two or three seconds later, it would light up, PC makes the sound again, and lights turn off.  It would repeat this for various number of times, before the keyboard would eventually have the lights stay ON, and look as if it was working, but none of the keys would respond.  When I check Windows 10 \"Device Manager\", I would see the keyboard listed as \"Unknown USB Device (Device Descriptor Request Failed)\"  The Device Status read as, \"Windows has stopped this device because it has reported problems. (Code 43) A request for the USB device descriptor failed.\"  I like this keyboard.  I would purchase another, but I worry that there may be a design or manufacturing flaw, causing an increased chance of the device failing to be recognized by the PC.  I have only tried two of these keyboard, so I cannot be sure.  But 0 out of 2 is not a record that would inspire the confidence for me to recommend this keyboard to anyone.I get this exact same behavior and error on all 3 PC's... an HP desktop, a Dell/Alienware desktop, and a Microsoft Surface Pro 4 Tablet PC.  My Redragon S107 Keyboard and mouse combo continues to work just fine.  I have an older USB keyboard being used instead of the two Redragon K509 keyboards.  Neither K509 keyboard was damaged, dropped, or misused in any way.  They get plugged in, and remain in a fixed position from that point forward.  No food or drink is consumed near them, because they are located around other sensitive equipment.I should have attempted a product return on Amazon, but it was already outside the window of time I could return or exchange the product.  That is on me.  However, the manufacturer still would not admit that the keyboard was defective in any way, in spite of the obvious evidence to the contrary."
    },
    {
        "review": "I liked the responsive feel, I've been using it for some time and it holds up well, without peeling off and turning on all the keys, for the price I think it offers good value.  It has several lighting options, you can deactivate the light among others."
    },
    {
        "review": "I bought this keyboard for work to use in the office. I already have a Ducky One 2 Mini at home for gaming and wanted something similar but on a budget for working as I am a developer and prefer typing on a mechanical keyboard than anything else.I read the reviews and didn't doubt the quality. Receiving it, it also didn't fail me on that.I received it today, ordered it last night so it came quick.I ordered the red switches, which I think will take some time to get used to as I'm used to Cherry MX Browns but thats not a problem. When I received the keyboard, the packaging was solid. It reminded me of when I bought my Ducky One 2 Mini, the box is high quality and it's amazing for the price. The one thing I noticed was how heavy the box was, which said to me that this keyboard would be solid. The box was also sealed which I appreciated. The keyboard in the box was wrapped in bubble wrap which was hella thoughtful. For a $50 keyboard, there's alot of work put into it and I totally appreciate that especially for those who are just starting out using mechanical keyboards, this is a great starter.The keyboard itself is hella solid. It was alot heavier than I thought it would be but i Love that because that means the keyboard won't move easily. It's a thick base, and is amazing for the price. It's very comfortable to use too. It was also a bit bigger than I thought it would be but I think I just got used to my Ducky One 2 Mini too long lol.Theres even little adjusters on the back of the board to raise it if flat doesnt work for somebody (like me). I bought this keyboard for one of those reasons. Also, the box came with a instruction mini booklet which is nice because nowadays with budget buys, you dont get those kinds of things that explain how the product works. Also, it came with a red dragon sticker which i was completely stoked about. It also came with a keycap remover, I already have one but an extra one will be handy.Theres 9 backlight options and they're all sick. super easy to change too. I'm just a steady RGB kinda gal so I didn't really care about the options too much but if you do, the options are great. cool part I love about this keyboard too is the arrow keys. My Ducky One 2 Mini doesn't have that and I don't really care but for developing, those can come in handy, so i'm stoked. Red switches are relatively quiet, it's not super loud but it's got a nice click to it.I'll update for long term use at some point, but I dont think i'll be disappointed.Overall, do I recommend this? Yeah. 100%. For the price, its a hella good deal. Great budget buy, great starter mechanical keyboard, and wicked solid itself."
    },
    {
        "review": "@i.m_deep33 :The Redragon Dyaus K509 is an excellent choice for gamers seeking the perfect balance between affordability and performance. Despite being a membrane keyboard, it offers a **semi-mechanical feel**, providing tactile feedback and a satisfying keystroke that closely mimics the feel of mechanical switches. This makes it ideal for both gaming and typing, delivering a responsive and comfortable experience.One of its standout features is the **vibrant RGB lighting**, with numerous color modes and dynamic effects that add flair to any setup. The low-profile, ergonomic keys ensure minimal fatigue during extended sessions, while the build quality is impressive for the price. If you're looking for a budget-friendly keyboard with a **mechanical-like typing experience**, customizable RGB, and reliable performance, the K509 is one of the best options available."
    },
    {
        "review": "What a keyboard,for such a price keys are smooth , and the lights are so nice and bright.The keyboard is very responsive and has a large compatability."
    },
    {
        "review": "Parfait"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "AULA WIN68 HE is my first ever HE keyboard, after years of being in the world of mechanical keyboards and exclusively favoring the brown switch (tactile) I always watched many videos and people praising HE keyboards and the edge they give to you in gaming primarily. We all know of the famed Wooting 60 HE which started this trend in gaming world both the price and being only 60% give's it only a small and competitive market for a few individuals that can afford it or need it for professional e-Sports. As primarily a single-player gamer I was blow away with how much HE switches changes your experience of playing and gives you for the first time ever something that gamepads had over keyboards, and that is you don't need to push the key all the way for it work, both only a slight push fulfils the given command that you want (MMOs and racing games benefit from this the most). The web software is one of the best features of this keyboard and it allows you to customize every single key however you want it, every key supports rapid trigger as well as customizing the dead zone as well as primarily the sensitivity of the keys. The standard version that I have allows you to customize the sensitivity from 0.8mm to larger if you prefer (default being 1.70mm, while mechanical boards clock at 2.1mm). Keyboard is hot swappable and you can change your magnetic switches really easily. Brightness and RGB is also good with no complaints in that department (you can customize it in the web software). The build quality is also good and think that board was upgraded to PBT switches since when typing on them the feel sandy and rough, sound quality is quite since magnetic switches are linear switches (both better in every way), and the keyboard is wired only which I prefer since I am using it on my personal PC and have no need to use it elsewhere. HE keyboards are definitely the future and every professional or enthusiast e-Sport gamer simply must have this keyboard because it gives them and edge in every way that mechanical keyboard simply cannot give at the moment! I recommended this board for anybody who is on the fence over coming to the HE world."
    },
