[
    {
        "review": "This is certainly a bargain priced mechanical keyboard but that doesn't mean it's bad; in fact, it's excellent.  I got the model with silent red switches.  I already own a Cherry silent red without the number pad but I wanted a full size.  This one is ever so slightly less silent, likely due to an aluminum frame (a plus) rather than plastic.  But it is still quiet enough to use in the bedroom at any hour.It is wired only.  I actually prefer a wired connection rather than fooling with Bluetooth or dongles.  To save space there is no plastic \"frame\" around the keys but this is just a matter of looks and you may prefer it, as the aluminum base is very attractive.It did take a little positioning to reach a stable state but that may have been my desk as much as the keyboard.  There are tabs to raise the back but  I don't use them.The keys are clearly marked and have a good feel to them.  Let's see if with use they take on a shine; it's too soon to tell.This is a keyboard I will use a lot as I do most of my work on my home desktop setup.  So far the typing experience has been exactly what I expect from this type of switch; there is enough of a tactile feel to be reliable but of course it's limited by the damped switches.  (I do love the positive solid feedback of the very loud, clicky blue switches but my home office is in the bedroom and that doesn't work out.)The only thing I can't speak to is the software for remapping the keys.  This can be downloaded from the Cherry site.  But it doesn't run on my Linux system so I can't make use of it.  However I really see no need to remap any of the keys, and if I really need to do that, there are other ways.I definitely rate this keyboard at 5 stars.  It is great value for the money and gets you into the wonderful world of mechanical keyboards without breaking the bank.  I have had great luck with Cherry products and this one is another winner."
    },
    {
        "review": "If you thought other companies made good key switches, then you are in for a surprise with this keyboard. Cherry absolutely nailed it with these new MX2A switches. I purchased the version with the Cherry MX2A Silent Red switches. Now, I basically hate linear switches. Really, I usually can't stand them, but these new silents reds are the smoothest, and most buttery feeling switches I have ever felt. I have been using mechanical keyboards my entire life. Since before the patent ran out on the Cherry MX switches and the only mechanical keyboards you could buy had Cherry switches. Now, everyone makes their own switches, with all kinds of \"features\" like hall effect, rapid trigger, analog, adjustable actuation, magnets, etc.. Here's the issue with all of those though, they feel like crap to type on and are super loud. I have spent thousands on keyboards over the years. I still have over 15 mechanical keyboards sitting in storage that I stopped using. I am not exaggerating either. To give you an idea of where I'm coming from, my favorite switches used to be Cherry MX Brown. Now they are Cherry MX2A Silent Reds. I haven't even mentioned how quiet they are too. Unbelievably quiet for a mechanical keyboard. It's definitely the quietest mech keyboard I have ever used, which is saying a lot. Some people may think the keyboard is fairly basic, and in all reality, it is. It doesn't require software, doesn't have RGB or any lights (besides the led for caps lock and such), it doesn't have a removeable cable, or a super high polling rate over 1000Hz. Visually and functionally speaking, the keyboard is simple. But that a huge positive in my book. It does have full N-key rollover, so if you do use it for gaming, there won't be any issues there. I don't need to install any bloat to use the keyboard. I don't need to configure anything. It's just plug and play. It's fairly compact for a full-sized keyboard too, so be aware of the slightly more compact layout. My only real complaint is that it does not have 4 rubber feet on the bottom to keep it from sliding around. There are 2 rubber feet on the bottom front, but on the bottom rear, the feet are hard plastic, so it doesn't stay in place super well. There are rubber pads on the bottom rear feet that fold out, but I don't use a keyboard angled, so they are useless for me. Other than that, one thing, this keyboard is perfect for me. Am I going to use it for my hardcore gaming sessions? No. I actually purchased it for office work, and it's going to be perfect for that. However, will I be purchasing another keyboard with these same exact switches for my gaming pc? 100% YES! Look, you may not like the design of this keyboard, or its simplicity, but do not sleep on these new Cherry switches. Cherry is back with a vengeance, and if you're looking for a new keyboard, get one with these new Cherry switches. Whether it be this keyboard or not. The switch is king, and Cherry is back on top.Update: I now own 3 of these keyboards, lol, all with the Cherry MX2A Silent Red switches. One for the gaming pc, one for the work office, and one for the home office. They can't be beaten when it comes to typing quality. No regrets."
    },
    {
        "review": "I got this keyboard 3 weeks ago and I am very impressed. I used this at work and it suits my needs perfectly. It is extremely elegant and the keys are so satisfying to press. It feels very solid and hopefully should last a very long time.I share an office with one other person and the silent red switches are perfect for my environment. I would definitely recommend this keyboard to anyone who wants a simple but effective office keyboard."
    },
    {
        "review": "Great productivity keyboard. Satisfying “thunk” on usage with fast throw. The switches being reds are easy so it doesn’t take much effort and it’s a good mechanical keyboard if you are coming from a cheaper style membrane or scissor style keyboard but want to venture into the world of mechanical keyboards. Not perfect but pretty good especially for the money."
    },
    {
        "review": "I have always had mechanical keyboards but they are loud and are not appreciated by coworkers in nearby offices.  This keyboard solves the problem and sacrifices nothing to do so.  It turned out to be quieter than I thought it would be and feels great for all day typing!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Has held up extremely well! My typing speed has held up through a fairly intensive use for this while using Discord fairly heavily, while writing 3+ hours per day and while gaming on fairly heavily (Helldivers 2, Deadlock, Counter-Strike). The keys are a little heavy but in a way that seems well-built rather than is exhausting to type with. It's very nice to type with and gives good feedback. The keys spring back in a very responsive manner and provide a very good typing experience. The layout and feeling of typing with it overall has been really nice, no complaints of things like function keys. The dedicated calculator function key is unexpectedly more helpful than I had given it credit for and all function keys work with Linux."
    },
    {
        "review": "Almost perfect, but the function key row is terribly integrated. It's too close to the number row. Same issue for the arrow keys and number pad. They're all too close together and I hit adjacent keys. Since every other keyboard in the world has separation, due to muscle memory I find myself hitting the function keys when reaching for the number row. I have giant hands though, so may be less of a nuisance for normal-handed folks. They should have made the keyboard a quarter inch taller and half inch wider and fixed this."
    },
    {
        "review": "When I started looking for a keyboard all I wanted was easy push keys and this one fits the bill. I love the raised keys and the buttons are very smooth and easy to press. I work at a computer all day and love this keyboard. It has significantly eased my finger fatigue. Would recommend 👌 Also the keys are very quiet. I have had this keyboard for several months now and its still like new."
    },
    {
        "review": "I was using an Ornata v3 before this, which is pretty close in price. I wanted something I could use for both work and gaming. Here's a comparison that might help if you're looking for where this excels against the Ornata and similar competitors:No lighting: I'm tired of everything having freaking lights on them! I could just disable it in the settings I guess, but then I'm just paying for a feature I'm never using. Only keys with a light are Capslock and Numlock to let you know they're on.Keys: Good tactile feel and not too loud. Just enough of each to make you feel like you're typing. I use it for both gaming and work and it handles both jobs well. Very responsive for gaming. Some might consider it a little loud if you're in a cubicle with someone and you have to type a lot, but that's what headphones are for I guess! You won't find a more quiet mechanical keyboard that stll has that tactile typing feel, so you'll have to choose which you value more. (feel > sound for me)Size: A big more compact that some keyboards, but that's a plus. No lost real estate around the edges, just well-spaced keys and easy typing. You don't feel cramped at all if you're a touch typer. It has some heft to it. I often sit with it on my lap and it's fine. Not bouncing around like leaf, but doesn't cut off circulation to your legs.Durability: Again, it has some heft to it. When I pick it up I have a habit of snagging a key and pulling the cap off. I actually broke a key or two on my Ornata because the cap connectors are flimsy plastic posts and trying to find replacements was a nightmare. These keys are well-made and durable; haven't lost one yet.Only negative to me was the Calculator(???) button in the top-right. When I have it on my lap I was accidentally pressing it against the edge of my desk a lot and opening calculator app(s) mid-game; very frustrating. It was a simple job to go into windows settings and remove the mapping to the key (google for instructions) and now it's not an issue for a recliner like me. Anyone having it sit on their desk will likely never have this issue.I was a little leery of the price point when purchasing, but I'm seeing the value in the design, layout and build quality. I don't regret my purchase at all.  In the last couple years I've gone through Razer, Steel Series and Logitech keyboards all around the $100 price point and this is by far my favourite. Highly recommend it."
    },
    {
        "review": "perfect for silent work environments, very nice to the touch"
    },
    {
        "review": "Extremely happy with product"
    },
    {
        "review": "Excellent keyboard, works very well for gaming.The problem is the price: when I bought mine for $101 CAN, they were decent value for the money.Now a year later they want $216 CAN for them, and...that's a little absurd."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "My first mechanical keyboard and sounds exactly how I imagined! It’s nice solid , creamy click noise! So satisfying!!!I loved the key color combo!  The RGB lights are customizable, down to each key! Super cool. I am overall impressed with my purchase, as I was hesitant to buy something I wouldn’t like the sound of. I was also hesitant because there are so many options but I am glad I picked this! Good value for your money. The keyboard is heavy and feel super sturdy, doesn’t feel cheap. The keys were very responsive to touch and the Bluetooth was easy to setup with my windows laptop.The only thing I don’t like is the look of the keys, they do have a sheen/texture to them I don’t like but honestly, overall, I don’t mind it at all!Great buy!"
    },
    {
        "review": "This is my first mechanical keyboard, and I finally understand what all the hype is about.The sound is incredibly satisfying. It has that nice mechanical sound without being overly loud or obnoxious, but it also isn't so quiet that you lose the satisfying typing experience. The typing feel is probably my favorite part—the keys feel incredibly smooth and satisfying, and so far everything has felt flawless.The build quality is also really impressive for the price. It has some nice weight to it and doesn't feel cheap or flimsy at all.The smart display is a fun addition. Is it necessary? Absolutely not. 😂 But it's definitely nice to have. You can use it for things like Bluetooth connection and volume settings, and you can also customize it with your own photo or GIF.The one area that could definitely be improved is the software. It's not particularly user-friendly, and finding the correct software to customize the keyboard took me longer than it should have. Once I actually found it, though, it wasn't too difficult to figure out with a little patience. The software itself also feels a little chunky and dated. That said, this has zero effect on the actual performance of the keyboard, so it's a very minor complaint.I've had absolutely no issues with wireless connectivity. I've used both Bluetooth and the included cable and haven't experienced any disconnections, lag, or other problems. One thing to note is that you'll need to use the included cable when connecting the keyboard to the software for customization.The battery life has also been surprisingly good so far. I haven't actually had to charge it yet, so I can't speak to the full battery life, but I work from home about 9 hours a day and also casually game in the evenings. After two days of that, the battery has only dropped about 5%.Overall, I have very little negative to say about this keyboard. The software could definitely use some improvement, and there could be better information provided about where to find and how to use it. But that's a very small complaint compared to everything else this keyboard does well.For my first mechanical keyboard, I'm extremely happy with it. Everything about it has exceeded my expectations. 5/5 and definitely recommend!"
    },
    {
        "review": "tl;dr: I am EXTREMELY happy with this keyboard. Build? Beautiful. Sound? Amazing. Functionality? PERFECT. Price? FANTASTIC. If this keyboard fits your specifications for your desk and you happen to work with two different computers at the same time, get it ASAP.More details:Previously I had a significantly smaller RK 68 which was great for me. It was perfect for what I wanted, a small profile and clacky keyboard. Unfortunately, I spilled soda all over it and it died so I had to get a new one. Fortunately, it was this keyboard.In my search for a new keyboard I had a couple things I was looking for:- A green color way to match my set up- A nice clack to it.- A numpad attached.Clearly it's not something very specific. But during my search I came across other features that I had no idea I wanted. But this one hit pretty much what I was looking for so I decided to give it a try and boy did it blow my expectations out of the water.== Design ==Pros:This keyboard looks sleek. Before, to build something like this I had considered paying my friend to build it from scratch but the colorway and the key placements are just so clean. With multifunctional keycaps, they've added the second function on the side of the keycap so you can easily see the primary and the secondary without it feeling too cluttered.The back lighting options really make it fun to customize. They give you default options you can run with and each of those options you can tweak to your liking in terms of color.The digital display seems more like a part of the design the way it links to the volume knob, rather than an after thought like some other keyboards. This provides another option for you to make the keyboard your own by letting you upload a gif to just loop by default.Cons:No complaints really, except the digital display occasionally pops off slightly, requiring you to press it back in but it hasn't become too much of an issue for me yet. Also, uploading the gif to the keyboard takes a good amount of time (something like 5 minutes) , so if they could fix that speed that would be amazing.Oh one more complaint is that it's too tall. I had to get a wrist mount just to feel comfortable typing on it.== Functionality ==Pros:MAN, this keyboard is PACKED with features I never thought I would need. Aside from the aforementioned backlight customization, something that has made my life so much better is hidden behind the digital display. It's base functionality of controlling the volume of your computer works like a charm but it's when you get into the submenus that you can really see where it shines.You see, I am someone who bounces between a macbook pro for work and a windows PC for play and so I'm constantly bouncing between the two. Before, I had a mac specific keyboard and a windows specific keyboard, both occupying space on my desk. This keyboard has eliminated that need in a sleek and elegant way by allowing me to hardwire into my desktop and bluetooth to my macbook. From there, I can easily use the digital display + volume knob to switch my connection between the two. Heck, if I needed to add another couple I could. It seems like I can have up to 5 different devices connected (3 bluetooth, 1 wired, 1 wireless dongle). So if for some reason I had another device, I could easily switch between the two.You might ask yourself, but what about the difference in layout between macs and windows machines? Oh my friend, you see... THEY THOUGHT OF EVERYTHING. Through the same digital display, you can swap the layouts between the two types. I was totally fine with that until I read the manual a bit more and found you could even use a hotkey JUST for that. Remember the secondary functions of the various keys? If you look on the side of the 'A' and 'S' keys, you can see that they read 'WIN' and 'MAC' respectively. So to switch formats, all you have to do is hit 'Fn' + 'A' or 'S' to switch between the two. This really pushed my like for this keyboard to love.Cons:The only thing I can really think of is a nitpick and is possibly even already a function, I'd just have to read through the manual a bit more. It would be nice to be able to use a shortcut to bounce between the different keyboard connections like you can switch formats.== Sound and Feel ==Pros:I am having a BLAST typing this review. Every keypress feels so satisfying as the switches engage and disengage making you really feel connected to the keyboard. The sound is more thocky than clacky which is extremely pleasing to my ears, especially after coming from a clackier keyboard.Cons:While this keyboard is probably 50 percent quieter than my last keyboard, it's still definitely louder than a membrane keyboard, so if you're looking for silent, this isn't what you want. But if you're looking at mechanical keyboards, then this most likely isn't you.== Price ==I feel like this keyboard is well worth the 89 dollar price tag for what you get. I feel like even a couple years ago, you'd have to pay 300 bucks to someone to custom make something like this with the design options. Sure, it's not as bespoke as the true customs, but I feel like it holds up just as well.Overall, I'd give this keyboard a 'have no fear, buy away' if your needs are similar to what I've mentioned above!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I recently got into keyboard building and I chose this keyboard for my first build. I love that it’s basically full size but has a compact feel. I choose the sky blue option that came with the pale green tactile switches. I enjoy the feel of a tactile switch, but the switches that came stock were a little too high pitch for me. I swapped out the switches for some hand lubed Leobog Greywood v3s (sound test in video). The keyboard has a surprising amount of weight to it, and the knob and screen are very useful.Pros:South facing RGBSounds amazing (imo) after I swapped the switches and put new keycaps onThe LED screen and the knob are extremely handy and a really cool featureBattery works REALLY well - when the RGB is offTri-mode feature - I personally have some issues with the Bluetooth with one specific computer of mine (they won’t connect unless the keyboard is on and in the right mode before I turn my computer on) but I don’t have this issue with any of my other devices and believe it’s likely due to an old Bluetooth adapter.Cons:Battery - I did a full test of this and the battery with RGB on is honestly terrible in my opinion. I started my workday with a fully charged battery, and it had drained to 28 percent after 10 hoursSoftware - Can’t seem to be able to change the clock from 24hr format to 12hr formatSoftware for the keyboard itself is definitely lacking, and there were little resources I could find on how to use itAll that being said I don’t regret buying this keyboard at all. It’s a major upgrade from what I had before, and I’m extremely proud of my first build."
    },
    {
        "review": "First mechanical keyboard I’ve owned, can’t imagine I’d need anything different. Bought two of these, one for my workspace and one for my wife. We both needed accurate and responsive keys for frequent data and password entry, the lights and display are just bonus points. I change out my display for random gifs and RGB weekly using the web portal, easy enough once you get the hang of it, takes 5-10 minutes to upload a gif. Can confirm battery lasts about a week with moderate workday use. Keys are on the quieter end of clacky, not as obnoxious as some mechanical keyboards I’ve used in the past. Key travel registers about 75% from fully depressed which took me a tiny bit of time to adapt to. More often now, I’ll think I missed a letter because I didn’t press quite hard enough, but look back to find it registered after all. Comes with a few replacement switches, key puller, and usb-c cable which is nice. I’m sure there is better quality for more money out there but I’m not the demographic for it, this is all the keyboard I think I’ll ever need."
    },
    {
        "review": "When I first got a gaming PC, I figured I didn't really need a super expensive keyboard and that a cheap one that sounded \"clicky\" and had lights was fine enough. I also didn't want to bother with charging a wireless keyboard. However, I started to hear from YouTube videos and TikToks about \"creamy\" and \"thocky\" keyboards. I immediately started to do research, because I was obsessed with the sound. This is one of those rare purchases that I cannot stop thinking about how happy it makes me. I have absolutely no qualms with it. Here's what I love:The sound is excellent. Videos that I watched were a good indicator, but as other reviews have said, they really do not do the keyboard justice for how it actually sounds. The letters themselves are very creamy, while the function buttons (spacebar, shift, enter, backspace, etc.) have a much deeper \"thocky\" sound to them. If you're on the fence, at least try it and return if it's not for you.The functionality is incredible. There are a ton of lighting options that you can play with. You can mess around with them in the software on your PC. But because this keyboard has a small LCD screen, you can also change them directly on the keyboard itself. I have mine set to light up the entire keyboard cyan from whichever key I press, expanding out.Speaking of the LCD screen, you're able to upload GIFS or images to the screen, which is a really cool feature.As far as connectivity, you have every imaginable option available to you. I have my gaming rig connected via the 2.4USB wireless adapter. You can also connect up to three devices via bluetooth. And of course you can opt to hardware in.I love the the keyboard has both a USB-A and USB-C port. I am a huge advocate for technology having USB-C as an option, as it is much more convenient, provide faster transfer times, and faster charging times.Speaking of charging, the battery life on this keyboard is excellent. I seem to get about a week's worth of charge with extreme use daily.I know that the keyboard is hot swappable, meaning you can swap out the switches, which ultimately determine the sound of the keyboard, but personally, I haven't messed with that, as I love the way it sounds now.Overall, this is an excellent keyboard. If you're in the market for a wireless keyboard that sounds great, this is the best option out there! I typically don't like spending a ton of money on stuff unless I am absolutely sure of its value, and let me put your mind at ease that this keyboard is worth so much more than what the price tag shows."
    },
    {
        "review": "Today, I initially wrote a negative review of this keyboard due to issues with missed or repeated keystrokes, or keys continuing to fire until another key was pressed. However, after extensive investigation (because I really wanted to keep this keyboard for its mechanical build), I finally discovered that the issues were caused by an obscure, lingering USB driver from a device no longer connected to my PC. That driver was behind all the trouble. I hope this revised review is published before the original one, which is no longer valid.After 15+ years of using Cherry MX Blue switches on a single keyboard that eventually got damaged, I was in search of a replacement. This keyboard seemed like a good option - more compact than my full-size board and featuring “tactile” pale green switches. I hoped they would resemble MX Blues in feel, just without the audible click. Unfortunately, these switches aren’t really that much (or at all) tactile. I don’t understand the claim. There is a point somewhere along the key travel where pressure slightly decreases, but you’d need to press very slowly and have an extremely sensitive touch to notice it. In actual typing, it feels - at least to me - indistinguishable from a regular linear switch. That said, I’ve gotten used to them and didn’t have much trouble adapting after so many years with MX Blues. And the 'thocky' sound still sounds nice.The build quality is solid. The board is sturdy, heavy, and a definite improvement over my old keyboard. I’m not particularly interested in the display or LED lighting. I don’t see the point - especially since the keycaps aren’t translucent, so the lights don’t help in the dark. Omitting those toddler-toy-like features and lowering the price - or populating that space with more keys - would be more useful, in my opinion.I used the included keycap puller to swap the positions of the 'Home' and 'Del' keys, then made the change in the RK software. It took just a few seconds. These settings are retained even if you unplug the keyboard or uninstall the RK software entirely from your PC.All things considered, in terms of mechanical construction, this is a very good keyboard. I do wish the switches were more tactile, but that’s just a matter of personal preference. These switches are still decent, and I’ll probably swap them out in the future for some Kailh clicky switches."
    },
    {
        "review": "The keyboard part is awesome and the keys feel good with a satisfying clack that isn't sharp or too loud.  I use this on a Mac and it does have a Mac mode.  However, I find it hard to use with the wireless dongle (doesn't work on my Mac) and just have to use it wired.  Fortunately, it does have a supplemental USB-A port for my wireless mouse transmitter.  The screen is OK and the volume knob is useful.I think this is a Windows keyboard and Mac compatibility was added afterwards as the F keys don't work even in Mac mode to adjust volume or activate programs like a native Mac keyboard.  Still, the keyboard is worth keeping even if the program needed to control the LED lights and other functions is Windows only."
    },
    {
        "review": "Introduction. The keyboard arrived to me new but clearly used. The package was sealed by a second sticker applied on the previous one, the keyboard had fingerprints, the booklet as in the photo, the power cable even pinched, all the bags ruined. In short, if I buy a new product it must be new, otherwise I would have bought a guaranteed used one for less. I can understand a possible return during the withdrawal period but at least the cable had to be replaced. Going to the top of the range Royal keyboard Kludge. The type of switches mounted are not declared, they arrived with the tactiles and they are still excellent, I hadn't had the opportunity to try them. Very pleasant and which cause a minimum of resistance and then lower decisively. I don't mind them at all. The surface of the keys is slightly reduced on the flat part, which helps to avoid accidentally pressing unwanted keys. The sound is quite pleasant, a little dry but not annoying, probably influenced by the rigid structure of the keyboard. Writing is facilitated by the reduced width of the surface of the keys even though they do not have the ergonomic inclination that other keyboards of the same range offer, but the tone on tone on some keys makes them not immediately identifiable. There is no backlight. An absence that I find inexplicable in keyboards that cost over €100 but I see that, excluding the classic gaming brands, there is little to be found. The keyboard has two height levels with rubberized feet, but it is quite high. Personally, I use it without a riser. Full RGB, totally modulatable via software but with the possibility of managing everything via the integrated screen. Good brightness and intense colours. Possibility of connection via cable, included in the package, via Bluetooth and with dongle. The latter with a lateral magnetic seat. It also has two physical buttons. On one corner we find the selection of connection between cable or key and a little further on the on and off button. The control of the small monitor is comfortable and easy to read, as are the LEDs for Black Num and Caps Lock, also present as LEDs near the Esc key. The signs on F, J and 5 are also easily identifiable by touch. The small monitor has a menu function for various settings which can be navigated via a practical wheel. Battery level, date, type of connection, caps lock and block num, if it is in Win or IOS mode. In short, it shows what is needed and is easily readable. It is also possible to upload GIFs limited to 60 images. A little bit and goes to remove the other info. Nice option but I remain of the opinion that, having a small additional monitor available, you should be able to make better use of it. For example with the operating temperatures of the components, the percentage of use, etc. The software is complete. It offers to save 3 different command mappings and fully manage the lighting. Not very intuitive, I must admit. There is a menu whose use I am not clear about and no information is provided either in the paper manual or in the one available on the site (both in English). Excellent keyboard, overall the one I am finding better with than the others I have tried (and I have tried several in the €50/150 range). It is really a shame about the lack of backlighting as well as, seeing that it is present on other products, the lack of being able to combine the magnetic wrist rest with it."
    },
    {
        "review": "The keyboard works flawlessly and has a really great mechanical feel and sound. The keyboard delivery was very prompt and thank you to the team for providing a hassle free online purchasing equipment"
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a great and reasonably priced keyboard. The key sound/feel is super pleasing, and the quick switch between devices/volume/effects with the dial and screen is awesome."
    },
    {
        "review": "Good keyboard, with nice click sounds and fun to type! The build is sturdy and the keyboard is heavy. I am expecting it to last long. I liked the small screen for configuration. There is a time display on the keyboard, which I don't find useful.Once charge last about two weeks with bluetooth connectivity."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is my 2nd Royal Kludge keyboard and I am very impressed. I've had mechanical keyboards from Logitech, Keychron, Corsair, Drevo and Redragon and I like my RK ones more. This one in particular is just super satisfying to type on. I love the creamy sound from the keys, it's unlike anything else I've owned so far. The green and clay-red color scheme also looks awesome. Keycaps are soft touch but not shine-through which is a bit of a disappointment but not a deal breaker. The little screen and knob is easy to use and actually useful. It's nice to change settings in a menu vs using function keys, and being able to check the time when enjoying full screen entertainment is also handy to me. My audio setup is also able to use the knob as a volume control which is an added bonus. All three connectivity modes work well for me - Bluetooth is reliable, 2.4Ghz is fast and reliable, and wired mode is also fast and reliable. Range on BT/2.4Ghz is average, but since this isnt really an HTPC keyboard I don't think it matters much.My only complaint is the battery life. RK claims they have a gigantic battery in this keyboard but I only get 2-3 days of use between charges, and it takes a loooooong time to charge so it ends up being plugged in a little more than I'd like. If I keep the RGB lighting on the 2nd lowest setting, I can get another day or two of usage but I wish I didn't have to charge this keyboard once a week.I really like this keyboard overall and I'd definitely recommend it! It's a good keyboard for full price but RK has sales all the time so I'd wait for a 20-25% discount, where it becomes a killer deal in my opinion.Edit: 3 months in and still very fun to type on and 100% reliable. I just keep the backlighting off most of the time now to conserve battery and it lasts a while without the backlight on 2.4Ghz link (2-3 weeks light use). Only thing I don't like after a few months of use is having to press fn + PgDn for the End key."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "I am very satisfied with this smartwatch. The screen is high-quality, responsive to touch, and easy to set up. It connects seamlessly to my phone, and Bluetooth calls come through clearly. I also appreciate the wide range of options for tracking sports activities and monitoring metrics like heart rate and sleep.The battery life has been excellent with normal use, and the design is comfortable enough for all-day wear. Overall, it has performed exactly as expected, and the experience has been great since I first received it. I would certainly buy it again and recommend it to anyone looking for a practical, feature-rich, and user-friendly watch."
