[
    {
        "review": "I have tested numerous “smart watches” in my quest to find a decent replacement for my ever faithful and reliable, Fitbit Versa 4. I purchased it almost four years ago to replace the original Fitbit Versa. But then Google took over Fitbit, and I was looking for a reliable alternative to the Fitbit products. I tried a Samsung Galaxy watch 7 that lost steps by the thousands each day. I tried an Apple Smart Watch that sometimes had to be charged more than once EVERY DAY! I tried a Parsonver (less expensive) but even though it was advertised and labeled IP 68, (indicating that it would withstand submersion in water) I actually received an email from the company before the watch was even delivered advising me to be careful even showering or washing my hands with it on! SERIOUSLY???? And the step counts were not even close to correct!  Then I tried an Amazfit BIP6 (also advertised as IP 68, but clearly IS NOT) and thought I had finally found what I was looking for, until it completely malfunctioned after only 3 weeks of use. So Amazon replaced it with ANOTHER BIP6, and that one lasted almost 5 months before it began to seriously malfunction and the touchscreen no longer worked. So my new search began and I decided to give Garmin a try as my walking and swimming buddies swear by their Garmins, and the results that they get with them compare favorably to my old Fitbit. I have now only had the Garmin Vivoactive 5 for about a month, but so far, it is AWESOME! It tracks extremely well with the Fitbit, and I have had no issues whatsoever with water infiltration. If that changes, I will be back to downgrade my rating, but so far, so GREAT! This proves that you don’t have to buy the most expensive models of ANY brand of fitness watch to find one that does what you need it to do. Most of them have more functions than you will ever need or use anyway. I bought separate, different color bands to customize it and love that you have a lot of choices for watch faces, so pick the one that works best for you! (Although - once again, Fitbit has a much better and more user friendly watch face selection portal!)The one potential “glitch” I thought I had finally discovered with my Vivoactive 5, but which turned out not to be an issue, occurred when I was flying internationally. I was returning from Europe to the US on an evening flight where the time in the US was six hours earlier. So both my FITBIT and my Garmin thought that when enough flight time had elapsed, they went past midnight and started their metrics over as a new day - meaning steps completely zeroed out and started over. But not long after I landed at 7 pm US time, my Fitbit picked back up with the previous day’s step tally, but my Garmin still showed the reduced step count tallied as a “new day”. I was disappointed with this until I checked the metrics in the Garmin app and noted that the “two day tallies” indicated on the watch itself were actually (appropriately!) counted and displayed as a single day step counts in the app. So yes, I am sold on the Garmin. But I do still have to wonder why if FITBIT - even early versions - could have successfully addressed ALL of the issues I have found with so many competitor brands, why can’t all the others? Maybe I shouldn’t have been so wary of Google taking over Fitbit when I started investigating other Smart watches, but I learned a lot about different brands and their bogus IP 68 and 5 ATM ratings!So to reiterate, Garmin is exactly what it says it is, and Fitbit is as well. Do your comparisons!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I've been using Garmin for running for years now. They've improved a lot! This one does everything I need and more. I like the body battery, sleep info. The 5 syncs up to GPS much faster than my 4 to run or walk. I love the music, however, my headphones sometimes disconnect to the watch while running. Not sure if it's the headphones or the watch. It's pretty easy to use if you've had one before. It has changed on the strength where you hit the bottom button vs tapping the watch to enter weight/reps (don't have to do that anymore, not that it was a problem). Overall I'm super satisfied. Can see it better as well with the new display."
    },
    {
        "review": "I'm coming from an Apple Watch Series 5. I had gotten frustrated with the battery life and sleep tracking and wanted to see if a more fitness-focused watch would be a better fit. I'm not a big runner or someone who does a lot of intense exercise, but I do like to track my exercise and sleep and get nudges to move when I've been sitting down for too long. I also like to get notifications on my wrist without needing to pull out my phone.Compared to the Apple Watch, the first major thing I noticed was this is nowhere close in terms of smarts. I had gotten used to Apple's smooth interface, built in microphone and speaker for voice commands, and often useful apps that can be used without needing to get my phone out. That being said, after a few days I can confidently say that I don't need them. I would argue that Garmin knows what this is and what it is not - it's a fitness device first and foremost, and everything else comes second. And on that basis they nailed it - the fitness tracking has been excellent and noticeably better than the Apple Watch especially in sleep tracking. I also much prefer their approach to goals, which tracks exercise over the course of a week rather than focusing specifically on individual days. Whenever I was sick or had a down day I would feel guilty if I didn't close all of my rings on my Apple Watch, but I don't feel that same pressure with the Garmin. I do miss being able to use voice commands with my watch, but I could have gotten a more expensive Garmin if I really wanted that, and I don't think it's worth it for me.The second thing I noticed: not having to charge your watch every day is a game changer. This was easily my biggest frustration with the Apple Watch since it's marketed as a device you wear 24x7 and yet needs to be charged every day. And considering how long it took to charge my Series 5, this was a major annoyance. I couldn't charge it overnight, because I wanted sleep tracking. I couldn't charge it in the morning, because I like to start off my day with exercise and it doesn't charge fast enough to do it after a workout and before work. And if I charged it in the evening, I would often forget to put it on before bed and miss out on the sleep tracking. The Garmin is a breath of fresh air by comparison - I charge it about once a week, and with my relatively light usage it can probably go close to two. I took a multi day trip recently and I didn't once have to worry about charging my watch, which was a pleasant change.Overall I think this watch is a great fit for a more casual user who isn't running marathons but wants to track fitness and sleep and get some extras like smartphone notifications and see the weather at a glance. If you want something with more smart features and better build quality (i.e. more metal and less plastic), then the Venu might be a better fit. Or they have the Forerunner if you do run marathons. Or a bunch of other models for more specific use cases. But I would highly recommend giving this watch a try if you're looking for the same things that I am."
    },
    {
        "review": "Great so far. I tried another brand the caused a rash from the heart rate monitor (not the band because I swap them for cloth or leather). Garmin Vivoactive 5 has not caused any skin irritation! I am pleased with the metrics it gives. The app takes some getting used to, but it isn't complicated to navigate...just different from fitbit. So far the battery charge lasts for at least 7 days. It charges quickly. I like the screen size, metrics and words are easy to read. The screen reacts well to touch even with a screen protector on. It is easy to access the watches features. I like the wake up summary. It gives me prompts to move and stretch. It gives sleep advice and measures body battery throughout the day. The watch is comfortable to wear and looks great with everything. I swap between a nylon band and leather band. I would highly recommend."
    },
    {
        "review": "I’m someone who really really did not want a watch of any sort, especially not an Apple Watch or anything else that is bloated with unnecessary hardware that needs to be charged every single day. Charging a phone every day is enough of a pain without needing to add any other junk, so no thanks to the useless gimmick-loaded “smart” watches.But I really needed to do fitness tracking because without some sort of gadget I just have zero idea how lazy or not lazy I’ve been lately and whether or not I need to get at least some minimal exercise.And my #1 must have feature is week-long battery charge life. This watch is good enough, even though the battery life might be more like 5 days in some cases if you’re using it for activities a lot where the gps is turned on. But for those not running with the gps every day or constantly tinkering with it a full battery charge seems to last a week. That is, charge it for an hour on Sunday and no need to charge it again until the same time next Sunday. You could get the battery to last longer if you’re willing to shut off absolutely everything that might use more power.I started out with a Garmin vivosmart 5 because I didn’t think I needed anything beyond that, but I have sleep problems and decided I needed better sleep tracking. So I ended up with this one even though I don’t really care about random silly “apps” on a watch.Overall this watch has been excellent. It has a very good UI and the fitness tracking features a quite good. The sleep tracking is decent, but still has its issues that hopefully Garmin will fix with software updates. In particular the “nap tracking” doesn’t seem fully baked yet, and things like laying back in a recliner reading a book or watching TV can get falsely detected as a “nap”. Then there’s no way to delete a false nap on the watch or in the Garmin Connect app.There are still a few minor bugs that some people will run into but they seem to be things that can be fixed with firmware updates and I haven’t run into anything completely feature-breaking.If you feel like messing with it you can also install some “apps” like custom watch face designs or sunset/sunrise times, more detailed weather and such. But even without installing anything the watch does everything needed for health and fitness stuff.Almost all of the apps that come with the device can be uninstalled, so if you don’t need “women’s health tracking” for example then you can just remove that to free up space.Their mobile app is pretty well designed and only awkward in some parts of the configuration.The watch reliably synchronizes as long as you leave the phone app running in the background and I’ve never had any Bluetooth connection failures, lag, or lost pairings.Things that the watch is not good for are things that you’d typically use a phone for like communication, turn by turn navigation, maps, games, and such. But I don’t want a watch to do any of that stuff and I’d rather have it save its battery charge so I don’t have to charge it constantly.So overall, this is “the” watch you want if you want as little nonsense as possible and don’t want to worry about battery charge all the time but still need full health and fitness tracking stuff beyond what the $75-150 models offer. And there are also no subscriptions required."
    },
    {
        "review": "I was concerned the watch face would be too big but I got used to it right away. The size makes the display easy to read so I can keep track of my steps with just a glance. I also use the bike riding function (GPS) to track miles.  I wish it would convert miles biked to steps taken but not sure any watch does that. I used the golf app the other day for distance and scoring. With any new electronics it takes a while and patience to figure it out but once I did I really like it. I'm still learning. I also like the sleep tracking function.  Long battery life."
    },
    {
        "review": "The Garmin vívoactive® 5 has been a great watch to help monitor my daily activities, sleep, heart rate, and so much more. I wasn't so interested in the idea of connecting to my phone but rather the health benefits. It was a great purchase. The screen size is not too large for my small 5 inch wrist, but also large enough that I can clearly see the screen. The watch was easy to set up, make adjustments to as I learn about the various functions, and was easy to connect to my phone and work benefits. I like that I am able to select which features I would like to use. Garmin is very compatible with many fitness programs. I don't have good success wearing a silicone strap so I did purchase other bands which were simple to remove and connect to the watch head. I believe Garmin was a very good choice for a first health and fitness watch for me."
    },
    {
        "review": "Just received the watch today, it’s nice but I have some concerns. For starters the band is completely silicone and plastic- the buckle piece is plastic, even the case around the watch is plastic. For $200 you’d think it would at-least be metal. The setup was easy- but came with very little instruction for other things like changing watch faces, setting up apps, etc. Learning how to use the watch and what the buttons all do is pure guessing.The charger is USB-C- if it wasn’t for my laptop I would have no way to charge the watch. Should have come with a compatible block.UPDATE:After using the watch for several days I do like it. While the overall construction (plastic casing) could be better, the overall operation works great. Steps and heart rate seem accurate. The display looks nice, customizable, and the watch is comfortable. The stress levels  don’t seem to be accurate. I get “high stress” alerts when all I’ve been doing is laying in bed all day.Just add a titanium casing to the surrounding of the watch and provide more thorough instructions on overall use + updates for accuracy on certain apps and it would be perfect!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I'm writing this review about 3 weeks after purchase, so I've already gotten to know the watch well. The product is amazing and meets all my expectations and even more. Great workmanship, the watch is light and the strap is very soft, so you practically don't feel it on your hand. A fantastic display that shows everything perfectly, even in direct sunlight. With continuous heart rate measurement, smartphone notifications turned on, recording several activities and making several payments a day, the watch lasts 7 full days on a single charge and usually has about 20-30% of the battery left. For me, it's a fantastic result. Great customizable dials that can be downloaded for free from the Connect IQ store. The watch will be perfect for a person who wants a small and light watch with contactless payments, continuous health monitoring and activity recording. Of course, for professional athletes, Garmin has better devices, but for amateurs it is a very good choice."
    },
    {
        "review": "I’ve been using the watch for a while now. It’s amazing and well worth the price. Highly recommend"
    },
    {
        "review": "So far good. Size just nice not too big or too small. comfortable to wear all day in and out. For health enthusiasts. Sufficient data work as it is. Not too many unwanted features which could confuse and hard to navigate around. My second simply watch for health tracking purposes"
    },
    {
        "review": "Excellent product like all others from the brand. It took me a while to choose, as there are so many options. Just very happy with it. Besides being too cute."
    },
    {
        "review": "It took a while to arrive, but so far it works perfectly."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "Okay so it took a little bit of effort trying to figure out the app you have to install but it actually does say it in the guide. It's a DA fit. I'm now able to call people you can only add a contacts at a time but you can still not have to like add people you can just dial a phone number and you'll hear it ringing my person didn't answer the phone. I haven't figured out the text message thing yet because I was in a text message conversation with my friends and I have to look at that. I'm able to listen to YouTube and Pandora from The watchAt first it wasn't connecting but I realized after I shut the watch off I forgot to turn the Bluetooth back on to pair it from my phone. The Bluetooth should be y66d. I was going to throw this one out & not even bother returning it and find a different watch I'm thrilled it working. The device time and location is all set before it was all off the weather's working so just give the watch at least 20 minutes of your time patience is key here. I'm actually obsessed my old watch did not have these capabilities it did not play music I could not see text messages or send out calls. Update I set up the watch Sunday june 7th, it is now Tuesday almost noon June 9th and my watch is already down to 10%, the battery life does not last as long as my first watch would last over a week"
    },
    {
        "review": "I am beyond happy with my new watch! You get the luxury without the cost. It has so many amazing features using the \"Glory Fit App,\" which is very user-friendly.  I'm able to take calls, make calls, and text from the watch. Listen to music by connecting via bluetooth.  It allows you to customize countless features like temperature units, weight, and metrics. You can choose between multiple digital face diplays in your watch settings. You can also set the length of time display stays lit before it disappears. It features: training plans, goal setting and allows you to connect with friends. The watch straps are interchangeable so that you can attach different colors or styles. You can monitor your heart rate, blood oxygen levels, sleep, step count, weight, and more! It has a voice assistant and alarm clock as well. You can also sync most of your Apps on your paired device (I did my my phone), so that you can receive notifications and even use the same functions on your watch simultaneously.  For example, I paired my youtube account so that whenever I receive a notification in the Youtube App of a new video, it shows that notification on my phone as well. I can even listen to YouTube using the play, pause, and stop buttons from my phone. I also synced our security camera device so that I can receive those notifications as well while away from home. I am able to select any number of applications I want to individually sync or not. This is great so that I can be selective and not have my watch going off every second of the day.  It helps me to stay motivated as far as my fitness goes because I'm able to set goals - it sends you an achievement reward notification, which sounds once a goal has been reached. It has so many features that I haven't even begun to tap into. There is a slight learning curve, as with sny smart device, but it's very  user-friendly after you patiently program and customize each feature to fit your needs and get the hang of it.I love the color selection options. I chose the black and light pink/rose gold color (for him & her), I also ordered (from another seller) face covers for protection. The covers were very easy to slip on (see in photos & video). It comes with a convenient USB, and the watch is packed in its own individual box.  Note: THE BOX HAS (2) WATCHES PICTURED BUT ONLY COMES WITH ONE WATCH.  I just want to mention it because my pictures & video show (2) watches, but it's only because I bought them separately in those exact colors pictured on their box. I took multiple pictures to help display some of the features and to demonstrate the ease at attaching a face cover (sold separately via another seller). This is an amazing buy, and it makes the perfect gift. Be sure to check out the video ~ hope this helps!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Good watch for the money. You do have to download an app. It is not overly complicated to navigate. I struggled some getting it to connect with the app but I’m not tech savvy. Battery life was good. It would last for two days. It charged quickly. It didn’t track my sleep well, which was one of the reasons I bought it, but it does have to be in constant contact with your wrist and I don’t like my watches that tight. It did do a decent job on vitals and had charts to show weekly levels. The negatives were you had to change your settings to a certain exercise to get a more accurate result. Especially on steps. Since I’m up doing a lot of walking, lifting, bending and sometimes crawling when cleaning the house I found you get a better representation of steps if you changed the setting to indoor walking. 😁 The drawback on any of the exercise settings I would forget to switch back to normal. Which is a negative on me.The next one is a very personal preference. I really wanted to be able to change the bands. I like sparkles and my daughter likes blues. I couldn’t find any that would fit the watch. We ended up making a few cuts here and adjustments there to get them to fit but I don’t think most people would want to do that.My husband bought me an apple watch for my birthday and my daughter didn’t like having anything on her wrist so I gave them to the grandsons 10 & 9. They are obviously not gentle with them but they are still going. I would place that into the sturdy column."
    },
    {
        "review": "I’m super happy with this purchase. I honestly wasn’t expecting to like this smartwatch as much as I do, but it surprised me in a good way. The screen is big, bright, and very clear, which makes it easy to read messages, check notifications, and track my workouts throughout the day. The fitness tracking also seems pretty accurate from what I’ve used so far.One thing I really like is being able to answer calls from the watch. The call quality sounds decent, and it’s convenient when my phone isn’t right next to me. It’s also very comfortable to wear all day and doesn’t feel heavy on my wrist. The battery life has been solid too. I usually get around two days before needing to recharge it.The funniest part is that almost everyone who sees me wearing it thinks I’m wearing an Apple Watch lol. It definitely has a premium look to it without the expensive Apple price tag. Overall, I’m really glad I bought it."
    },
    {
        "review": "I really like this smartwatch. The screen is clear, it’s easy to use, and the call feature works great. It has plenty of fitness options and is comfortable to wear. Great value for the price and definitely recommend it!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I loved this watch for the price.  But I purchased 2 watches on May 8th and received them on May 12th.  The first watch literally died completely after 2 weeks of using it.  Tried charging it, tried holding the button...nothing.  I am deaf, and I rely on the vibrations on the watch when someone texts me.  I never used the call function, so I can't tell you how well it works.  Compatibility, it's great!  Connectivity, it stays connected for the MOST part, but at times, it won't stay connected.I wish the battery lasted longer.  It lasted less than 7 days!  Not the kind of watch I wanted.  There are other watches I've had in the past that lasted close to a month!  This one sucked on battery life.  The sleep monitoring sucked.  It doesn't record it well at all—the heart rate and oxygen work just as they're supposed to do.I cannot contact seller to tell them their product sucks.  So, on to a watch search that would last longer than what I have now."
    },
    {
        "review": "Im at little on the fence about this watch. For the price it is great and when I first got it I was in love. Now that I've had it for a few weeks and have been able to use it I'm not sure how I feel about it all the time.  It adds steps randomly, like even when I'm sleeping (I don't move in my sleep) I wake up and it'll say i took like 400 steps. The heart rate monitor and blood oxygen monitor seem to work just as intended. I got this watch to help track my sleep and this is where I keep having issues. Sometimes it won't register that I slept, or doesn't record it properly (and i have even started wearing the watch backwards so that the sensor is on my wrist so it can get a better read).  Today it said I slept for 3 hours while I was out running errands. I also accidentallyet the watch die the other day (it lasted a few days without charging), but it seems like since then I've been having a lot of issues with the Bluetooth,  the call features, tracking and notifications.The app is also pretty basic.  Sometimes it's hard to find the settings your looking for and they don't give you many options to begin with. When I go in to look at my sleep it shows a graph which is nice, but if you want to try to see the times you have to press on this tiny sliver and most of the time it highlights the wrong thing and doesn't really break anything down for you.I know I got this watch at a great price, but I will be honest I have been looking for a different one similarly priced, considering sending this back.I guess if my opinion changes I'll update my review."
    },
    {
        "review": "Wifey loves hers. We submit recorded data to insurance company for extra incentives."
    },
    {
        "review": "I was pleasantly surprised by this smart watch. The quality, in general, is good, as is the software and the application to pair it via Bluetooth. Below, I will try to break down the pros and cons: PROS: - Multitude of functions that facilitate daily activities, such as sports, music, news, phone functionalities, etc. - Very intuitive navigation menus. - Great display presentation of its different options. - Design adapted to the preferences common to all users. - Variety of presentation of icons, as well as spheres, both analog and digital. CONS: - To name a few, the improvable duration of the battery, for a user who, without handling it continuously, uses the watch for basic daily functions."
    },
    {
        "review": "Chinese crap. Stay away"
    },
    {
        "review": "I purchased this Smart Watch recently and was very satisfied. Here are some of the features that impressed me the most: Ease of use The interface is extremely intuitive and user-friendly. Even those who are not very familiar with technology will find it easy to navigate between the various functions. The instructions are clear and pairing with the phone was quick and smooth. Charging Time One of the things that impressed me the most was the battery life. With average usage, I was able to get the charge to last for several days without having to recharge frequently. This is especially useful for people like me who don't want to worry about having to plug in the device every night. Features The Smart Watch offers a wide range of features, including fitness tracking, message and call notifications, music control and much more. All these features work perfectly and add significant value to the device.DesignThe design is elegant and comfortable to wear. It is suitable for both everyday use and more formal occasions. Furthermore, the quality of the materials seems very good, giving a feeling of robustness and durability. In conclusion, I highly recommend this Smart Watch to anyone looking for a reliable device, with long battery life and easy to use. It exceeded my expectations and I am very happy with my purchase."
    },
    {
        "review": "Quality, price, excellent, long battery life, large dial, easy to use"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "I have tested numerous “smart watches” in my quest to find a decent replacement for my ever faithful and reliable, Fitbit Versa 4. I purchased it almost four years ago to replace the original Fitbit Versa. But then Google took over Fitbit, and I was looking for a reliable alternative to the Fitbit products. I tried a Samsung Galaxy watch 7 that lost steps by the thousands each day. I tried an Apple Smart Watch that sometimes had to be charged more than once EVERY DAY! I tried a Parsonver (less expensive) but even though it was advertised and labeled IP 68, (indicating that it would withstand submersion in water) I actually received an email from the company before the watch was even delivered advising me to be careful even showering or washing my hands with it on! SERIOUSLY???? And the step counts were not even close to correct!  Then I tried an Amazfit BIP6 (also advertised as IP 68, but clearly IS NOT) and thought I had finally found what I was looking for, until it completely malfunctioned after only 3 weeks of use. So Amazon replaced it with ANOTHER BIP6, and that one lasted almost 5 months before it began to seriously malfunction and the touchscreen no longer worked. So my new search began and I decided to give Garmin a try as my walking and swimming buddies swear by their Garmins, and the results that they get with them compare favorably to my old Fitbit. I have now only had the Garmin Vivoactive 5 for about a month, but so far, it is AWESOME! It tracks extremely well with the Fitbit, and I have had no issues whatsoever with water infiltration. If that changes, I will be back to downgrade my rating, but so far, so GREAT! This proves that you don’t have to buy the most expensive models of ANY brand of fitness watch to find one that does what you need it to do. Most of them have more functions than you will ever need or use anyway. I bought separate, different color bands to customize it and love that you have a lot of choices for watch faces, so pick the one that works best for you! (Although - once again, Fitbit has a much better and more user friendly watch face selection portal!)The one potential “glitch” I thought I had finally discovered with my Vivoactive 5, but which turned out not to be an issue, occurred when I was flying internationally. I was returning from Europe to the US on an evening flight where the time in the US was six hours earlier. So both my FITBIT and my Garmin thought that when enough flight time had elapsed, they went past midnight and started their metrics over as a new day - meaning steps completely zeroed out and started over. But not long after I landed at 7 pm US time, my Fitbit picked back up with the previous day’s step tally, but my Garmin still showed the reduced step count tallied as a “new day”. I was disappointed with this until I checked the metrics in the Garmin app and noted that the “two day tallies” indicated on the watch itself were actually (appropriately!) counted and displayed as a single day step counts in the app. So yes, I am sold on the Garmin. But I do still have to wonder why if FITBIT - even early versions - could have successfully addressed ALL of the issues I have found with so many competitor brands, why can’t all the others? Maybe I shouldn’t have been so wary of Google taking over Fitbit when I started investigating other Smart watches, but I learned a lot about different brands and their bogus IP 68 and 5 ATM ratings!So to reiterate, Garmin is exactly what it says it is, and Fitbit is as well. Do your comparisons!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I've been using Garmin for running for years now. They've improved a lot! This one does everything I need and more. I like the body battery, sleep info. The 5 syncs up to GPS much faster than my 4 to run or walk. I love the music, however, my headphones sometimes disconnect to the watch while running. Not sure if it's the headphones or the watch. It's pretty easy to use if you've had one before. It has changed on the strength where you hit the bottom button vs tapping the watch to enter weight/reps (don't have to do that anymore, not that it was a problem). Overall I'm super satisfied. Can see it better as well with the new display."
    },
    {
        "review": "I'm coming from an Apple Watch Series 5. I had gotten frustrated with the battery life and sleep tracking and wanted to see if a more fitness-focused watch would be a better fit. I'm not a big runner or someone who does a lot of intense exercise, but I do like to track my exercise and sleep and get nudges to move when I've been sitting down for too long. I also like to get notifications on my wrist without needing to pull out my phone.Compared to the Apple Watch, the first major thing I noticed was this is nowhere close in terms of smarts. I had gotten used to Apple's smooth interface, built in microphone and speaker for voice commands, and often useful apps that can be used without needing to get my phone out. That being said, after a few days I can confidently say that I don't need them. I would argue that Garmin knows what this is and what it is not - it's a fitness device first and foremost, and everything else comes second. And on that basis they nailed it - the fitness tracking has been excellent and noticeably better than the Apple Watch especially in sleep tracking. I also much prefer their approach to goals, which tracks exercise over the course of a week rather than focusing specifically on individual days. Whenever I was sick or had a down day I would feel guilty if I didn't close all of my rings on my Apple Watch, but I don't feel that same pressure with the Garmin. I do miss being able to use voice commands with my watch, but I could have gotten a more expensive Garmin if I really wanted that, and I don't think it's worth it for me.The second thing I noticed: not having to charge your watch every day is a game changer. This was easily my biggest frustration with the Apple Watch since it's marketed as a device you wear 24x7 and yet needs to be charged every day. And considering how long it took to charge my Series 5, this was a major annoyance. I couldn't charge it overnight, because I wanted sleep tracking. I couldn't charge it in the morning, because I like to start off my day with exercise and it doesn't charge fast enough to do it after a workout and before work. And if I charged it in the evening, I would often forget to put it on before bed and miss out on the sleep tracking. The Garmin is a breath of fresh air by comparison - I charge it about once a week, and with my relatively light usage it can probably go close to two. I took a multi day trip recently and I didn't once have to worry about charging my watch, which was a pleasant change.Overall I think this watch is a great fit for a more casual user who isn't running marathons but wants to track fitness and sleep and get some extras like smartphone notifications and see the weather at a glance. If you want something with more smart features and better build quality (i.e. more metal and less plastic), then the Venu might be a better fit. Or they have the Forerunner if you do run marathons. Or a bunch of other models for more specific use cases. But I would highly recommend giving this watch a try if you're looking for the same things that I am."
    },
    {
        "review": "Great so far. I tried another brand the caused a rash from the heart rate monitor (not the band because I swap them for cloth or leather). Garmin Vivoactive 5 has not caused any skin irritation! I am pleased with the metrics it gives. The app takes some getting used to, but it isn't complicated to navigate...just different from fitbit. So far the battery charge lasts for at least 7 days. It charges quickly. I like the screen size, metrics and words are easy to read. The screen reacts well to touch even with a screen protector on. It is easy to access the watches features. I like the wake up summary. It gives me prompts to move and stretch. It gives sleep advice and measures body battery throughout the day. The watch is comfortable to wear and looks great with everything. I swap between a nylon band and leather band. I would highly recommend."
    },
    {
        "review": "I’m someone who really really did not want a watch of any sort, especially not an Apple Watch or anything else that is bloated with unnecessary hardware that needs to be charged every single day. Charging a phone every day is enough of a pain without needing to add any other junk, so no thanks to the useless gimmick-loaded “smart” watches.But I really needed to do fitness tracking because without some sort of gadget I just have zero idea how lazy or not lazy I’ve been lately and whether or not I need to get at least some minimal exercise.And my #1 must have feature is week-long battery charge life. This watch is good enough, even though the battery life might be more like 5 days in some cases if you’re using it for activities a lot where the gps is turned on. But for those not running with the gps every day or constantly tinkering with it a full battery charge seems to last a week. That is, charge it for an hour on Sunday and no need to charge it again until the same time next Sunday. You could get the battery to last longer if you’re willing to shut off absolutely everything that might use more power.I started out with a Garmin vivosmart 5 because I didn’t think I needed anything beyond that, but I have sleep problems and decided I needed better sleep tracking. So I ended up with this one even though I don’t really care about random silly “apps” on a watch.Overall this watch has been excellent. It has a very good UI and the fitness tracking features a quite good. The sleep tracking is decent, but still has its issues that hopefully Garmin will fix with software updates. In particular the “nap tracking” doesn’t seem fully baked yet, and things like laying back in a recliner reading a book or watching TV can get falsely detected as a “nap”. Then there’s no way to delete a false nap on the watch or in the Garmin Connect app.There are still a few minor bugs that some people will run into but they seem to be things that can be fixed with firmware updates and I haven’t run into anything completely feature-breaking.If you feel like messing with it you can also install some “apps” like custom watch face designs or sunset/sunrise times, more detailed weather and such. But even without installing anything the watch does everything needed for health and fitness stuff.Almost all of the apps that come with the device can be uninstalled, so if you don’t need “women’s health tracking” for example then you can just remove that to free up space.Their mobile app is pretty well designed and only awkward in some parts of the configuration.The watch reliably synchronizes as long as you leave the phone app running in the background and I’ve never had any Bluetooth connection failures, lag, or lost pairings.Things that the watch is not good for are things that you’d typically use a phone for like communication, turn by turn navigation, maps, games, and such. But I don’t want a watch to do any of that stuff and I’d rather have it save its battery charge so I don’t have to charge it constantly.So overall, this is “the” watch you want if you want as little nonsense as possible and don’t want to worry about battery charge all the time but still need full health and fitness tracking stuff beyond what the $75-150 models offer. And there are also no subscriptions required."
    },
    {
        "review": "I was concerned the watch face would be too big but I got used to it right away. The size makes the display easy to read so I can keep track of my steps with just a glance. I also use the bike riding function (GPS) to track miles.  I wish it would convert miles biked to steps taken but not sure any watch does that. I used the golf app the other day for distance and scoring. With any new electronics it takes a while and patience to figure it out but once I did I really like it. I'm still learning. I also like the sleep tracking function.  Long battery life."
    },
    {
        "review": "The Garmin vívoactive® 5 has been a great watch to help monitor my daily activities, sleep, heart rate, and so much more. I wasn't so interested in the idea of connecting to my phone but rather the health benefits. It was a great purchase. The screen size is not too large for my small 5 inch wrist, but also large enough that I can clearly see the screen. The watch was easy to set up, make adjustments to as I learn about the various functions, and was easy to connect to my phone and work benefits. I like that I am able to select which features I would like to use. Garmin is very compatible with many fitness programs. I don't have good success wearing a silicone strap so I did purchase other bands which were simple to remove and connect to the watch head. I believe Garmin was a very good choice for a first health and fitness watch for me."
    },
    {
        "review": "Just received the watch today, it’s nice but I have some concerns. For starters the band is completely silicone and plastic- the buckle piece is plastic, even the case around the watch is plastic. For $200 you’d think it would at-least be metal. The setup was easy- but came with very little instruction for other things like changing watch faces, setting up apps, etc. Learning how to use the watch and what the buttons all do is pure guessing.The charger is USB-C- if it wasn’t for my laptop I would have no way to charge the watch. Should have come with a compatible block.UPDATE:After using the watch for several days I do like it. While the overall construction (plastic casing) could be better, the overall operation works great. Steps and heart rate seem accurate. The display looks nice, customizable, and the watch is comfortable. The stress levels  don’t seem to be accurate. I get “high stress” alerts when all I’ve been doing is laying in bed all day.Just add a titanium casing to the surrounding of the watch and provide more thorough instructions on overall use + updates for accuracy on certain apps and it would be perfect!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I'm writing this review about 3 weeks after purchase, so I've already gotten to know the watch well. The product is amazing and meets all my expectations and even more. Great workmanship, the watch is light and the strap is very soft, so you practically don't feel it on your hand. A fantastic display that shows everything perfectly, even in direct sunlight. With continuous heart rate measurement, smartphone notifications turned on, recording several activities and making several payments a day, the watch lasts 7 full days on a single charge and usually has about 20-30% of the battery left. For me, it's a fantastic result. Great customizable dials that can be downloaded for free from the Connect IQ store. The watch will be perfect for a person who wants a small and light watch with contactless payments, continuous health monitoring and activity recording. Of course, for professional athletes, Garmin has better devices, but for amateurs it is a very good choice."
    },
