[
    {
        "review": "After testing several wearables, the Samsung Galaxy Fit3 has completely blown me away. If you are looking for a device that delivers premium health tracking without the bulk or price tag of a full-scale smartwatch, this tracker is an absolute home run.​Here is my breakdown after putting it through its paces daily:​Superior Sleep Analytics: The sleep tracking feature on this device is incredibly sophisticated for its price point. It doesn't just log when you fall asleep and wake up; it breaks down your sleep stages (REM, light, deep) and gives you a legitimate, actionable sleep score. It’s light and slim enough that it doesn't bother my wrist at night at all.​Flawless Menstrual Cycle Tracking: The health tracking integration is seamless. Logging and predicting periods is straightforward, reliable, and integrates perfectly with the Samsung Health app dashboard, keeping all my data in one clean, easy-to-read spot.​Accurate Step Counting & Daily Metrics: The pedometer is highly accurate and doesn't pick up phantom steps from just moving your arms around. The beautiful, crisp 1.6-inch AMOLED display makes checking your step goals mid-stride incredibly satisfying, even in direct sunlight. Unbeatable Battery & Value: Because it runs on a streamlined operating system rather than a heavy smartwatch OS, the battery life is spectacular. You can easily go days and days without even thinking about a charger, which completely outperforms premium smartwatches that require daily charging.Final Verdict: At this price tier, the build quality and premium aluminum frame feel luxurious, and the feature set rivals trackers double its price. It provides total reliability for daily health monitoring. If you want top-tier metrics on a budget, this is the one to get!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Arrived quicker than scheduled so that was a nice surprise.  I love the soft pink color and I also like the larger screen.  My last fitness tracker was smaller screen.  This looks similar to a well known expensive brand watch.  I just got it today and  got right to programming it so I could start wearing it.  I can't rate it on durability or accuracy yet but I sure hope it will be more reliable than my last fitness tracker which I won't mention the brand.  I like the brightness of the screen which I find easier to read outdoors.  UPDATE: Samsung Galaxy Fit 3 (2024) review continued:  I'm quite pleased with this item as it seems to actually work each day! Imagine a fitness tracker that actually works and doesn't randomly stop mid day!! Another plus that I discovered by accident is my watch reminds me when I'm out of range of my phone.  This helps me to remember to not leave my phone behind if I'm working outside or over at my son's house.  I have gotten home and realized an hour later that my phone isn't in my bag.  This watch vibrates right away when the phone isn't in range.  As an older person this is a help for me.  I want to have my phone nearby in case I have a medical issue.  I also love the bigger screen and the watch is very responsive which was another frustration with my last fitness tracker.  I love the long charge battery life.  I doubt I'll ever go back to my prior fitness tracker as it was so maddening to NOT be able to figure out the issues and sometimes it would \"wake up\" and start working again.  This Samsung Galaxy Fit 3 is easy to wear and provides the health tracking numbers that matter to me.  Thumbs up."
    },
    {
        "review": "This watch is honestly almost perfect. Literally like a 9/10. The design is sleek, the watch face is not too big for my tiny wrist, has a vibrant and attractive display, and the watch band is really nice and soft! I had a fit bit charge 4 before this one and it finally quit on me. I wanted a low budget option because I haven't been consistent and don't have 300 dollars to waste for it to sit around if I end up not staying consistent. For 50 dollars this beauty fits nearly all my needs. Paired to my samsung phone immediately when I pulled it out of the box. Super easy to set up, and tons of different ways to customize the watch face. Have I mentioned its so pretty?? I don't typically wear a watch but I wanted the health metrics to track my heart rate and steps. This is one of two mild caveats. It doesn't actively track your heart rate. Every couple minutes or so it pings your heart rate to get more of a time line than a live feed. No worries, it pings often enough you get a good idea. Only mildly sucks because I use the live feed heart rate monitor to help clue me in to if I might have a panic attack or if I'm overreacting. Another thing. I have been using google health to track my calories and set specific goals for some of my macros I'm trying to hit. Unfortunately, the samsung health app doesn't get this specific and just gives you the suggested daily intake as a metric rather than letting you set your own. What sucks more is that these two apps don't sync with each other. So now I have 2 seperate apps to track my health. All in all. Its really a fantastic little gadget. For $50 I would 10000% buy it again if they came out with a newer version. Will update again after I've had it a few weeks."
    },
    {
        "review": "I just received my watch today 2/15 it came fast as promised. I can already say that I like the watch. The color, it was easy to setup and pair to my S24 Ultra with no problems. It's slim, sleek, and cute on my wrist. When I got the watch it was already at 76% I used my phone to charge it to 100% I love the different functions the watch offers. You can adjust the brightness to your liking. In will update on the battery life after a week. It has many different options face changes for the watch and it's easy to read. And for the price it's really affordable. I'm not disappointed at all about purchasing this watch. I'm a HUGE FAN of Samsung products.Update 2/17 - I wanted to vive an update after me having the watch since Sunday afternoon 2/15. The battery life does not live up to as it is advertised for 13 days. You'll probably get about 4 or maybe 5 days at best. Right now as I'm updating my review on this watch I'm at 39% and it's Tuesday evening 2/17 so I know maybe tomorrow I'll have to charge it. The accuracy of the sleep tracker is good. In the Samsung Health App it gives a breakdown of how many hours you slept, if you snoring it records your snoring and you can hear yourself snore. On \"smart thing\" samsung app it shows you where your watch is or you can go under the wear app and click on \"find my band\" and it'll take you right to the \"smart things\" app and it'll show you where your watch is located. Overall I'm satisfied with the watch. So many features. I changed my 5 star to 4 due to the misinformation on the battery life. Would I recommend it to someone that's looking for just a \"fitness\" app I definitely would. I how the review helps the next purchaser. 3/07/26 UPDATE: The charge on the battery last 6 days. I tracked how long the battery would last without notifications. I charged it last Sunday (afternoon) & this morning 3/07 it was at 3%. Another cool feature that I came across. The walking feature has a voice commentary that tells you how far you been walking, the length of time it comes on when you reach your 1st mile in thought that was cool that it actually talks to you."
    },
    {
        "review": "I am really impressed with how user-friendly the SAMSUNG Galaxy Fit 3 is. The 1.6\" AMOLED display is incredibly vibrant, clear, and easy to read at a glance, showing all my essentials like the time, date, and activity stats perfectly.The setup process was a breeze—the companion app is very easy to install, and navigating through it is smooth and intuitive. I love how hassle-free it is to scroll through my health metrics.Two of my favorite features so far are the outstanding battery life—it easily keeps up with me without constant recharging—and the sleep tracking. It has been incredibly helpful and insightful to check my sleep quality each morning. It’s a fantastic, straightforward device that delivers exactly what it promises! I bought Meliya 2-Pack for Samsung Galaxy Fit 3 Screen Protector Case for only $5.99"
    },
    {
        "review": "I change to this one from fitvii and I don't regret it. The price is very affordable and has a great quality. Even the band feels more expensive that it looks on photos. You can customize the tiles on the face. It pairs beautifully with the wear app on my Samsung phone. It doesn't take calls, but gets notifications, which is good when you are working and dont want to be interrupted. It has all main health tracker functions. I was specially interested on the sleep tracker. And it looks nice and fit for my small size wrist. Great find!"
    },
    {
        "review": "My wife and I have been fitbit fans since the Charge 2 (2016).  Before the google purchase, fitbit was a strong leader in lower cost fitness trackers.  It did concern us that our fitbits would die frequently (nearly each one we had died within a year from the Charge 2, 3, 4). However after talking with customer service they would send us replacement.  So, ok, we can deal with that.  (Even though in the past few years you had to provide a ton of documentation including, IIRC, your drivers license.)I had a fitbit 6 that I got for Christmas 2024.  Wow, a color screen.  LOL.  I needed a replacement as the screen on my Charge 5 kept getting dimmer, and dimmer, to the point when I could not read it outdoors and barely indoors.  So I was gifted the Charge 6 and it was big step up.  But it took me a few months until I realized that the floors option was gone, not just not working, but removed, to make space for other features I could care less about.So I looked around in late June and discovered the Fit 3.  So I bought one.  I am SO GLAD I did.  What do I need?  Step counter, heart rate, floors, distance, calories.  Oh, and the kicker for me was AIRPLANE MODE.  I study various energetic sciences including BioGeometry and the EMF coming from our devices is not benign.  I don't wear a tracker to sleep, don't want anything live, and keep my blue tooth turned off unless I am actively syncing.This is what I see comparing Fit 3 to Fitbit Charge 6.   What the Fit 3 has as an advantage:  Larger screen, airplane mode, beautiful faces, much cleaner UI on the watch and in the android app(s) (I am using this with a Pixel 7 phone).  The ability to sync only turning on bluetooth.  A few years ago fitbit/google basically said we aren't syncing your fitbit unless you have both location/GPS and bluetooth turned on.  Sometimes you could get the fitbit to sync with location off but this was tricky/spotty.What advantages did the Charge 6 have for me, personally.   None really.  It cost about 3x the amount for the Charge 6 compared to the Fit 3.  That's ridiculous.In a head to head comparsion of measurements this is what I see.   Fitbit Charge is often slower to start your step counts and the Fit 3 is quicker on that.  So you steps counts are going to go up and perhaps the Fit 3 does count some arm motions as steps when it should not.  But the Fit 3 is still very close (within 1-3%) with the Charge 6.  The Fit 3 tends to undercount calories compared to the Charge 6, I can't tell which is more accurate.  On the Charge 6 bluetooth syncing was always a laborious pain taking sometimes minutes to get the past few days of data and often having to turn bluetooth off/on and/or restarts to get it to sync.  The Fit 3 is very fast *most* of the time.  I did have one instance where the Fit 3 was not syncing in the Health App and I had to open the Wearable app and trigger a sync from there, which worked.The big thing is battery life.  I have automatic activity tracking turned on and except when syncing in the evening I keep it on airplane mode.  I charged it overnight last Thursday/Friday before I first put it on.  It's now Wednesday evening, nearing 6 complete days of use and my battery level is at 55%.  That frugal energy use is really good and is on par with the earlier monochrome fitbits.I am very pleased with my Fit 3 purchase.  Back in the early to mid 2000's I was very excited about Timex was doing just in digital watches (Iron Man, etc).  The Fit 3 harkens back to those kind of days for me when you could get a very full functionality wrist device that looked good, worked great, and was a good deal.  This is definitely worth a close look if you don't need an higher priced fitness/communication device that is emitting EMFs into your pericardium meridian 24x7.  Well done."
    },
    {
        "review": "I've had a long line of Fit Bits and when I found out Samsung has a non-smart watch tracker, I decided to give it a chance. (I don't want to pay smart watch prices.)Out of the box, the Fit 3 was in Spanish, but when I put it on the charger, it reverted to English. It stayed English until I got a new phone and had to reset the tracker in order to pair it with the new phone. The tracker reverted to Spanish and putting it on the charger did not change that this time. I pressed the top option on the device's screen and got to choose from a whole list of languages. I chose English, but this is where someone who wants another language will find it.The charge on this item is supposed to last up to 13 days. I've had this device for 3 months, and I got 6 days out of each charge (7 if I let the device die) for the first 2 months or so. Now, it last 4-5 days. I don't have the display always on and I don't consider my use of this device heavy by any means. 6 days is what I got out of my most recent Fitbit.My Fit 3 charges from 20% to 70% in about 20 minutes and to 100% in about an hour. The trick is to get the charger properly seated, because the first couple of times, I don't think it was on correctly, as I got the main display, not the charging percentage display, and it took about 2 hours to reach 100%.I wore the new Fit 3 side by side with my old Fitbit Charge 6 for 24+ hours to compare - 2 nights and one active day. The first night, the Fit 3 recorded 20 more minutes of sleep and the second night, the Fitbit recorded 20 more minutes. I don't know how either determines when I am asleep and not quietly reading, so I figure the results are comparable.The Fit 3, while behind on step count early in the day, ended the day with almost 1,500 more steps than the Fitbit. Both counted activity (cleaning, for instance), not just pure steps. I'm OK with this - I figure any movement is better than no movement.There are many more features that I don't use. I may in the future, but I'm happy with what I do use. I tried taking photos on my phone (also a Samsung) with my Fit 3 as a remote shutter button. I like that feature!Text notifications come to my Fit 3, but not all of them. When I am using my phone, notifications do not go to the tracker (I have found that this is the default and I can change it if desired). Group texts never do (they will go to my car, so I don’t know why they won't go to my Fit 3), and random regular texts don't. I found in the Health app where I can pick and choose which apps will send notifications.All told, I like this device. It’s not perfect, though. I miss the feature that Fitbit has that reminds me to move each hour. I'm now forced to choose between step count, floor count (I assume that means how many stairs I climb), or active hours. I like counting steps, so that's my main goal. I like seeing the hearts light up throughout the day as I get closer to my goals. I also have active minutes and active calories burned as goals, but I don’t care as much about those."
    },
    {
        "review": "Excellent smart watch to pair with your Samsung phone. Many functions such as monitoring sleep, heartbeat, stress etc."
    },
    {
        "review": "I picked up the pink one and it looks absolutely gorgeous on the wrist. The colour is a soft, subtle pink — not loud or plasticky at all — and the slim band sits really comfortably without feeling bulky. I've worn fitness trackers before that felt like a brick on my arm, but this one is so lightweight I genuinely forget I'm wearing it sometimes.The screen is bright and clear, easy to read even in sunlight, and swiping through the menus feels smooth and responsive. Setting it up with my phone was quick and straightforward — no technical headaches, just paired it and it was ready to go.For day-to-day tracking it covers everything I need — steps, heart rate, sleep, calories, stress monitoring. The sleep tracking in particular is really detailed and I've found myself actually paying attention to it and adjusting my routine. The battery life has also genuinely impressed me. I charge it maybe once a week which is so convenient compared to smartwatches that need charging every day or two.The band is comfortable enough to sleep in and the clasp is secure so I never worry about it coming loose during workouts. It handles sweat well too and I've worn it through some pretty intense sessions with no issues.For the price, this thing punches way above its weight. It's not trying to be a full smartwatch and it doesn't need to be — it does its job brilliantly. The pink colour just makes it even better. Highly recommend, especially if you want something stylish, lightweight and reliable without breaking the bank."
    },
    {
        "review": "Excellent battery life"
    },
    {
        "review": "Difficulty with the connection"
    },
    {
        "review": "So far so good not worn for very long yet so may change to 5 star after more use"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "I absolutely love my new smartwatch! From the moment I started using it, I was impressed with how easy it was to set up and navigate. The watch has a sleek, attractive design and feels comfortable on my wrist.I especially like being able to keep up with my notifications, track my activity, monitor my health and stay connected without constantly reaching for my phone. The features are easy to use, and the display is clear and bright.For the price, I feel like I got a great value. It looks good, works well, and has many of the features I was looking for in a smartwatch. I’m very pleased with my purchase and would definitely recommend it to anyone looking for an affordable, reliable smartwatch!"
    },
    {
        "review": "UPDATE after 2 weeks of owning this Smart Watch for Women: WOW! It's been a week and a half since I last charged this smart watch, and it is only down to 16%! The battery in this watch is AMAZING! SO much better than the Versa2 I had before![Earlier] So far, I am very impressed with this smart watch! (I previously had a Versa 2 and after about a year the battery barely lasted 2 days, which became very annoying, and then it just stopped charging at all)This Smart Watch for Women is VERY user-friendly. Its time, date, timezone, heart rate, steps etc all work extremely well. And it does much more than my Versa 2 ever did. This smart watch also receives AND sends calls, and did it seamlessly! I haven't yet used the text function to send, however, it has received all my texts and alerted me.It works well with the app, which was easy to download and set up. The BluTooth works well also. I'm also happy that this watch is waterproof (up to a certain depth) because I will be paddleboarding this summer, and swimming.The watch came with two bands from which to choose, which is nice. And it came with its own charging cord, proprietary at one end, but USB on the other. It seems to charge quickly. I would definitely recommend the Smart Watch for Women, especially for those who want  a lot of the functions of an Apple Watch, but don't want to pay that exorbitant price! This watch is SO much more reasonable, AND it looks very nice on."
    },
    {
        "review": "So realistically, I have gone through 4 (yes this is my 4th) smart watch this year (I think?! Might be 3 but anyways) and thos one by far has FINALLY given me everything I've been looking for. Well, almost everything. Still cant text out on it but, it is what it is.So, some interesting things about this watch compared to the others ive had are the accuracy of the biometrics taken, the screen quality, and overall battery life. I am even liking that when I misplace my phone is actually rings MY ringtone and not some weird built-in one from the watch company. I even love that you can change the notification tone ON the watch itself too. Pretty snazzy there.Now here's some downfall in my opinion (this is my personal opinion, use its information as you see fit). Firstly, the paid membership app im not thrilled about and its not for the price, thats fair across the board. Its the lack of knowing the time frame parameters used as well as this being noted in the listing itself. Another is, the lack of it being fully customizable (i.e. you make it your very own) backgrounds as well as the limited number you can have (4 is the max). But again, this is my opinion and im one who likes fully customizable things as I like to be different. Only other \"flaw\" is that it only has 1 button which sticks out a fair ways for my liking and digs into my wrist... Do wish it was a little more, sleek if you will in that department.But ive added photos to show the level of things offered as well as how it does look very asteticly pleaseing. Which is nice little bonus! So maybe im nitpicking on some things, oh well!All in all though, its beyond worth getting. Not had a connection issue like past ones, and the band it even came with wasnt just silicone (oddly wearing that causes my skin to \"eat\" itself so I cant wear them).So I hope this helps someone out there!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Had this watch for a couple of weeks now. This watch is not bad, it is also not the greatest. I don’t think it accurately measures steps and heart rate is hit and miss, seems like it depends on how you hold your wrist or how tight you wear the watch. The phone locater feature doesn’t always work either, and you can’t customize the watch face with your own photo or answer text messages. That being said, the workout features that track distance and speed, etc. are great. The sleep tracker works well and just about all the other watch features are pretty cool. I do like that you can answer your phone and talk through the watch because there’s a microphone which has the ability to connect to Siri. All in all I would say 3.5 stars. Oh and the charge does holds well, just like with anything though it depends on how much you play with it.Update: After writing this review I was contacted by Nora from the company’s customer service team and offered either a replacement or refund of the watch that I purchased. It was super unexpected but highly appreciated, and gave an undoubting sense that this small company cares about their products and the reception they receive. The interaction and level of customer service alone is enough to give the watch a higher rating, as it’s obvious the company cares and that’s more than most places these days. I would overall recommend"
    },
    {
        "review": "I absolutely love this watch! It looks beautiful, has so many useful features, and it’s been working great for me. The battery life is good, it’s easy to use, and the customization options are amazing.I also have to disagree with one of the 1-star reviews claiming that you can’t use your own photos as the watch face. That’s simply not true. I was able to set a personal photo with no issues at all. Maybe they didn’t know how to use the app or set it up correctly.Overall, this watch exceeded my expectations. It’s stylish, functional, and offers a lot for the price. I highly recommend it!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Was pretty easy to set up with the app, it came with pretty clear instructions. If you read some of the other reviews it's helpful as well to get it set up. It does a lot more than I thought it would! I just wanted something that would count my steps and my heart rate and this does a million more things than just that. Blood oxygen levels and stress monitoring? Excellent! I can even listen to music and make phone calls on this watch! It works on my Android just fine and even lets me connect my Bluetooth headphones at the same time the watch is also connected with Bluetooth."
    },
    {
        "review": "I've come to really like this rose gold smartwatch that I bought as a replacement for a Fitbit Charge 4. I have narrow wrists so it's big in comparison but as said, I'm getting used to it. I love being able to read it at a glance without squinting. The screen is sharp, crisp, gorgeous color and easy to read.Set up was initially easy but when I started using the watch, customizing screens, layout of icons and grant it the permissions it needs, I had to turn to googling \"IDW28\" and found most of the answers i needed. I am fairly tech savvy and after a lot of frustration, I could only solve my issues this way. (The companion app was no help.) The biggest problem was the watch would compete with my ear buds, phone or bedside speaker to take over Bluetooth control, and attempt to direct the audio to the watch. Audio would cut in and out as it repeatedly tried to take control. I finally figured out what was wrong when I realized a phone call I received was switched to my watch. I had a Dick Tracy watch! 😂 Lol and the audio is not nearly loud enough to make calls on our listen to music.Overall the watch with its phone app is exceptional to monitor steps, heart rate, sleep patterns, exercise sessions, stress level, water intake, weather and control your phone's music app, camera and much more. It also alerts you to notifications of messages and emails. Essentially it's the same notification that your phone displays (not the whole email or text) and you cannot respond. It does not measure blood pressure as some reviews suggest. It will connect to your AI asst but I don't need that.I do wish for a way to enter water intake in the watch but for now that has to be done on the phone app.It charges fast and is easy to hook up. The battery lasts about a week but you're mileage may vary depending on your settings.I would definitely recommend buying if you are looking for an easy to read screen that is a giant step up from something like a Fitbit. If you want to send messages or emails, respond to them, see images attached in same then look to spend 5-10 times the price. Me? I'm happy with the purchase. I don't need to make calls on a watch or compose replies to texts and emails on my wrist. I really hope they solve the problem competing for Bluetooth as I'd like to turn on some of the features I had to give up to solve the problem."
    },
    {
        "review": "Hello there, future watch owners. If you're like me, you've spent wayyy too much time agonizing over which knockoff smart watch to buy, and have probably read every review ever written. So, here is a list of every observed helpful and unfortunate aspect of this quality timepiece. I've been using it about a month.Pros:It looks nice. The screen is large enough to read and navigate easily, without feeling like you have a tablet on your wrist. The responsiveness is decent and the picture is clear. The swipe-right shortcut saves your most recent selections.There are lots of options for what it can do. Workouts, music, phone, calculator, stress, sleep, O2, heart rate, water intake, and something I found especially helpful as someone who leaves their phone lying around, Find My Phone. I've used most of these but have barely scratched the surface on the sports/workouts.Notifications show up with a red dot rather than a large icon, which I like and are easy to read and clear.I really like being able to adjust the brightness on the screen, and having a shortcut for the wake gesture; it's easy to turn off at night - because it's bright enough to burn your retnas - and back on during the day. The flashlight function is very helpful and almost absurdly bright. The alarm works well.The battery has been fantastic. I probably have less notifications set up for social media and email than some, but it easily lasts me a week+, which is way more than my poor, retired 4 year old Versa Lite ever did.Cons:The app sucks. It's  like they tracked daily notifications with \"app unpaired from device\" and forgot to fix it. I am re-pairing every couple of days. It links up quickly but I wish it would stay linked.There are no screen protectors available that I've found and as a result, there is already a scratch on the face. There is also a chip off the wrist sensor already, which makes me wonder about the longevity, and my skins safety.I wish that when you pressed the side button from a selection, it would take you back to the menu, rather than back to the home screen, but that's minor.I also haven't found replacement bands. I don't care for the cloth band but I like the silicone. Eventually it will wear out and I would like to be able to replace it.I haven't braved the water with it yet because I'm afraid of drowning it, so I can't speak to that.I think that for the price-point, it is worth it. I threw the extended warranty on it, so I'm basically covered for three years if something messes up and then I just get a new one.Rant over, enjoy your day 🙂 hope it helps!"
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a great budget smart watch for someone who is maybe interested in trying one and doesn't want to spend hundreds of pounds on a top branded one.This watch offers you a wide variety of apps and facilities that you would find on a top branded watchIt has lots of health apps such as heart rate, sleep, blood oxygen levels and stress levels.It also has a wide variety of fitness apps included which can help you begin your fitness journey with things like walking plans and running plans, or to continue in a journey with more advanced features.The display is large and clear,  colours of vibrant and it's a very sensitive screen allowing you to easily navigate your way through the various menus and settings.You will download an app indicated in the instructions, this will allow you to fully personalise your watch as well as store your data - obviously your watch has a limited memory and you will need to store data externally.It charges using a special magnetic USB cord which attaches to the back of the watch.Charging is fast.It's incredible how much you get in this watch for such a low price, it's fantastic for people on a budget or someone just wanting to test the water to see if a smart watch is for them."
    },
    {
        "review": "This smartwatch delivers far more than I expected at its price. It’s stylish and comfortable to wear, and the pink gold version looks particularly refined without feeling too flashy. The screen is clear and responsive, and basic navigation feels smooth.Bluetooth calling has worked well, and the battery life has easily lasted a full week with moderate use. I like the health features, especially the heart rate and sleep tracking, which seem reasonably accurate for general wellness. Notifications come through reliably, and I’ve found the hydration reminders and camera shutter function more useful than expected.The included extra strap is a nice touch, and the ability to swap bands with standard 22mm ones adds even more versatility. While I didn’t dive deep into the fitness modes, the range of options is impressive for anyone with an active routine.The app has room for improvement, especially in reducing clutter and simplifying customization, but it does what’s needed once set up. Overall, this is a very capable smartwatch that gets a lot right, especially for everyday tracking and light communication needs."
    },
    {
        "review": "I can’t seem to find anywhere to message seller direct. My watch won’t charge or turn on.I bought in December so I know it’s been 7 months, however it does say 5 year guarantee and to just message the seller if any issues, sad this isn’t made easy.Review update:  After writing this review the company kindly reached out to me and offered me a full refund or replacement watch. I requested the replacement and I am very happy I did, my new watch arrived within days. The watch was easy to set up and sync with my phone. It is working beautifully and after using for a few days I still have good battery life. Very different to my first experience so it must just have been a faulty one.The watch itself has some nice features like the weather app, steps monitor, BP monitor etc. It also sends me notifications when I get messages through my phone including Whatsapp.Thank you so mush for taking the time to resolve my issue and I look forward to using my watch with confidence."
    },
    {
        "review": "This feature-packed watch goes beyond just telling time, diligently tracking your steps and much more. Setting it up is a breeze, and personalizing the watch face to match your style is a real treat. With its stunning design and the comfortable Velcro band option, it not only looks fantastic but performs exceptionally well, rivaling watches at three times the price – making it an incredible value."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "This Galazy fit 3 is amazing with all the functions. I fell twice last year so I was thrilled that this has a fall detection on it and will call emergency help. Everything is really accurate.  It was super easy to set up. It comes with a couple instruction booklets. Plus it has steep by step instructions to follow on the screen. The battery lasts a long time when I turn down the display light. Great value for all you get at an affordable price.  I linked it to my health records.  My Dr recommended me getting one of these a long time ago and I'm glad I finally did. It is compatable with amazon music so I can put on my headphones and control volume from my wrist. I still have to have my phone on me. But that's not a big deal. It's nice to know how I'm sleeping too. I have narcolepsy and it heals me show my sleep specialist how I'm sleeping at night."
    },
    {
        "review": "I am honestly so impressed with this watch, especially for the price point. It does pretty much everything the more expensive \"big name\" smartwatches do, but without the high price tag.It has become an essential part of my daily routine. The activity tracking is spot on, and it’s been incredibly helpful for keeping me accountable and motivated to reach my goals. I love that it tracks my sleep and monitors my stress levels—that stress tracking is a huge plus for me.But my favorite part is how durable it is! It is completely waterproof—I wear it swimming in the pool, in the shower, while washing dishes, and even out in the rain, and it has never failed me once. Plus, it charges incredibly quickly, so I barely have any downtime with it. If you’re looking for a reliable, sleek feature-packed tracker that actually keeps up with your busy life, I highly recommend this one!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I'm replacing an old and now dead Fitbit that lasted far longer than most. It was always unreadable in sunlight. This Galaxy is perfect for me so 5 stars, but the heartrate is not accurate. Besides that, it's great! Light weight, comfortable wristband even playing golf or pickleball. Phone call and text notifications very handy and the phone finder has saved a lot of time searching. Large numerals, brightly lit and super easy to read in the brightest light. Claims to be water resistant but with some caveats so I remove it when water is involved. Sweat is no problem. Battery lasts about 5 days and recharges fast."
    },
    {
        "review": "This little watch does everything I need. I didn't want a \"phone on my wrist\" but wanted something light weight that tracked my steps, was a timer, was connected to my phone for messages. Thus does all this and more! It tracks my sleep better than my old Galaxy watch ever did. It's lightweight and comfortable to wear. I can see my messages, and control my audio on the watch. While I can't personalize the watch face, there are enough choices that I have the face I like. Perfect little watch for what I need. I do NOT need nor want AI on my watch (nor phone for that matter) and was happy to find one that does not offer it!I gave it four stars due to the faulty fall detection function. Twice it tried to call 911 just because I rolled over in bed. Then when I actually fell, it did nothing. I turned off the fall detection as it's useless.But other than that, it is a great little watch for those who want a simpler life!"
    },
    {
        "review": "This watch is 100% AMAZING!I recently left the Apple ecosystem and switched to the Samsung Galaxy S26 Ultra, and I absolutely LOVE it. I am NEVER going back to Apple.I've owned several top-of-the-line Apple Watches over the years, but when I really thought about it, I only ever used them for the heart rate monitor, telling time, and tracking my runs. I couldn't justify spending hundreds of dollars on another smartwatch.So I decided to give the Galaxy Fit3 a try, and I'm honestly blown away. When I ordered it, I thought it would just track my steps, heart rate, and the time and I was perfectly fine with that. Instead, I found a fitness tracker packed with features I wasn't expecting.If you have a Samsung phone, this is an absolute no-brainer. It pairs perfectly, does way more than I ever imagined, and for around $50, the value is unbelievable. Save your money and get this you won't regret it!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Wanted something simple to use, and this is just that. Set up was pretty easy. I couldn't get my notifications to work properly when I first set it up, but all I had to do was reboot it and had no issues after that. Stays charged for about 2 weeks.  Had a generic watch prior to this I that I had to connect to the app everyday so it would stay linked.... I dont have to do that with thus one. I think its great and especially at this price!!!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Great for heart rate and sleep tracking. If you don't want to get a full blown smart watch get this one. The other features are decent. For the 45ish bucks I am quite happy with it. Step counter seems to register +10% higher than actual. Battery life is 3-4 days. Charging is super fast and easy."
    },
    {
        "review": "The product works great. No subscription to use. The only issue I had was that Samsung takes 2 weeks to switch your phone number over for 2 step verification. So for about a week I was unable to even start using the device. I said screw it and made a new account.The band is to small for my large wrist, but it was easy to replace with a better band. Not sure why all watch manufacturers do that..Anyway tech side, you get what you need. Stress, heart rate, sleep, step, food, calorie, period tracker all in one. You can also connect with your doctor on there, but I have not tried that function yet.It's worth the price and I even got my wife one."
    },
    {
        "review": "At this price? This is 80% of a full smartwatch and a fraction of the cost. I'm extremely happy with it as a fitness tracker. Can control my music playback, works as general purpose HR tracker. Very light and comfortable with excellent battery life, goes for many days. (Since I don't sleep with it, I stretch it to weeks)What it lacks from a full smartwatch? Payments (I don't need), answering calls plus some fancier health tracking like ECG or VO2max. But for the price, who cares? I wear it and don't worry about it. I would prefer I could set the watch without phone and also use standard watch straps, but hey the price!The Samsung app is one of the best for general health tracking. Best by far for nutrition tracking. Serious fitness enthusiast may find it a bit lacking. Shame the watch doesn't work with iPhone.This is a highly recommended device unless you are CERTAIN you need all the fancy features of a Galaxy or Pixel."
    },
    {
        "review": "Why to purchase expensive health trackers while this one does the basics.Excellent sleep tracking along with the snoring if wanted.Excellent activity and steps tracking.Good batter life that stays for aprx up to 14 days depending on your usage.Light and easy to wearThe only negative thing it has a screen, Samsung should issue fit4 without a screen"
    },
    {
        "review": "The battery of this lasted me 5 days with always on display on and without turning on the sleep mode at night. i also kept my phone connected all that time.. So maybe the 13days claim isn't right. but 5 days seems like a decent enough time.Connectivity is really great with the phone. I got notifications in real time.The band is of great quality. there is no itching or anything. Accurate workout calculator. Steps counter is 90 percent great i think. I would say there is an error of around 500 steps give or take during the day. That's not much of a problem according to me. Sleep tracking is really really good. Loving being able to track my sleep so accurately.I love this watch overall. Looks great on my wrist . The dial isn't too big. Doesn't feel heavy and clunky."
    },
    {
        "review": "Not bad, battery lasts about a week"
    },
    {
        "review": "High quality, more than two years old and in excellent condition"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "I've had Garmins and Fitbits only in the past and this is just a huge, massive upgrade. Love the customizations that can be done with the watch too with the cool looking bands and faces that I bought separately on Amazon. I've had it for a year without any screen protectors and it's been durable enough that it's held up well with very little wear and tear on it, but I'm wise enough to use protectors for it now. My only complaint is the battery. It's a bit disappointing that the battery barely lasts a day. I've had to buy 3 chargers for it. One charger for my car, one for work, and one for home because this thing dies fast if you plan to work out with it and use it all day. Still, I love this watch."
    },
    {
        "review": "I have had a Samsung Galaxy watch since they first came out. My first one is still going strong, but I needed one that would track my activity in the water, since the majority of my activity is water related. This watch is great for that! It has been in the water with me now almost daily for weeks and works like a charm! I have an app that will automatically set the watch to the water mode, or I can set it myself. It counts each step, stroke and workout moment - and seems to do so accurately. The size is a nice upgrade from the earlier versions, being slightly easier to read. All my apps and information were easily transferred to this new watch, making the transition seamless. It doesn't vibrate quite as strongly as my last one did, so I miss a notification here and there, but since my phone is never more than a few feet away from me, that really isn't an issue. Time will tell if it lasts as long as my first watch did, but I am really so impressed with this watch. To me, it is worth spending the cash to have the added assurance of counting my water activities."
    },
    {
        "review": "I'm normally a regular watch wearing person but have been contemplating on getting either an apple watch or samsung to switch things up, I finally switched from apple products to Samsung and decided to go all out with the samsung tech and I couldn't be happier. I love the size , the appearance,  battery life is good. I literally have no complaints. I love how sleek and elegant it looks regardless what band you have. I also love all the features it has. Calling, GPS, wifi ,accuracy , notifications and more. Super happy"
    },
    {
        "review": "I ordered this watch because my fitbit suddenly stopped working. I honestly was just going to get another fitbit but decided to look around at other options for smart watches. I primarily used my previous watch for tracking my steps, sleep, and golf play time, but the selling point to this watch was the additional apps that I get access to. I use 18 Birdies when I golf and this watch is compatible with that app. I haven't used it yet since I just got it last week, plus it's been raining every day, but I can't wait to test it out. I also go to the gym and listen to Spotify when I'm working out. I can connect my headphones to my watch and listen to music. I'm still learning so much about this watch but so far I love it. I did have to turn off the AOD after I got it because it was draining my battery faster than I wanted it to. The reason I dropped a star is because the battery life is decent but not great. I have to charge it daily, but I do like that when it's fully charged I get an alert on my Samsung phone to prevent overcharging. I'm a very petite woman and ordered the 40mm. I was afraid this might be too big for my tiny wrist but it really isn't. I honestly like the size and don't think it's too big at all. Overall, I'm really liking the watch and would highly recommend it to anyone who may be on the fence about getting one."
    },
    {
        "review": "Overall I'm happy with this watch that seems like they're phasing out some features for this particular device. But it tracks my heart rate throughout the day among other useful features. If you're wanting some of Samsung's more advanced features you should probably just go with the newer watch. Capable of Samsung wallet meal tracking water intake. Definitely get yourself screen covers and a case especially if you work Outdoors.Messaging it's kind of tedious using this I mainly just use it to look at incoming messages at a glance. Call qualities decent but the volume is kind of lacking in my opinion. As far as battery life goes I find myself charging in the morning and evening you just charging the evening but the battery get pretty low as I reach the end of my day and sometimes with die before I get home . Battery takes between 30 minutes to an hour to charge depending on how low you let it get. I'd wait for this one to go on sale if you're going to pay full price I'd get the newer versions."
    },
    {
        "review": "Watch is nicely designed and easy to use. The apps are great and the set up is seamless with the phone. Audio seems good. I wish there was a smaller watch face but overall its a great investment."
    },
    {
        "review": "BOTTOM LINE:  if you don't need the 3 Labs - Advanced Glycation End (AGE) product index, the  Antioxidant index (does not work anyway), or the Vascular Load, ***Go With the WATCH 7!***  If you do get a watch 8, return it to Amazon as soon as it screws up!Watch 8 #2 is now \"inching up\" on its O2 measurements.  At first, it was 2% points over my Innovo FDA-Approved O2 device.  That's okay, because as long as it was consistent, I could deal with it.  Now, it is 3% over my Innovo -- My Watch 8 #1 and Watch 7 did the same.  I hope this is not a harbinger of failure.  It also must be tightened down painfully snug at night.Oct 14, 2025.  Bought new watch 8, now Watch 8 #2I am riddled with inflammation, so pursue antioxidants.  I take 3 supplements twice a day.  Yet the Antioxidant index stays low.  Farce.******************************  Below is the Watch 8 #1Oct 10, 2025 - Samsung refunded costSent it in a second time.  Kept checking (two weeks) and nothing.  Finally, Samsung offered a refund.  Not for the tax, of course.  Awaiting refund.Sep 26, 2025  Already Failing with Same ErrorsWas on the phone forever with Samsung, being fobbed off on every department imaginable.  I bought a Brand-New Watch for $350, and they \"repaired it\" only to have the same problems!I am so mad I could chew a horseshoe and spit nails.  I wish I'd never bought this thing!  If you are stupid enough to buy it, return it to Amazon at the first fail!  It is not YOU!  The sensor array is P-poor and it fails.  I miss my Watch 4.Sep 25, 2025  Samsung repaired the watch under warranty.  It wiped out all trending in the app.\"New\" watch still has \"blanks\" in its monitoring (picture).  Samsung says, \"wear snugly\".  It is already so snug it hurts (picture).  Furthermore, the \"O2\" monitoring is 5% MORE than my commercial O2 monitor.  I guess I have to go back to wearing five different monitors that are correct.Sep 15, 2025.  The watch has become worse.  I will contact Samsung under the warranty.Anytime I get sweaty, the watch stops tracking on the wrist!  I'm skinny - BMI 20.2.  It's a fitness watch, for God's Sake!  One expects it to work when you are sweaty!  But no - it stops tracking the heart rate and the oxygen level.  This is not at all what one wants in a Fitness tracker!Aug 31, 2025.  The watch has developed issues with tracking heart rate.I was walking - spanking pace - and the watch stopped tracking my heart rate.  I stopped for a directed measure.  It said \"put your watch on\".  I took the watch off and cleaned it, and had same result.  I finally rebooted the watch and it worked again.  I DID NOT have this problem with the Watch 4.Aug 5th, 2025.  Anti-oxidant scan doesn't work right.The scan kept saying low - eat a carrot.  Three days of carrots and it is still low.Also, I tried to take another scan and the software kept saying \"Put your watch back on.\" - but the watch WAS on.  Too many bugs.Aug 2nd, 2025  My initial review wasn't accepted; I am unsure why.I hemmed and hawed about Watch7 and Watch8.  I went for this Watch 8 due to features.  I am very disappointed.First, I have many Samsung-branded chargers.  I was very disappointed that the Watch8 band would not allow the Watch 8 to work on the flat chargers.  I guess you could put a weight on the watch, like 2 lbs (!) to press it down, but I believe Samsung should have designed their Watch8 to be compatible with its supporting chargers.  See first pic.Second, this is a 40mm watch.  I compare it to the Watch4 and the Watch 7, the only others with which I have familiarity.  The Watch8 face measures 40mm, but the display is smaller by 2mm, making it a 38mm display.I cannot at this time comment on durability as I have had it three days.  Color is personal choice.  EDIT - Watch began failing before 30 days!  I thought it was me.Functions.  The Watch8 is extraordinarily bloated by location-tracking bloatware.  I do not do use email, bixby, wallet, etc. from the watch, but it will not allow me to uninstall these apps.  Furthermore, every app tracks your location with precision!  I have no idea why outlook would need location, for example.The Watch8 has the same processor as the Watch7.  Comparing it with the Watch4, I do not see any difference in performance.  I would have expected better.The new Health features are AGE - Advanced Glycation End products.  Not sure what the low - adequate - high means, and there isn't a lot of information on the web.  It also does the antioxidant index - very low - low - adequate.  I tried the recommendation --- eat carrots.  My value did not change.  Lastly, is vascular load.  It is supposed to an indication of lifestyle factors affect on your health.  Basically, get sleep, exercise, and good diet. Shrug.The Watch8 is supposed to have a better battery.  However, I do not see the Watch8 lasting any longer than my Watch4.  I still charge the Watch8 at my noon-ish nap time, and see the Watch 8 is down to 66% at noon, and again down to 66% at night, after having been charged at noon.  If I could uninstall some of the not-used apps, it may be better, and maybe not.All in all, after seeing all the additional tracking and inability to turn it off, the questionable value of the new \"features\", the failure to be compatible with industry and brand standard equipment, I wish I'd gone with the Watch7."
    },
