[
    {
        "review": "Best purchase I made all year. I started running recently, training for a marathon at the end of the year. I used to spend my time trying to take my run time and miles to try and calculate my pace. Now I just look at my watch and all the metrics are right there for me. It can take all of my runs and predict my race times. I no longer have to guess how many miles I put in for my run or for the entire week. It gives you heart rate, HRV, VO2 Max, step counts, sleep scores and suggested training plans. Battery life is good. If you’re running a lot and using the GPS, the battery will drain faster. I tend to charge after my long run.If you’re in training, and looking for a good sports watch, consider this one."
    },
    {
        "review": "Ok so I purchased this Garmin 165 watch for just $200.  So the price was excellent.  It has a nice bright amoled screen and has a good build quality. However, I only use this watch as a fitness, wellness and running watch.  That is, I turned off the notifications.  I don't see the point of trying to read a notification or message on a watch face when my phone is right next to me. So with notifications turned off I got 13 days of battery life before the watch went down to 9%. I also do not have on the \"always on display\".  With this set up you can get 13 days of regular (non smart watch) use.  This is what I wanted the most, a descent battery.  So having to just charge the watch every 2 weeks or twice a month is really great.  I also wore the watch every day and did walking workouts.  One thing to note :  I DID NOT SLEEP WITH THE WAATCH ON... I do not know if that helped the battery life but I generally do not sleep with any watched or rings or anything restrictive when I sleep.. I need comfort... lol..  But I did sleep one night with it and it was comfortable.. just not my thing... but great watch, connected to GPS is seconds, gives good info in the app and is comfortable.  Highly recommend for someone who is into fitness... it can track most activities like running, walking, swimming, hiking, trail run, in door run etc.. most general activities....  look... there is really no need to go out an buy a $500 or $900 watch.. this one does the job at $200..  no point getting a watch that gives you tons of stats that you will not use or understand...  if you do triathlons or want those pretty maps on the watch then by all means get the more expensive watch but if you just want to keep healthy and run.. then this watch is prefect.."
    },
    {
        "review": "As a half marathon runner training for a full marathon, I wanted this watch to be my full time training partner (and bully at times..lol). I notice a large improvement from my previous watch (apple watch series 10 I believe). It has great tracking for mileage, pace, HR, and a more accurate Vo2max. The sleep metrics are detailed well, I just wish the alarms were louder. The set up was very easy to complete, just create a Garmin account, sync your watch, and boom, you’re in. The battery life is insane, up to a week of battery for me (depending on usage) and very fast to charge. I like that the bands are removable to make it easier to clean the watch after getting sweaty or dirty. The watch itself is clean looking, I love the black color with the grey band.However, it was a little confusing for the first few days of me having it, trying to learn how to use it, navigate it, and get the hang of where everything is. The manual doesn’t help much 😅😅.Over all, best (impulsive) purchase I think I have made so far! I don’t regret it and look forward to continue training with the Forerunner series! 💪💪"
    },
    {
        "review": "I’m a mediocre runner and cyclist who’s recently gotten into longer, timed hiking events. I upgraded from the basic Garmin Forerunner 35 for better tracking and more fitness options, and so far, I’m really happy with it.It’s a little overwhelming at first with all the features and settings, but it does exactly what I need it to. Coming from the 35, the battery life on the 165 isn’t quite what I expected -- but let’s be honest, the 35 was basically the Nokia Brick of GPS watches, so my expectations were probably unrealistic. To that end, it does charge really fast (like, an hour or less.)I am a smaller female. I stuck it out with the 35 for so long as I don't want some huge watch eating my wrist. While the 165 is maybe a little larger than I'd like, it's not gawdy or uncomfortable.If you’re willing to spend a bit of time learning the menus, this is a solid, reliable watch that tracks workouts well and gives you plenty of data to geek out on (I pair with with Garmin Connect). I apprehensively purchased the one with the white strap, but so far, so good. It still looks as new as the day I first put it on, and it's already taken a beating!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I’ve been running for over 20 years and have only ever used by Apple Watch and before that my timex iron man running watch. Getting a dedicated running watch like this is a game changer. So many more metrics and options to use while running and to review after running has really made me a better runner. I’ve had this watch about 8 months now and have had no issues at all. Heart rate functions great for all day monitoring. I have used a chest strap for a long time for activity monitoring. Even w this watch chest strap heart rate monitoring is the only way to go for accuracy. Wrist based monitors are ok, but if your training and trying to hit certain heart rate zones I would to recommend a chest strap. W that said. Compared to my Apple Watch this wrist based monitor is better. It locks on faster and seems to be pretty accurate. Screen is easy to see in bright sunlight and inside as well.  U get access to free training plans within the Garmin app which is easyto use but looks a bit outdated. W this watch you don’t get training load or training status or readiness like the more expensive Garmin watches but u do get training effect for each workout for aerobic and anaerobic metrics. I use iPhone and have the watch set to notify for calls and text only and the rest of my phone notifications are send silently to my notification widget on the watch which I love. Helps minimize distractions. The watch stays connected very good to my iPhone 14 and always syncs w the Garmin app. The strap the comes w it is comfortable but I did switch it out for a nylon strap which I feel is better and lighter. I would highly recommend this watch for its functionality, metrics and price when u can find it on sale. Overall Garmin products are over priced. But having a worry free experience when paired w an iPhone and accurate training metrics is worth it for this particular device."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is my very first smartwatch, and I am absolutely blown away by it! The colorful AMOLED display is incredibly bright, clear, and easy to read. Since it’s my first time using a smartwatch, I am still learning all the features, and it feels like there is a huge variety of functions to explore.One of the best things about this watch is how lightweight it is. It is so light that when I am wearing it, I barely even notice it's on my wrist!Another big difference from traditional watches is the rubber (silicone) strap."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a nice watch with very nice features.  Was a great price for this kind of watch and has enough features at this price range, but cannot compare with the other watches that cost more. But this does what I need it to do. It's small enough and light enough for a small wrist as well as a bigger wrist.  Using the app, you can made the readout bigger if needed.  Numbers track very well, similar with the Well known watch that costs more, as compared on their app with garmins. It is comfortable to sleep with and the battery life is great! Overall, a great watch that is really accurate."
    },
    {
        "review": "It seems like a good watch and can do all the things for health/fitness/recovery metrics that Garmin is so well known for. The band was a little skinny for my liking (kinda looked like a “girls” watch if I’m allowed to say that). And perhaps it was a learning curve, but it just didn’t feel very user friendly. Maybe hardcore fitness people aren’t as concerned with changing songs (or maybe they don’t listen to music at all) and doing “smart watch” things. If you want to track fitness metrics, this is great. If you want a smart watch, this probably isn’t the one for you."
    },
    {
        "review": "Excellent! Does what it promises! It was a gift and the person loved it!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I have been using a G-Shock GBX-100 for a couple of years now to track my runs but I also wanted to track my heart rate at the same time.  This watch ticks all the boxes for me.- track steps- accurate distance (GPS)- track heart rate during runsbonus for me- running courses- coaching- resting recommendations- electronic wallet payment via Revolut (the only credit card supported in singapore)- basic navigation ie: direction/distance to a saved location- sync's with Strava and SG's Healthy 365 app- really bright visible day/night- uses standard quick release 20mm straps- charges very quickly via any USB C power source- lots more features as various apps can be downloaded into the watch, eg: compass, mapsCharging is via a USB C cable that pokes into some exposed contacts at the back of the watch.  The contacts being exposed to sweat/grime, I don't know how corrosion resistant those are. I had a \"near\" failure where it charged intermittently but fortunately it was resolved by cleaning the contacts."
    },
    {
        "review": "I’ve been using the Garmin Forerunner 165 for a few weeks now, and it’s honestly one of the best purchases I’ve made for running and daily tracking. The bright touchscreen is gorgeous—super vibrant and easy to read even in direct sunlight, which makes checking stats mid-run a breeze.GPS is spot-on for pace and distance, wrist-based heart rate feels accurate, and I love the adaptive training suggestions and recovery insights that help me avoid overdoing it. Battery life is solid too—easily lasts a full week with regular runs, and up to 11 days in smartwatch mode.Lightweight, comfortable to wear all day, and packed with way more features than I expected at this price point. If you’re getting into running or want a reliable Garmin without breaking the bank, this is it. Highly recommend!"
    },
    {
        "review": "First impressions: Minimalism in its purest formThe Garmin Forerunner 165 catches the eye if you are a fan of simplicity and functionality. It is not the typical watch with premium finishes, but it does convey lightness and a design without extra things. If you're looking for something minimalist to accompany your day, go for a run or wear it all day without it bothering you or singing. Run without distractions! This watch is the definition of “put it on and go.” If running is your thing (whether for a hobby, to train seriously or simply because it helps you breathe better), the Forerunner 165 leaves aside the unnecessary extras and concentrates on what is most important: measuring your distance, pace, heart rate well and giving you useful data while you run or walk. The GPS engages quickly and the tracking is accurate, with almost no margin of error as one would expect from a Garmin. Health and motivation at the touchDo you not mind counting steps? It's okay, the clock does the same. Do you like monitoring your sleep or your stress levels? You can also do it with this model, although don't expect a thousand premium functions or mega-advanced data. It's perfect for those who want the basics, but well made and easy to understand, without a thousand menus or complications. Autonomy and extras The battery is another highlight: it easily lasts a week without drama, even training for several days. Unlike more expensive ranges, here you do not have wireless payment or response to messages, but you do have basic notifications so that you do not miss anything important while you run. Who is it for? In short, the Forerunner 165 is ideal if you are looking for a watch to start running or you simply want a simple and reliable one, without spending a fortune or fighting with functions that you will never use. It is always to the point, it is comfortable and discreet, and it does the essentials to motivate you every day to go out for a run or move more, especially if you are one of those who enjoys things without excess complications."
    },
    {
        "review": "The watch is absurdly good! Lots of features and app integration. I use it for swimming and the indicators are great! For sleeping, the indicators are extremely accurate. Great entry-level Garmin. It has an advanced operating system and simpler hardware, which makes it a great value for money!"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "I purchased the Xiaomi Mi Smart Band 10 after comparing it with several other fitness trackers, and the value for the price was hard to beat. After watching multiple YouTube reviews, I felt confident it was the right choice.The Smart Band 10 is simple, clean, and not overly flashy. The setup process was straightforward, though you do need to pair it with the Mi Fitness app, which requires creating an account. Once set up, the device tracks heart rate, sleep, steps, and provides handy notifications like incoming text messages. It covers all the essential features most people need.The Smart Band is pretty light weight and sometimes I don't feel it on the wrist which can be worrisome as you may bump it into things  around, which I've done a few times but no scratches just yet but that's something to take note of.The battery life is truly impressive. I fully charged the device on November 22nd, 2025, and as of writing this review, it has been running for 14 days straight on that single charge and is sitting at 45% battery. With power-saving mode, you can extend it even further.However, there are a couple of downsides to consider. The most noticeable is the lack of physical buttons, which you can see in the product photos. You can power the Smart Band 10 off from the settings menu, but the only way to turn it back on is by connecting it to the included charging cable. This hasn’t caused any issues for me yet since I haven’t needed to turn it off, but it's something potential buyers should know. I also question the accuracy of the calorie and step counters at times, as the numbers don’t always feel consistent.Overall, if you’re looking for a simple, affordable, and reliable fitness tracker, whether for personal use or as a gift, the Xiaomi Mi Smart Band 10 is a solid choice.**Update on December 13, 2025, the Xiaomi Mi Smart Band 10 is currently on 19% of charge after a 21 day period going off one charge cycle. The Smart Band estimates that it could last for 4 more days. This device has been really impressive so far."
    },
    {
        "review": "The band is great. The battery literally lasts me like 10-11 days with a single charge. The screen quality is stunning (amoled) and it can go really bright. It has those essential features such as heart rate, weather, compass, camera button, flashlight (using the screen), media/music controller. It is also water proof. I use this band to work at a restaurant. Especially when i work in the back of house. If you work rough job this band is good. And before i forget, this band is super light when i use it for sleeping monitoring i can barely feel it"
    },
    {
        "review": "This smart band has been super helpful for tracking my step count and workouts. I feel that the readings are accurate, and I am amazed at how well this works for the price point. It's very sleek and cute, and you can customize the home screen. You can also get notifications from your phone, which is a great addition. It holds a charge for weeks!"
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a fantastic fitness tracker! I love the rose color.  It is comfortable, feels to be of quality, has a beautiful bright screen that is very responsive to touch unlike others I have tried.  Tracks , calories, steps,  time working out for a variety of exercises and sports activities.  Also includes Sp02 , stress and sleep monitoring.  I love this watch. I like it better than others I have tried ...including fit bit.  Only downside is I am unable to change to Imperial system from. Metric on the watch or through the app.  It is a small inco vengeance compared to the other benefits.  People have complained  about the app but it has been fine with the apps ease of use and functionality with exception of metric units only.  I was able to change weather to Farenheight from Celcius though. Overall I am very happy with this purchase!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Its exercise features are limited but is excellent for casual activity monitoring; sleep quality, steps. It does continuously monitor vitals during exercise but does not sound alarms or inform you without having to touch the screen to see current vitals, heart rate etc. For SpO2 you need to swipe the screen and click measure and keep still for several seconds for the oxygen level reading to complete.Build quality is good, interface is smooth, and easy to use. Great attention to detail. Materials and finish is excellent so far, the band is okay but a little tricky to get the prong to find an opening sometimes. It is very lightweight and fairly small too so is easy to sleep with and wear in general.It is great for what it does, but I had to knock off a star for lack of workout monitoring capability and actually reflects mismanagement of customer expectation or misalignment between product advertisement and actual product."
    },
    {
        "review": "I previously owned the Fitbit Charge 6 and was constantly disappointed. Its step‑tracking data was inconsistent, and the wristband felt heavy and uncomfortable for all‑day wear. Switching to this Xiaomi smart band has been a night‑and‑day difference, and I am truly amazed by its performance.Its step counting stays accurate during daily walks. The 1.72‑inch AMOLED screen is much bigger than the small display on the Fitbit Charge 6, with tons of free watch faces for full customization. It includes many practical tools that Fitbit does not have, such as real‑time weather updates, a handy flashlight, stress monitoring, and my favorite feature: remote camera control. I can take self‑shots easily by using the band to trigger my phone camera, which works perfectly for taking photos alone.Battery life is outstanding. A full charge lasts up to 21 days, while my Fitbit Charge 6 needed charging every seven days. Best of all, this tracker costs far less, with no mandatory subscription fees for health analysis.This lightweight, well‑designed fitness band gives fantastic value for money. I absolutely recommend it."
    },
    {
        "review": "Had to retire my old Fitbit because it was very faulty, and I was no longer able to sync to the app (ever since Google bought them out, their quality has drastic gone downhill). Through some research, many people recommended the Xiaomi Mi Smart Band. From a quick glance, it has everything that I was looking for. I don't use my watch for any heavy activity or exercises. However I do track things like my sleep, heart rate, O2, steps, and stress levels. This watch has an incredibly long battery life, and is very easy to navigate on the watch and in the app. Wish I would've known about this band sooner, I would've switched over a long time ago."
    },
    {
        "review": "I have purchased two other low-priced watches of this ilk (both around $30). At first I reviewed them favorably, but their lifespan was not long. I wanted something that did not require synching to my phone.This watch has now been with me only about a month, BUT it's much better than the others, at its roughly $50 price. I have resisted purchasing the high-priced Garmin and others, hoping to go a cheaper route and spend money on something else.So far, this is EXTREMELY better than the previous cheapo watches.Good tracking of sleep and informative.THE ONLY CAVEAT is this: the watchband is designed \"backwards.\" It is very difficult to snap back on."
    },
    {
        "review": "Nice watch, I changed from 4 and there is a huge difference, my button is a bit stuck when changing the strap"
    },
    {
        "review": "I bought the watch for my mom, who only wanted to see her notifications and some health tracking data. It has a great screen, and the battery lasts for weeks."
    },
    {
        "review": "I think it's a really satisfying quality product in this price range. Of course, there may be some errors compared to higher-priced models, but the screen displays your heart rate, daily calorie consumption, and number of steps, which is very useful for managing your daily life. It also has a lot of extra features such as sleep time management, a timer function (it's convenient to use it right away without having to open your phone), and a function to find your phone in the house. It lasts more than a week without charging. For those who are thinking about getting a smartwatch, I'd recommend this product without any hesitation.Even if it doesn't fit, it costs about 5,000 yen, and I want this function to be a little more accurate! I think it's a good option to get one before thinking about changing to a high-priced smartwatch like this. This will also prevent you from suddenly spending more than 50,000 yen and realizing that you don't need something that high-performance after all. I am quite satisfied with this product, and thanks to this product, I am also concerned about the high price range. I think this is a product of such quality."
    },
    {
        "review": "Works great and easy to carry"
    },
    {
        "review": "I fell for this watch and no regrets, I started sport again and it analyzes everything, sleep, cardio, training, weight changes via the application... great for the unbeatable price"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "I absolutely love my Fitbit Versa 4 and would not want to be without it. I have been wearing a Fitbit for at least the last 15 years. I appreciate that it keeps track of my steps and reminds me to walk at least once an hour. However, recently, it might’ve turned into a lifesaver for me. I woke up to an alert, that I was in atrial fibrillation during the night. I would have never known otherwise. Also, my heart was racing for about 3 1/2 days, I knew I had to take it extremely easy until my heart rate comes down.. Google Health urged me to seek medical attention. I immediately went to urgent care and they referred me to see my cardiologist as soon as possible. I did that and they found there is a problem. It is yet to be resolved, but I am on top of things, only because I have a Fitbit. So I recently bought a new one because I do not want to be without and my current one had a little bit of a crack in the case and yet, it was still functioning fine. I wear it to sleep, to swim, and shower. Never had a problem. Battery lasts for about a week. Be aware, never plug it in directly to an outlet, it should charge by attaching it to the USB port on your computer. No complaints.. . I’ve seen others with much more expensive watches, but this does it for me. Oh, I also love the fact that Amazon has so many sellers who have the interchangeable, watch bands, for much less the cost than the original Fitbit band. Back to the Versa 4, I love the large face, my boyfriend’s watch look! Very smart looking."
    },
    {
        "review": "When I first got this, it would not maintain sync.  I updated the firmware on the watch, tried doing a factory reset twice, uninstalled/reinstalled the fitbit app twice, ensured that the app wasn't allowed to go to sleep when in the background... nothing worked.  It would work for a few minutes, then when I tried to command Alexa, it would say connection lost.  I decided to contact support, and they informed me that my app was outdated.  Come to find out, version 4.04 of the bitbit app is too old, and will not work.  The latest version, 4.06.7 had not been released for my phone yet, as is typical ridiculous play store behavior.  Google... are you listening?  When a developer releases an update, you shall IMMEDIATELY make it available to EVERY device!  I manually downloaded the apk and installed it, and everything has been working great since.  Alexa follows my direct orders immediately, phone calls come through, texts come through, and I can sync with ease.Overall, I suppose this is an improvement over the Versa 2.  Screen is a little bigger, but the notification vibrations don't seem to be as forceful, even when set to strong.This uses google wallet instead of google pay as the Versa 2 did, so my AmEx will not work for contactless payment.Phone call feature is nice, seems to work OK.  I can hear them, they can hear me.  You can only answer calls, you can't make them.  The $50 brand x watch I ordered to replace this one before I got the sync figured out was able to do that.  It could also control my camera and music player, neither of which is possible on the Versa 4.Google maps is nice if you walk in unfamiliar areas.  I'll probably never need it, but it's nice.Battery life seems to be improved over the Versa 2, but mine was 2 years old so I'm sure the battery had gradually decreased it's charge capacity.  I used it for several days, and he battery was still at 55%.Skin temperature sensor will one day be nice when it can sync with a smart thermostat and automatically adjust the temperature as needed.  But for now, it's just a novelty and has no purpose.  Yes, I know my skin is hot.  I woke up in a puddle of sweat.  Yup, I'm freezing, so my skin is cold.o2 saturation is nice if you have any relevant health problems.If you want to take full advantage of the watch, get the free \"Overview\" clock face by Chris Blyth.  It gives you current and average heart rate, battery percentage, fully customizable date format, allows for a larger clock that shows seconds, and you can have weather, and 6 other health stats displayed on the main screen, all at once.  I have steps, distance, calories burned, active zone minutes, floors, and BMI/BMR.  It all fits, and is easy to read.  Vast improvement over the default face, which shows no useful information.  I had th is face on my versa 2, and loved it.  So glad it's available for the 4.Bottom line, it's a good watch.  There are cheaper brand x ones out there, but none of them have NFC payment, and not all of them have Alexa.  And, you get what you pay for.  fit & finish feels cheap, the screens get washed out in the sun, finicky alexa integration and so on.  Best to pony up and get a refined product.  Be sure to update your fitbit app.  Their customer support said the latest version should be released within a week, but I circumvented the beurocracy and downloaded it manually.  Why wait?"
    },
    {
        "review": "After trying and returning several fitness trackers, I finally chose the Fitbit Versa, and it has exceeded my expectations. It fits comfortably on my small wrist, and the display is clear and easy to read. The health and fitness data are accurate, and it tracks my daily steps better than any other tracker I've tried.I also appreciate how easy it is to use and how well it helps me stay on top of my fitness goals. Based on my experience, I would definitely recommend the Fitbit Versa to anyone looking for a reliable fitness tracker. I also suggest purchasing the warranty for added peace of mind, just in case it's ever needed."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a warning to anyone who is considering purchasing this Fitbit model now - November 2024. DON'T DO IT! I bought this in April of 2023. When I got it, it seemed to be working fine. However, as the months have gone on - and as Google is slowly eliminating Fitbit altogether - my Versa 4 has started to malfunction more and more. Not only that, but the Fitbit app - for $10 per month - is no longer what it was at one point. Google is slowly taking away features of the app - and the watch itself. This company now, owned by Google, is being eliminated - do not waste your money. Go with an Apple Watch - or better yet, Garmin.Since my purchase of this watch, I've noticed a ton of odd incidents with it. First off, the GPS no longer works - so it doesn't track mileage, or speed or any of those types of things on runs, walks, hikes, bike rides, none of them. The sleep tracker is awful - and has always been awful. It can't tell when I'm asleep or awake. The heart rate data is wrong much of the time - showing higher heart rates than reality, especially if it is wet with sweat, pool water or the shower. My heart rate could be 70 - but the Fitbit is showing that it's 150. That is a daily occurrence. When I measure the \"stats\" with my Strava app on my iPhone, the Fitbit is always off.....sometimes by large amounts. The app features are slowly being changed and diminished as well - so it's not as useful as it was at one point in time. And, bugs - I've noticed lately that my Fitbit won't turn on sometimes. It just stops working. The wrist movement to turn the face of the watch on - that broke in the first month. Hasn't worked since. And as much as I want to like Active Zone Minutes, it's just not accurate. My heart rate will be 70, but my Fitbit will show 140 in the shower or sitting in the pool......so I'll get Active Zone Minutes for those \"activities\". LOLAnd one of the most frustrating parts for me is Google's elimination of Strava uploads from their Fitbits. When I bought it, Google allowed automatic uploads to Strava to see your rides, runs, walks, hikes, workouts, etc. Within a month after purchase of my Fitbit, Google eliminated that feature - and you can't even manually upload activities from a file.....it just shows it being empty. Fitbit covered for them by saying something must be wrong - but I found out through Google that they shut that feature down in order to drive Google Pixel sales(which don't work with iPhones, apparently).Today, Fitbit is ridiculous. It should be gone heading into 2025.....after Christmas sales. Do yourself a favor - spend the extra money on an Apple Watch or better, a Garmin. Garmin has every feature you could need or want - and then some. Yes, it cost more - but they work too! And Garmin isn't shutting down - that's why they're making the products they do. More accurate, more features, auto uploads to Strava, plus advanced features for fitness and training. All you could want or need. Even an Apple Watch has WAY more features than a Fitbit. Treat yourself right - and get one of those two instead."
    },
    {
        "review": "It arrived and would not charge.  I tried every USB I could fined and then requested a replacement.  This replacement was provided free of charge and very promptly.  For this policy I gave it more stars.  The new one arrived and also would not charge.  After some hours of research the internet told me it appears that modern usb blocks will not charge the device.  I dug put a very old one and it charged it perfectly.  The device does what it says but I am disappointed that the spo2 is not given in chart form.  As a CPAP user this would be very helpful to me.  Alas an average daily value doesn’t tell me much."
    },
    {
        "review": "Been wear5fo many months now, accurate, easy to use, keeps track of my health stats, never used messaging, but handy features, nice appearance, highly recommend this for health conscious people."
    },
    {
        "review": "I absolutely love my Fitbit Versa 4 in Pink Sand/Copper Rose. It’s lightweight, super comfortable, and the color is even prettier in person. The GPS, 24/7 heart rate tracking, and Daily Readiness Score make it perfect for keeping me on track with my fitness goals. I also love how easy it is to read my notifications and track my sleep — the insights are way more detailed than I expected.The battery life is amazing too. I can go several days without charging, and when I do, it powers up fast. It’s stylish enough to wear every day and functional enough for workouts, walks, and everything in between.If you’re looking for a smartwatch that’s cute, reliable, and full of helpful features, this one is absolutely worth it. I’m obsessed!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Love the versa 4 fitness smartwatch! It's very easy to use and is working well for me. The battery last all day with continuous use. When I go to plug it in after a full days use it is still at 96 percent battery life. I can receive text messages and email on it. I have not figured out how to respond on the watch yet so I still use my phone. It seems to keep my steps and everything else I log into it pretty accurate. I recommend this fitness watch."
    },
    {
        "review": "good quality, lots of features, works well"
    },
    {
        "review": "It was received on October 12, and it has completed two months of use and works perfectly, and I did not see any comments on it"
    },
    {
        "review": "Absolutely loved the watch! Fitbit pay works perfectly for public transportation and most merchant stores. Tracks sleep and steps well too so far. No offline music support was not an issue for me as I don't listen to music usually. Have not tried answering phone calls. Also realised no notifications on my watch for messages but will try to set it again. Overall, very worth it as I got Versa 4 at same price as Versa 3."
    },
    {
        "review": "Fitbit, is perfect to record data that everyday folks would like to no."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "This case looks much better in front of you than a picture can render. I have the Product Red iPhone XR and this is the red case. The color comes very close to the color of the aluminum frame and looks like part of the phone. At first glance it doesn't look like there is any case at all! The red color is a shiny, metallic red as opposed to the matte red color of the iPhone frame. It does not clash where the phone's color can be seen in the cutouts and it nicely compliments the color on the back of the phone! Contrary to what another reviewer said, this case does indeed have a lip for basic screen protection. If you lay your phone face down on a perfectly flat surface with this case on, the screen will not contact anything. That being said, it is minimal by design. If you think you may be tossing your expensive phone around in places where the screen could be impacted perhaps you would be better served with a more robust case. This case is a great value for what it is! It complements the phone's styling. It will protect your phone from scratches on the back and sides. I had an iPhone 6 Plus with no screen protector installed on the glass for four years. The only protection was the lip provided by the various cases I used. The glass never got more than the most superficial, barely visible marks in that length of time. Totally worth it to me for the arguably better user experience of the naked glass screen. Even the most pricey tempered glass screen protectors do not match the buttery smooth touchscreen experience in my opinion. My fingers do not slide the same way. Anyway, the glass on the iPhone XR is significantly tougher than previous phones and I don't anticipate a problem. I offer that information only to illustrate that, with proper care in handling, you can rock an inexpensive, minimalist case with no issues. It should be noted, as another reviewer has said, that the back of this case is NOT perfectly clear. The phone can be seen but there is a pattern or texture which is noticeable if you hold it to the light just right. Not a deal breaker at all for me. You can still showcase the beauty of your XR with this case but it could be an issue if you require a perfectly clear back. I think this pattern may have something to do with durability and I doubt it will be an issue to most users. There is a color coordinated lip to protect the camera lens and I love the custom color coordinated cutout for the flash and mic modules in the back. So many XR cases simply have a huge opening that looks like it was designed for a dual camera setup. I like the extra attention to detail. One caveat: The fit is snug. If you are like me and frequently take you case off for thorough cleaning or want to rock a different case for that day, care should be taken when removing this case. The iPhone's aluminum frame is slippery and you could launch your phone out of your hands! I bought several cases together to compare. The Ranvoo case is nearly identical to this one. That one is much easier to remove because it is ever so slightly thinner. It has the same attention to detail in the cutouts and the same pattern in the \"clear\" back. The tradeoff is there is virtually no raised lip to protect the screen which is why it's easier to remove. Both products are good for what they are. This Torras case is a remarkably nice product for the money. With proper care it could be the only case you need but at this price, it will be a nice option in your iPhone's wardrobe."
    },
    {
        "review": "Really love this case. It fits the phone perfectly without adding extra bulk, keeps the original look of the phone visible, and offers great grip and drop protection. Excellent quality!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Best phone case for a good price! The perfect type of material, sturdy yet flexible! Fits perfectly on my phone. Magnetic and looks clean!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Snug fit with my iPhone 17 Pro so it protects my phone quite well and I’m quite happy with it. Seems to be durable as I have dropped my phone a couple of times I’m very surfaces and so far, I haven’t seen a crack anywhere. Quality seems to be solid so far but only time will tell. Feels very slick and smooth, but maybe too smooth as it has been known to slide quite easily. There is a protector for the front screen (which I did not purchase), but I decided against it as I have discovered front screens reduce the phone‘s ability to detect commands with my finger after time has progressed and dirt/detritus builds up behind the screen after a certain length of time. I was concerned at first that the edge of the case on the back where the lenses are located would raise the phone too high and not allow charging when the phone was laying down on a charge mat, but that was not the case. Although, I did discover that exact positioning is important as just laying it down doesn’t always mean the phone is charging. So, I had to make sure to watch the screen when I wanna charge to make sure the mat and the phone aren’t position to charge when needed.All in all, this is a pretty solid product and I haven’t seen any yellowing in the plastic case as of yet, but it’s only been a couple months since I’ve been using it so maybe after a year I’ll reassess again. If there was one thing that would bug me about this case, is the raised lip of the back of the case surrounding the lenses. There was already a raised area on the phone for those lenses and this case just makes it that much more pronounced. I can only guess that the raised lip area on the back of the case is to further protect the lenses, but that also makes it harder for easy positioning for charging, and makes the phone bulkier when being placed into a pocket or bag. Still, this protects my phone and does what I want it to do so the raised area on the back is a minor issue that I can live with for now. Would I get another one? Hmmmm, maybe. Too early to tell at this point. But I won’t say no if something better comes out on the market."
    },
    {
        "review": "Good quality and easy to install for my stepdaughter’s new phone. She wanted a clear case to showcase her new phone and she added a picture to it behind the case. Looks cute and I know it’s more protected this way."
    },
    {
        "review": "Best cheapest clear case I've had! The price is super great! Ive had it for a out 3 months and its ever so slightly yellow! By this time, you can see the yellow on the edges but this has been exposed to the sun alot this summer and its barely yellow at all 🙌 i WILL be buying again!!!! 🫶🫶 you will not be disappointed!"
    },
