[
    {
        "review": "I was in need of an electric toothbrush. I was deciding between this and a large name brand, but took the chance on this one to save money. I was expecting just an average product, but this is outstanding! It cleans your teeth just as well as the big name brushes. The battery is really, really impressive. I did an initial charge when I got it and I've used it twice a day for over a month and it hasn't needed to be charged! It also comes with a bunch of extra brush heads. It also includes the charger and it charges quickly. It has a built in 2 minute timer which is very helpful. I ended up buying one for my husband and daughter too. The price and value is unbeatable."
    },
    {
        "review": "Honestly surprised by this one, being an off-brand and all. It's got a two-minute brushing cycle broken into four segments, so it nudges you to spend equal time on each section of your mouth. I didn't expect it, but it's actually gotten me brushing more thoroughly than I used to, just from following those reminders.There are also several vibration and brushing pattern options to choose from, which is a nice bit of flexibility depending on your preference or how your gums are feeling that day.Battery life is solid and holds a charge well between uses.For the price, this thing overdelivers. Highly recommend."
    },
    {
        "review": "My Phillips electric toothbrush stopped working and I wasn’t feeling good about giving Phillips more of my money on a new one.  Then I found this deal.At first glance, this looks like a scam.  The reviews are good, but the price makes you have a double take.  I took a leap of faith and I was simply in awe over a toothbrush.Power, yes you have it and more.  With more vibration settings than you really need (5 total), one setting has the speed and motion that will most likely suit you.  However, the last setting had vibration and sounds which made my girlfriend wonder if this was actually a toy for adult use.The brush will “stop and then start again” as you use it, as the way most electric tooth brushes function, to alert you when to switch your mouth location as you brush.  If you’re not familiar to this, the idea is for you to map our your mouth as 4 areas (left top, right top, left bottom, right bottom) thus when you feel the stop you jump to the next area.  The brush runs for 2 minutes and the “stops” are at every 30 seconds so you then move on to a new mouth location after each stop.  I’m not sure in the instructions if this can be turned off, but I’m so used to it as a way to know you’re getting the full 2 minutes.  More later about the power button if a two minute brushing would be too long for you.And oddly enough there’s eight (8) brush heads included, which is a big win.  If you purchased an electric toothbrush before they usually have 1 or 2 brush heads in the set.As for charging, the unit comes with a USB changer (wall adapter not included).  The instructions state you would get a week out of a charge.  After about 5 days, the unit didn’t seem to need a change but I wanted to get it to full power.  I placed the brush on the changer about 15 to 20 minutes before bed thinking I’d let it charge overnight. I noticed before going to bed the unit was fully charged.  I’ve owned the product for a few months now and I’ve never had the need to have it sit overnight since I’ve never seen a charge take over an hour much less 30 minutes.  To me, that’s pretty impressive for any cordless item.One funny fact (if you noticed) the name of the brand is “Bymore”, and being a Journalism major, I think the company font they used on the tooth brush is sci-fi retro coolness.  Anyway I do plan to “Bymore” since if the price point stays the same, you might as well for an extra unit in a different color, another charging base and to get extra brushes for the price.The only bad thing I can say about Bymore’s brush is the Phillips brushes I’ve owned had a single press of the ON/OFF switch to power off and on the unit.  This is different with the Bymore brush.  One press turns the unit on, the next press jumps you to the next setting (which as noted, the brush has five vibration settings.)  So once you find one of the five settings you like using all the time, you need to PRESS and HOLD the ON/OFF to power off, thus saving the vibration setting you like to use.  Otherwise, if you quick tap the power button you’ll just jump to the next setting.  Not a deal breaker, just a new kink you need to get used to.Like I said, this really has to be one of the best deals on Amazon. If you’re looking for a solid electric tooth brush, and not a heavy price tag, this is a no brainier.  Plus, if you don’t like the unit for your teeth, you have a really nice small powered brush for detailed cleaning around the house.Look over the other reviews, I’ve had two Phillips brushes in my past but I’m not going to hesitate to “Bymore” of these brushes again.  I think you’ll be pleasantly surprised and shocked what a deal you got."
    },
    {
        "review": "I picked up the Bymore Sonic Toothbrush in early November ‘25, after my old Wellness brush finally gave out after a near decade of service. For just $10, I wasn’t expecting much—but I’ve been pleasantly surprised. The battery life is phenomenal: I’m still on the first charge after six weeks of daily use. That alone makes it a standout in the budget category.👍 What I Like• Battery Life: Still going strong on the original charge. Huge win.• Price: At $10, it’s hard to beat. Great value for a multi-mode sonic brush.• Performance: Cleans well, with five brushing modes that actually feel distinct.👎 Minor Annoyances• Neck Thickness: The brush head’s neck is noticeably thicker than others I’ve used. It causes more drooling/spillage during brushing, which has led to temporary stains on shirts and messes on surfaces.• Molars Reach: That same thickness makes it harder to reach the far-back molars comfortably. I’ve felt friction between the neck and my teeth during deep cleaning.🤔 Durability?Too early to tell. It’s held up well so far, but I’ll need more time to judge long-term reliability.🧼 Final ThoughtsIf you’re looking for a budget-friendly sonic toothbrush with impressive battery life and decent cleaning power, the Bymore is a solid pick. Just be aware of the thicker neck—it’s a small design flaw that can lead to messy brushing sessions. If you’re prone to drooling or wear nice shirts while brushing, you might want to keep a towel handy."
    },
    {
        "review": "I have had this brush for 6 months and I love it. Hold a great charge and can take it in the shower and comes with replacement heads.All and all a great brush for the price."
    },
    {
        "review": "I purchased this electric toothbrush for myself and was very pleased with it. When my son mentioned that he needed a new toothbrush, I showed him mine, and he decided to give it a try. He ended up liking it just as much as I do.What sets this toothbrush apart from my previous electric toothbrush is the variety of speed settings and the gentle gum-massaging sensation it provides while brushing. Another feature I really appreciate is the smart timer. My previous toothbrush would simply shut off when the brushing cycle ended, but this one briefly pauses and recognizes that you're still brushing, then automatically continues with another cycle without requiring you to restart it.Overall, I would definitely recommend the Bymore Electric Toothbrush for adults. It offers great functionality, is comfortable to use, and provides a more enjoyable brushing experience."
    },
