        "review": "The toothbrush works really well & I love that it has its own charger"
    },
    {
        "review": "Good toothbrush.  Lots of replacement brush heads.  Charges quickly, holds charge well.  I got it at a very good price, but would not pay the regular price.  Delivery took a long time and it was delayed several times.F"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "Fantastic, works well, cleans great, and has a long battery life! This is so much cheaper than other name brands, and works just as well! Plus, the brush accessories and the case for travel make this a no-brainer to get. The value for the money just can't be beat. It works and feels like an electric toothbrush worth $80 to $110 while being a fraction of the price. This is my go-to brand for toothbrushes from now on!"
    },
    {
        "review": "12/15/23 Original Product Review (in case I return for updates).THE BASICS12/13/23 Found the Bitvae R2 Rotating Electric Toothbrush in Pink on Amazon and Placed Order.12/14/23 Received the product late this evening, securely packaged and undamaged. The pink hue is true to its image color, and I like it despite not being a \"pink person.\"TLDRThis morning, I brushed my teeth for the first time in two years without over- or under-brushing, sensitivity and pain-free, and without feeling like my teeth were shifting.THE FULL STORYLet me explain: In 2021, I got into a major cycling accident that changed a lot of my daily routine I took for granted more difficult. After going over my handlebars, I primarily landed on my mandible with my maxilla taking a hard blow, too. My chin bounced twice on the ground (only for my lower jaw to pop back) before my face (and the rest of me) slid on the concrete to a stop. My top row of teeth, well, the only way I know how to put it is that they immediately felt like they had slumped forward in my face (and they had).After a bit of dampened sound and ear-ringing, bright lights and loud, bothersome noises, not to mention the blood pouring from my nose, face, mouth, and wounds, I was treated (probably insufficiently) at the ER and headed to a local dentist. From the general feeling of skidding my lower teeth and lip on the pavement and having my head jolted enough that each front top tooth looked like someone had crushed ice in a blender and left it sitting in its container, I knew things were going to be bad.THE CONSEQUENCESNot only had the long and painful, but effective process of moving my jaw forward by an orthodontist been completely undermined, but all six top front teeth were, indeed, crushed. The palatine process of my maxilla (soft palate) had a thin, but long crack running back through it, and the other more superficial parts of my maxilla that surrounded my sinuses -- tiny fractures ran throughout.WHY OTHER TOOTHBRUSHES WEREN'T WORKINGSince then, tons of anesthesia, twelve surgeries, lots of drilling, filing, and testing, irritating crown fitting, teeth shifting, nerve pain and cold sensitivity...things haven't been the same. Try as I might, what used to be a simple, quick everyday task had become frustrating.I've always believed I shouldn't let the little things get to me. But often, it's exactly the little things that get to you, because they're not just little things. They're habitual. They're routine. Across the span of your life, they're actually really big things. How you approach your health -- including your teeth -- is one of those things.I've tried extra soft and soft manual toothbrushes, different brands, returned to my original Colgate; I tried using the Burst toothbrush and a similar \"sonic\" variety that my husband uses; different toothpastes; mouthwashes; flossing methods and flossers; coconut oil pulling; everything I knew to do before and that could be done or that recently came out...whatever friends and family recommended. In two years, I've not once felt like my teeth were fully cleaned or, if picked, prodded, and scrubbed well, fully stable.THE BITVAENothing has worked like this toothbrush. Today, my teeth feel clean, but my gums aren't sensitive, swollen, or bleeding from my being excessive thoroughness and over-brushing. And I've only tried the \"Clean\" setting.The timer feature in the Bitvae was a smidge startling because, eager to try it, I overlooked the manual details and was unaware that there was one lol. Let's just say it's obvious. But, if you know to expect it, it's brief and distinct -- a slightly different buzz that cues you to move on and kinda' propels you to, which (having OCD tendencies) I appreciate. And while I wouldn't say the Bitvae is quiet, it's certainly no louder than any of the other battery-operated competitors I've tried. (Because of the accident's damage to my ears, it honestly sounded a little like a quiet light saber at times. Weird, nerdy Star Wars reference, I know, but I'm just calling it like I...heard it.)As un-irritated as my gums feel, they also feel well-massaged. The tissue looks and feels clean and bright, without feeling or showing any signs of inflammation or agitation. From the front of my crowns to the crevices behind my molars and all in between, my enamel is evenly brushed and fresh. The Bitvae's brush head was small enough to clean all around without knocking about or hitting other teeth. Meanwhile its grip, heavy in my hand, was sturdy to hold, but not burdensome in any way. It was very easy to maneuver.My breath smells and tastes better (un-tinged by the last of accidentally brushing something too hard and more so than if I tried \"brushing gently\"), if that makes sense. And the Bitvae, having come charged, remains charged, worked smoothly -- better than I anticipated.I rarely (IF EVER) give products a five-star rating. I'm a meticulous person who, in herself and the products she purchases, almost always finds room for improvement. But, I'm truly satisfied with this purchase, which cost me less than half of what a similar Oral B would (when the Oral B is on sale for 50%+ off) have.CONCLUSIONBuy it. I don't like throwing away money, and I know all of this sounds dramatic, but an accident like the one I had is nothing short of that. Meanwhile, the Bitvae comes with a year's warranty, which is reassuring as a customer and as someone whose dental health has been affected for life. The return of a well-cared-for mouth with the ease I once had of \"just brushing my teeth\" pre-accident is amazing. To have brushing my teeth -- which had become a process in itself to grapple with every morning --  return to a brief, but effective routine is such a relief. I'm grateful for the bit of time I'll get back every day. I hope you'll take the opportunity to enjoy it, too!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Came nicely packaged with a sleek travel case and several spare brush heads. It feels a bit bulky in the hand, but it works great. One thing I noticed is that it doesn't have the pressure sensor light like my old Oral-B (which my better half ended up claiming 😅), but to be honest, I don’t really miss it.It has multiple modes: White (which I tried but felt was a bit too intense for my teeth), Clean (my go-to), Sensitive, Gum Care, and Tongue. So there's a good range to choose from depending on your needs.The charger is compact and gets the job done quickly, though I do wish it had a USB-C port. Battery life is solid—I use it once or twice a day and only need to charge it about twice a month. Overall, I’m really happy with it. It performs almost as well as the high-end brushes from bigger brands, but at a much more reasonable price."
    },
    {
        "review": "A great product at a great price and 2 years on it still works like new and cleans my teeth very well each time!I love that it has different settings and that unlike their other model (the one without the spinning circular head), it has a separate power and vibration setting button. I'm so glad that the company made that change because it was so annoying trying to turn off the brush only to accidentally switch settings and have to spend 3 minutes each time trying to reset it.It also comes with a warranty and you can register the product with the company to get discounts and product care from this one purchase, which I found really nice and made me trust them more.The battery lasts about 1.5-2 months before I have to charge it again (I brush 2x daily) so that's also a big plus for me.All in all highly recommend this brush because you really can't beat it for the price and value."
    },
    {
        "review": "I have their water flosser and needed a spinning toothbrush to target the brown stains around the teeth near my gum line. I've used manual extra soft tooth brushes and the vibrating tooth brush as well. The extra soft bristles are easy on my teeth and gums especially having gum recession in certain areas. I use the softest spin which still does a great job and can easily reach the rear. 1 downside is that the battery only lasts ~1 week as I'm very thorough with my brushing due to my dental needs brushing ~6 minutes average each time twice daily so you do need to charge it weekly but the benefits outweigh the need for weekly charging. The 8 brush heads are great which means I don't have to buy more brush heads for another 2 years. Highly recommend."
    },
    {
        "review": "I have used this for a few days now and I really like it!  It has different settings for different amounts of pressure depending on your sensitivity.  I like the timer function and that it lets you know every 30 seconds so that you can change the positioning of the brush.  It also came with extra heads that should last me a couple of years.  For the price, this is a great toothbrush!  I can't comment on battery life yet, as I have not used it for that long.  I am considering buying a second one to keep in my travel pack.Update Sep 25, 2024  I still love the R2! It works great and holds a charge well.I recently purchased an R1 just to use as a travel toothbrush.  It worked well at first, but after about 6 weeks it would just die during a brushing, even though I had just charged it.  The odd thing was, plugging it into the charger for 3-4 seconds would \"reset\" it and then it worked again.  This kept happening though.  By then it was past the 30-day return window.  I contacted their support and they were VERY helpful!  After some troubleshooting that didn't help, they promptly sent me a new one!  Time will tell if this one will do the same thing.  However, I am very happy with their support!That being said, if I were to buy a new one at some point, I would go with the R2 just because I like its features a little bit better."
    },
    {
        "review": "I always bought Oral B electric toothbrushes, but after my last one bit the dust, I decided to try another brand since a basic Oral B is quite expensive. Based on information I got from the internet, I decided to try a Bitvae Electric Toothbrush. It was a fraction of the cost.It was a little different from the Oral B, but now that I'm used to its feel, I'm very satisfied. The only drawback for me was the brush heads that came with it. They were too hard for my sensitive teeth. But I solved that problem by purchasing a set of softer brushes."
    },
    {
        "review": "I really liked this toothbrush until my second one died after six months...I got my first one in 2023 and it lasted 2.5 yrs B4 it died (turned off during use and no amount of charging could revive it). With the toothbrush being affordable and lasting so long, I got another one in 2025. I've been using the new one just over six months and it has now died as well and won't charge anymore...I'm switching to a new brand because while I may get another one that lasts me years, I don't want to risk getting another one that only lasts 6 months. It did a fantastic job and held a charge well etc for the time that it worked though. 😌I liked it so much that I also got my husband one in 2024 when he needed a new one. He's been using his for a little over two years now, so I'm interested to see how long his lasts B4 it dies.If you get it, enjoy it while it lasts! May be for years or it may be months, but as long as it works your teeth will be squeaky clean!"
