[
    {
        "review": "After using the set for a week, we’re very satisfied with this 5-piece knife set. Our previous expensive German knife set was over 20 years old and needed to be professionally sharpened.  So we decided instead of putting more money into the old set to test a new set of knives. Read some good reviews from this brand, so we thought it would be worth giving it a test.The 8-inch chef’s knife and 8-inch bread knife alone makes this set a great value. All of the knives are comfortable to use with the textured handles.One of my favorite additions to our old set is the 7-inch Nakiri knife. It makes preparing Asian vegetables much easier and I can finally replace my old Chinese cleaver. It’s a great size being shaped smaller than my old cleaver for chopping and slicing vegetables quickly and cleanly.Overall, excellent set for the price to replace our old knife set. The quality and value exceeded our expectations. I now need to be careful to not cut myself when using this new set of very sharp knives."
    },
    {
        "review": "A Little about me:I Have Been a Knife Enthusiast, Collector And Former Knife Maker For Over 40 Years.(Information Below From Various Research)A Little about PAUDIN:PAUDIN is a Fairly New Up and Coming Knife Company that was Started in 2017 By David XIAO. The Company has been in Business for about 9 Years and the Steels are Obtained From Different Parts of the World that is Manufacturing High Quality Knife Steel Like Japan, Germany, Sweden Etc. Etc. No One Actually Knows Where each Steel is Acquired  Because it is a Company Secret. Although PAUDIN Is a Recent Upstart, PAUDIN Makes the Knives in Yangjiang, China - A City Famous for Knife Making for Almost 1500 Years. PAUDIN has been Producing High Quality Knife Sets that are Functional and Beautiful for the Price.About the PAUDIN Zenith Collection 5 PCS Kitchen Knife Essential Chef Knives Ultra Sharp Set. These Knives have all the Best Qualities of the Chef's Essentials in the Kitchen. The Knives Include an 8\" Chef Knife, an 8\" Bread Knife, a 5\" Santoku Knife, a 7\" Nakiri Knife and a 5\" Utility Knife that are All Essential Favorites of Chef's. Each of the Knives is Full Tang Which means the Blade is the Full Length of the Knife and through the Handle for Superior Strength. The Handles are Beautifully made From G10 which is a Form of Fiberglass and Securely Riveted to the Knife Blades. The Knives are Perfectly Balanced and Fit Very Well into your Hand.For Me, the Most Important Part of the Knives are the Steel that is Used to make the Blades. The Steel used is CJ14C28N Also Known as SANDVIK Steel, Originally From Sweden. This Steel Has Been in High Quality Knife Making For Many Years. This is a High Carbon Steel that has Excellent Corrosion Resistance and Holds a Good Edge Sharpness at 58-60 HRC on the Rockwell Hardness Scale. The BEST PART-PAUDIN Takes the Hardness to a Whole New Level With Their Custom Vacuum Heat Treating and Cryogenic Quenching Process Boosts the Rockwell Hardness Scale to an AMAZING 63 HRC Which Rivals or Beats the Blade Hardness some of the FINEST Blades ever Made. The Process is Explained in their Listing and Is Extremely Interesting with the Photos. This Supercharged Hardness Makes the Blades Hold an Extreme Edge and Yet Be Resistant to Chipping. These Blades are Amazing For Hardness and Razor Sharpness.This 5 Piece Set is a Great Addition or Start to Anyone's Kitchen. They are Sharpened Beyond Standard Sharpening and Cut Very Easily. The Price on this Set is Also Amazingly Low and PAUDIN Offers Discount Coupons at Close to Almost 50% off. So Don't Wait or Hesitate, Get Yourself One of these Sets and You'll be Happy You Did."
    },
    {
        "review": "The knife set by PAUDIN includes 5 popular styles used in today’s kitchens. The set includes an 8” Chef, 8” Bread, 7” Nakiri, 5” Santoku and a 5” Utility knife. According to the product listing, the knives are constructed with CJ14C28N steel that undergoes Vacuum Nitriding and Deep Cryogenic Treatment to enhance toughness, reduce edge chipping risk, and improved corrosion resistance. The edge angle of the blade is listed as 13° and has a hardness rating of 63HRC. The  full tang handle is covered with G10 high pressure fiberglass laminate. The included product guide states to use a wood or bamboo cutting board and to hand wash and dry immediately after use. I could not find any mention of “dishwasher safe” in the product guide.I tested all 5 knives and they are all incredibly sharp. In fact, the bread knife is the sharpest bread knife I have ever had and cleanly cuts through hard crust without crushing the bread. The Nakiri, Chef’s and Santoku knives easily cut through a variety of vegetables with very little effort. While the Nakiri knife may look like a mini-cleaver, it is typically intended for cutting soft foods or vegetables. The G10 handles are comfortable to hold, grippy and are thicker in the middle. The heel of the knife has a curved cutout for the index finger, but creates a sharp point where the curve meets the edge of the knife. While this sharp point is easy to avoid on the larger knives, it is not the case for the 5” Utility knife because of the lower blade height. If you typically hold the knife with a thumb and index finger, it is easy to poke yourself because there is less physical space. I’m thinking of dulling the point with a stone to prevent skin punctures. Even though I’m on the fence with the heel design of the blade, this set contains a nice variety of very sharp knives that would be great in any kitchen."
    },
    {
        "review": "New knives are fun. Very fun. There is nothing like a fresh blade.I opened the box like a kid on Christmas. Five knives. All shiny. All new. All grinning at me in a menacing, helpful way.And they cut. Oh, they cut. Ultra sharp, just like the box said. I sliced a tomato and it barely noticed. Thin slices. Clean slices. Slices you could read a newspaper through.I chopped an onion. No sawing. No crushing. Just a smooth glide. The onion did not stand a chance. The onion did not even see it coming.Let me talk handles. Full tang. That is the good stuff. The metal runs all the way through the handle. No cheap little stub hiding in there. It feels solid. It feels balanced. It feels like it means business.That full tang is a good sign. It usually means quality. It usually means the knife will not snap when you get bold with a squash. So far, so good.But here is my big question. The only question that matters.How long will they stay this sharp?That is the real test. Anything is sharp on day one. Day one is easy. Day one is a honeymoon. The blade is fresh. The angle is perfect. The romance is real.Day two hundred is the truth. Will they still glide? Or will I be sawing at a tomato like a caveman with a butter knife?I do not know yet. I cannot know yet. Sharpness fades slow. It sneaks up on you. One day you are slicing like a pro. The next day you are pressing hard and hoping.So I am watching. I am cutting. I am keeping notes like a suspicious scientist.Right now they are wonderful. The full tang gives me hope. The build feels honest. But the jury is out. Way out. The jury went home for the weekend.Let me talk value. Five knives for a fair price. Good so far. If the edge holds, this is a steal. If it dulls fast, this is a lesson.So here is where I land. Great out of the box. Scary sharp. Solid handles. But durability is the whole game, and I have not played enough of it yet."
    },
    {
        "review": "I bought this as a housewarming gift and it turned out to be a huge mistake. The outer box arrived dented and beat‑up right out of delivery. The knives felt sharp at first, but they dulled super fast after minimal use. Cutting soft tomatoes is already a struggle. Some blades have tiny rough burrs along the edge. There is no proper block for storage either. Embarrassing to give this as a gift. Complete garbage for the price. Stay far away."
    },
    {
        "review": "These knives arrive sharpened and ready to use immediately, and have been a great addition to our kitchen. These knives are light, but they have just the right amount of small weight so that it can cut through thick meats and fruits easily.The Santoku Knife was probably the most unexpectedly impressive out of all of the knives. At first glance, it seems that the knife is too small to handle for everyday use. However, it is in fact this size, and how well it cuts is what makes it all-rounded. The knife is light, which makes it easier to cut faster whilst having control over it. Until I started using the Santoku knife, I did not realize how unnecessarily big other knives were.The Chef Knife is an average sized chef knife. I mainly use this to cut thicker, harder things. Although slightly heavier than the Santoku knife, it is still light which gives me a lot of control. With how well it is sharpened, it can cut through hard fruits like melon/watermelon like butter.The other knives are also nice and sharpened, and is high-quality overall.The price is quite high, and for this price it would have been nice if it came with a knife holder. However I can certainly say that these are one of the best kitchen knives I've used. I am hoping that the knives will retain its sharpness for quite a while before it will need sharpening."
    },
    {
        "review": "I’ve been very impressed with Paudin knives in general, and this 5 piece set certainly continues that. The knives are extremely sharp, and the handles are comfortable for my hands. With plastic handles and stainless steel blades, they are easy to keep clean and should last a very long time. It’s a little hard to judge the price; currently they are listed at one price but have a coupon for 40% off…at the list price I would say they are a decent value but that you might find other sets to be a better value; at the coupon price, they are a great value.I didn’t have any particular intention when I ordered this set, but now that I have them, this is going to be my traveling knife set. I’ve gotten used to taking at least one or two knives with me when I rent a summer vacation house, since they never seem to have decent or sharp knives. With the padded carrying case these come in, I can now take a very nice selection of sharp and high-quality knives with me. That’s a win.My final comment, and it doesn’t detract from my rating, is that I’d love this set even more if the santoku and small utility knife were just a little bigger. My favorite overall knife is a 6” chef’s knife, and this one is a little smaller than the ones I prefer. But overall, if these knives fill your need, it’s a great product."
    },
    {
        "review": "Amazing 5pc kitchen knife set by Paudin!  Made of sharp high carbon stainless steel, it features an ergonomic handle for stability/balance.  Dishwasher safe and easy to care for.  You get a 8\" chef, 8\"bread, 7\" Nikiri, 5\" Satoku and a 5\" utility knife in the set.These knives are razor sharp, so be careful of your fingers.  The post says dishwasher safe, but the insert suggests cleaning by hand and drying them fully to preserve them better.  The handles on these are so nice, they fit into my hand comfortably and have a great balance.  Inital test was with raw veg, and it made the job so easy.  I thought I had somewhat decent knives, but these are a different level.  Sharp!  I keep mine sharp but these are super sharp and knicked me when washing them.  The handle is made perfect and seamless, I wish all my knives had this handle.  I have used the Nikiri, Santok and Utility knife so far.....all perfect.  I am so impressed with this set and am considering a set as a gift.  I think these are going to last a long time, they are made so well.  Amazing knife set and I recommend them."
    }
]

