    {
        "review": "i love how well the camera performs, i was nervous at first ordering a used camera for the first time. but the quality is great. it did take a while to ship but i mean it was def worth the wait."
    },
    {
        "review": "Camera is nice. Works great. Nikon is a fine brand."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a great camera to get started in professional photography. The camera is really nice. Put some money into better lenses, The stock lense is decent but your going to want more."
    },
    {
        "review": "This camera more than meets the quality/price expectations of the average advanced user (I am referring to the user who does not use automatic mode all the time and prefers to use the usual elements of shutter speed, depth of field and other possibilities for creative control of the image). I am not going to repeat what you can read in any comparison that includes the D5100, about its excellent sensor and other features. What has been said will suffice, if you are a non-professional user and do not want to spend more than 500 or 600 euros on your camera, this is an excellent option. So far I have not found any disadvantages, except that the manual is in... I don't know, some Eastern European language, a problem that is solved by downloading the manual in Spanish (or English) in pdf from the Nikon site. Also, when the camera's viewfinder screen is in Live View, it is a little slower than when using the optical viewfinder. Bullshit. The \"kit\" lens, for normal photos, is fine. If you want more, logically, you need more complete and better quality equipment, especially a tele or zoom with a focal length of up to 300 or 400 mm if you are interested in photography of distant subjects. The flash is really powerful, taking into account that it is a device attached to the camera. My advice, after about five or six thousand photos from the time of purchase, is that this is a camera to take into account for those looking for a good mid-range machine."
    },
    {
        "review": "A small conclusion first: there is no more camera for the money in the amateur or middle class. In this respect, the Nikon D5100, like the D7000 (the latter in the semi-professional league), is a bargain. The operating concept is, by Nikon standards, relatively menu-heavy. But that's not a serious disadvantage, as all photographically relevant settings can be accessed by pressing the \"i\" button twice. The sharp display also shows tips and hints in a very beginner-friendly manner, and technical functions such as aperture and shutter speed are presented in a comprehensible manner using successful symbols. As is usual with Nikon, the camera is very well equipped. In addition to pure configuration options, creative photography is also not neglected. So is e.g. For example, a multiple exposure function is also available, in which two shots merge into one. In combination with the foldable monitor and the perspectives that can be achieved with it, the artist can let off steam here! What I also like is the very neat workmanship of the camera, which is subjectively more valuable than that of the big competitor's \"plastic bombers\". Plastic is common in this price league, but that also has a weight advantage. The robustness of the D5100 is still right. The crucial thing: as a great connoisseur of the Canon system (including full format), the built-in image sensor of the D5100 completely surprises me! The sharpness of detail and resolution is already great with the small but very usable 18-55mm VR. Really good at f/8. Extreme weaknesses, for example when the aperture is open or in the corners of the image, which are often seen in the inexpensive lenses of the competition, are not present here. Surprisingly, the annoying color fringes on contrast edges (chromatic aberrations) occur rarely or in a non-disruptive manner. A hobby photographer can't go wrong with Nikon's kit lens. And even ambitious people may like to stash the 18-55mm in their backpack as a light addition to high-quality fixed focal lengths. As expected, the D5100 works at its best with fixed focal lengths such as the Nikon 35mm 1.8. Even when compared to a full-frame camera from its major competitor, the 16 megapixel image sensor doesn't have to hide. The images appear very clear and are simply brilliant and beautiful - with Nikon, great image quality doesn't just start at €1,600. This also applies at high ISO settings. At least up to ISO 4000 there are still nice prints in A4 or A3 - that wasn't the case a few years ago. Regarding the autofocus: the 11 measuring fields are arranged very sensibly in the viewfinder. You can easily focus on the “golden ratio”. The accuracy is convincing; when the AF assist light is switched on, even at dusk. If you want even more flexibility, you have to choose the D7000 or D5200. Its 39 measuring fields have to be selected first for difficult subjects, which slows down the workflow a bit. Conclusion: small, cute camera with great qualities. With a \"kit\" lens, it's just right for a stroll, a weekend trip or a vacation. With high-quality lenses, it's almost outrageously smart in terms of the image appearance. The very successful contrast distribution with great detailing of even the critical lights as well as the vibrant colors are exemplary - the brand with the big \"C\" can create as much hype about its own new releases. Purely subjectively: the compact, slightly beefy housing with typical Nikon design elements makes the D5100 an \"eye-catcher\" and popular figure. The little one has it all - and I say that with a completely professional attitude."
    },
    {
        "review": "super"
    },
    {
        "review": "The images: very beautiful, fine, contrasty and with good sharpness, very good rendering of the depths of field: it's a real pleasure.... and the sensitivity: incredible! no noise, excellent performance! So ideal when you avoid the flash! Use: easy to handle with its handle, easy settings and in French (almost as practical as the settings of the Lumix from Pana!...). As for the possible presets and options, they are more numerous than necessary. The screen swivels in all directions, robust as they say, allows you to photograph from all angles. I've had it for a year, I make the manual settings and am very happy with this camera. I only regret not having taken an 18 - 105 lens, because the zoom is a little limited on this model. Charger: without cable, it attaches directly to the wall plug, which is practical: it takes up little space."
    },
    {
        "review": "Ok firstly let me say this is not a technical expert review, rather a review of an averagely intelligent electrical engineer that has taken up DSLR photography.My first and most important point is going to talk about Camarthan Cameras. I have had some technical issues with the camera, that turned out to be partly due to my still learning and a dodgy lens i was using. The level of support/service i got was nothing short of excellent. It has been an absolute refreshing change to receive prompt responses to problems, to answer questions on the phone and provide an excellent after sales care, to the point i've even bought a book for the D5100 and another lens of them. I have absolutely no affiliation with the company, in fact i don't think i could further away from them. I would recommend them to anyone wanting camera equipment on here 100%Now to the cameraI bought a Bridge camera and knew instantly i wanted a full DSLR, so against the wive's better judgement, i sold my Bridge camera and bought the Nikon D5100 after reading a thousand reviews on several different cameras.I came to the conclusion that all the major DSLR makers have plus and minus points, you have to decide what is right for you.The camera arrived with the 18-55mm lense and after reading the manual i carefully attached the lense. (trust me you are so nervous about damaging the sensor, the glass the cpu, but just line it up correctly, ensure your camera is off and insert the lense.Charged battery, which only took 45mins inserted battery and then SDXC card 32GB. Turned on and set the camera to program P. (Get off auto and use program until you are happy to move onto A,S, & M modes, which doesn't take long).Secondly after having POS and Bridge cameras i spent 5 mins trying to operate the zoom, LOL. You operate it manually by turning the zoom part of the lense.I am still waiting to get out in the field and really test the capabilities of the camera, but so far all the pet photos ive done are crisp and clear. ( and when they are not it is not the cameras fault.)I have since ordered a sigma 70-300mm lense and tested it and it is a superb budget second lense and should be enough for your needs for now.I am currently following books and video tutorials on getting the best settings for different situations then trying them and recording them on paper until i can embed them in the brain.One thing i am surprised about is the lack of auto ISO in S, A & M modes, but personaly i cannot see any graining on ISO settings up to 3200. But i dont inspect photos at A3 size with a magnifyinh glass either.Ok waffle overConclusionThis camera is seriously cheap for what you receive. It will perform all of the actions you require if you are not a proffessional photographer. Dont listen to some that say dont get the 18-55mm lense it is a good little lense to start off with and comes with VR and AF-S.There are a ton of settings and ways to get to them. Honestly ive spent more of the week learning these than actually taking photographs, as i beleive you need to be slick with settings to capture the moment.This really is a superb camera.If you get lost in the settings and think the camera is playing up, use the two green buttons to reset all the camera settings before thinking its broken (it will save you sending it off. speak to Camarthan Cameras on the phone, they will tell you what you have done _DOH)FinallyGo to the Nikon website and create an account, then register your camera for a full 2 year manufacturers warranty.Hope this helps newbies and anyone who is thniking of buying this camera."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "After seeing similar bundles for used and refurbished cameras for only a few dollars less I was skeptical that this one would truly be a new one, but it was.  Everything was wrapped and packaged obviously directly from the factory and not a scratch of ding on it.  Delivered quickly too.I've taken a few hundred pictures with it now and they've all come out nice.  (Pictures I posted are probably degraded by the internet to save bandwidth.) This is an easy to use camera in full auto mode as well as providing very good control in the program and full manual mode. If you're new to cameras that are more advanced than point and shoot types, take the time to learn how to use all the settings.  There are lots of videos online for this particular camera that are very helpful.The lenses seem to be good quality for this price range too. Yes, I guess it's considered a beginners camera, but also nice that it doesn't cost an arm and a leg.  All the other accessories were included too.  I used to shoot much more advanced photos back in the days of having a film camera with my own color darkroom, but after decades of being out of it (just got burned out), digital is a new world for me in some ways.  So for me this is a great camera for casual to even more serious shooting as I return to this hobby.  Time will tell how much I get back into this, but so far I think this is a great camera."
