[
    {
        "review": "Height is average, chair does not have a massage feature. Portability, stability, and sturdiness of chair is great. Chair is comfortable to sit on whether your working or gaming. I highly recommend this chair if you have back issues. Overall easy to assemble. My husband said it just takes a while to put the screws in place."
    },
    {
        "review": "I’ve had this chair for a few days now, and I’m really impressed with how much comfort and quality you get for the price. The setup was easily understood based on the instructions & it took me about 20–30 minutes. Once assembled, it looks sleek and felt sturdy. The functionality is great for a basic gaming / office chair & it is very easy to move around unlike others that you have to drag with you.The lumbar support & adjustable recline are perfect for long work or gaming sessions. The padding is firm but not too stiff, and the armrests sit at a good height. I use it daily in my office for video editing & gaming, and it’s made a big difference in my posture & comfort.Overall, this is a solid, budget-friendly chair that doesn’t feel cheap. Highly recommend for anyone looking for a stylish, ergonomic upgrade without breaking the bank."
    },
    {
        "review": "I'm a big believer in \"you get what you pay for\" and I usually advise to save up, make one purchase, make one investment and don't worry about it for a few years. But something about this chair's value really scales, especially if you can grab it on sale. I got mine in white for $75 including tax and shipping. Many quality gaming chairs cost hundreds of dollars. There a handful of cheap office chairs you can snag for $60. This chair compared to one of those generic office chair is worth the extra $15. If you're looking at this chair for $90 or higher, then the former statement is true (save up). Anyway. I've had this chair for a day now and it isn't perfect. I'll go over what I like and what I don't like.Appearance: It looks nice. Doesn't look or feel too cheap. The base is metal with plastic feet. Many chairs have plastic base with plastic feet.+1ptFunctionality: This chair has a handful of features--You can adjust the height. +1ptBut it only has about a 3in range -1ptIt has a lock to let you remain upright and can be unlocked so you can recline back. +1ptIt does NOT have a way to lock in the reclined position. -1ptComfort: This chair is pretty comfy. It has a removable/adjustable head pillow and a removable/adjustable lumbar pillow as well. +1ptIt is a little stiff. Some people may like that. It also may break it after a while. I've only had it one day so I will update if something new develops. The chair has a *mostly* comfortable width to me. It measures 19\" for arm to arm. But it has this little \"cushion\" that tries to contour your legs that makes it a little uncomfortable to sit with my legs spread even a little. I have to keep my knees closer together than I normally would to keep the \"cushion\" from digging into the side of my legs. Because of this, there is really only about 16\" of room for your butt or the sides of your thighs. I'm about 5'10\" Male 215lbs size 38 pants XL shirt. I think I am at the cusp of being able to comfortably sit in this. It was not made with larger people in mind. It does state a 250lb weight limit while we're on the topic. -1ptAssembly: I've assembled several office chairs before and this process was similar and just as easy. +1ptIf you aren't experienced or if you struggle with Ikea stuff, you may find it difficult. The hardware that they include is kinda junk. If you have the luxury of using your own hardware (or hand tools at least), I would highly recommend it. I had one bolt that had really junky threads from the factory and I couldn't do much about it, just had to cross thread it and tighten it the best I could. I had another bolt that stripped and the thread never would grab. Luckily there was one extra included in the packaging and I ended up being able to tighten it without cross threading. I think I got lucky. 90% of the assembly was smooth sailing and the few snags that arose, I knew exactly what to do. Again, if you aren't experienced with this, it could easily turn into a headache and a return to amazon over poor quality hardware"
    },
    {
        "review": "Very comfy"
    },
    {
        "review": "Very easy to put together. I got a black and white one, and it was simple but perfect for use. It’s lasted 3 and a half years, but the bottom now is beginning to rip apart, so we’re getting a new one. We occasionally had to tighten the arms and bottom back up as time went by, but it’s been a great chair."
    },
    {
        "review": "We have 6 cats and let me just say this chair has been through a LOT! We’ve had it for 3 years and still looks good"
    },
    {
        "review": "At roughly a year-ish of ownership.It became my main office/computer chair, replacing a pretty standard office chair originally bought at staples/office depot.  Work from home so in this chair a pretty consistent amount of time, beyond leisure time/work outside of this.Pro's - pretty durable.  I'd say its gotten its fair share of wear and tear and it's still holding pretty well. No rips that I see, arm supports still holding strong, can lean back and shift the chair up/down with no issues.  Head rest isn't half bad, same with the included lower lumbar support.(Sort of) Con's - It's a little smaller width wise than I'd realize.  The previous staples office chair wasn't that expensive, but it had a few extra inches in the seat so it felt a bit more leisurely than this.  It's not terrible, but check the width dimensions just to be sure.The seat cushion you sit on isn't much of a 'cushion' in the sense that it's soft or has any 'give' when you sit, its pretty solid.  The good of this means it kept its form over time - the bad is it isn't very comfortable after extended periods; depends what you're expecting.  This sturdiness also translates to the occasional bit of not so comfortable sitting with the family jewels.I bought it the intent to see if this style of chair is worth it, considering some of the competitors want $500 for these.  It's good for the price point, but with some shortcomings so to me, it was worth it over spending $500 and having the same complaints."
    },
