    {
        "review": "I’m really impressed with this 27-inch Sceptre gaming monitor! The screen size is perfect for gaming, working, or everyday use, and the picture looks clear and sharp. I really like having DisplayPort connectivity, which makes it easy to connect to a gaming PC. The modern design also looks great on a desk without taking up too much space. It’s a nice choice for anyone looking for a larger gaming display at a reasonable price. Overall, a great combination of size, performance, and value!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Really good monitor the sound is just really loud and a little scratchy however that wasent an issue once I adjusted it. It looks really clean and the instructions to build were very easy. 10/10 for gaming and if u have an office job. For 27 inches and 180hrz it’s one of the best you can get for its price. Very light and thin making it not to heavy on your desk. The screen responds the moment I click my mouse. Amazing monitor would recommend"
    },
    {
        "review": "Great monitor for myself. Has great color quality with Overwatch, Baldur’s Gate 3, and other high intense deeply colored games. Set up took 15 minutes. The instructions are very clear and it comes with an HDMI cable for set up. The monitor can be adjusted every which way which works great for my dual monitor set up. Great value for the quality compared to other brands."
    },
    {
        "review": "Honestly, wasn’t expecting it to be this good. I figured when I started it up that it would be broken considering the horrible packing job that was done. Monitor was just floating in a box four times the size with no packing or anything. The tape was there for decoration essentially.  Anyways, it’s so thin and light I was really surprised it’s a really good monitor and for the price it’s great.  Better than some monitors I’ve gotten for three times the price.It has basic monitor setting capabilities via the side buttons. Can we talk about the fact it has dedicated buttons and not just that weird stick a lot of companies are switching to? Loved this. It has basic customizable settings for gaming, movies, etc so nothing crazy. Played various games with it and no tracking or anything and THAT was the biggest shocker. It’s a real ips and not a va. I know that shouldn’t be a surprise but you’d be shocked how often some sellers on Amazon do the switch up.One complaint is the fact the company shipped it with a display cable and not an hdmi. Everything is hdmi now. I dont have a port for anything other than an hdmi and if you use an adapter, there’ goes the whole quality standard and reason for using the display cable in the first place. So I had to go out to find an hdmi to even just test the screen which was more of a hassle than I wanted. The cable it came with is going in a drawer to be forgotten forever. Oh and it has built in speakers. They aren’t the best but considering, I was impressed with how big of a performer it is.My last note is the c type port in the monitor is so close to the edge that it’s very hard to find a cable that is thin enough to connect. Picture is included for reference. A lot of the cables I get are chinky c types for durability reasons and none of then were thin enough to connect. I use a docking system for my computing needs which happens to directly use the usb c specifically. So overall, it’s a great monitor. Like really good overall capabilities."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a nice monitor. Worked out of the box on FreeBSD (2 machines with a KVM switch). However, if it's on  a desk and you can only approach it from the front, it's difficult to connect cables (in my case, HDMI and power cable). I had limited space and have it on a stand on my desk. The desk is against a wall so the only way to reach it is the front. My case may be somewhat of an outlier.  My other complaint was the base.  They provide screws as well as a magnetic screwdriver, but, in part due to my clumsiness, it was difficult attaching the screws properly and the monitor twice came off the base.  This is almost certainly more my fault than Sceptre's, but it seems to me that ever other monitor I've set up was much easier to attach to the base. All of my earlier monitors were smaller though, the largest 32\" and that may be a factor.Though I was getting so annoyed I was thinking of a 3 star rating, between the hard to reach ports and the stand, but realized much of that is Just Me(TM).I am not a gamer so I can't speak about that, but it seems good video quality, and on FreeBSD it goes to its default resolution of 3440x1440 @100 with no configuration on my part. This is using a KVM for 2 machines, one with an Nvidia Geoforce GT 1030 and one a Beelink with Cezanne Radeon Vega series VGA)I also didn't use the built in speakers so I can't speak of those, either."
