[
    {
        "review": "So far so good. I have a 16 year old PC and the plug and play still worked. The 16 year old monitor no longer worked so this monitor was inexpensive but it only comes with a HDMI cable so if your PC only uses A VGA cable you have to buy one."
    },
    {
        "review": "Great monitor, good picture quality . Bigger than I expected exactly what I needed for my work from home space"
    },
    {
        "review": "Just bought it and I like it a lot! Good prices"
    },
    {
        "review": "I run multiple monitor setups across my office for different purposes — including writing, business operations, and video production. Not every machine needs a high-end display, and that’s exactly why I picked up this KOORUI 22” monitor.This one is dedicated strictly to my business correspondence system — email, documents, and general communication tasks — running off an Apple M1 Mini. For that purpose, I wanted something affordable, clean, and easy on the eyes for long sessions.This monitor fits that role very well.Text is sharp and easy to read, which is the most important factor for how I’m using it. It’s excellent for writing, reading, and basic graphic placement — but not for critical viewing of those graphics.Color is where you start to see the difference between this and higher-end monitors. It’s a bit washed out and lacks the vibrancy and precision you’ll get from more premium displays. Brightness is moderate, but in this case, that actually works in its favor — it’s very comfortable for extended use without eye fatigue.PROS:* Very affordable for what it delivers* Sharp, clean text — excellent for email and document work* Easy on the eyes for long sessions* Lightweight and simple setup* HDMI and legacy VGA connectivity* VESA compatible for flexible mountingLIMITATIONS:* Colors are somewhat muted — not ideal for photo or video work* Brightness is moderate, not high-impact* Lacks DisplayPort and USB-C connectivityREAL-WORLD USE:As a dedicated communication monitor, this is exactly what I wanted. It does its job without distraction and without the need for expensive features that wouldn’t add any value in this role.However, if your work involves photography, video editing, or anything requiring accurate color and high visual fidelity, this is NOT the monitor for that.BOTTOM LINE:For business use, writing, and communication-focused tasks, this is a strong performer at a very attractive price point.For color-critical work… look elsewhere.This earns a solid FOUR-STAR rating from me — and would have pushed into five stars with more modern connectivity options like DisplayPort or USB-C.Recommended for the right use case.If this review helped you make a decision, feel free to click “Helpful” — always appreciated."
    },
    {
        "review": "Good monitor and nice size."
    },
    {
        "review": "This monitor has two limitations: 1080p and 100Hz; if neither of these are limiters for your use, you'd be INSANE not to buy this. I bought 2 for a WFH office, but initially used them for a personal gaming laptop as I built a new gaming desktop. I ran them as 60Hz and was able to play GTAV, RDR2, etc, with no issues. The color is VERY rich, setup is easy, stand is solid (I saw at least one review claiming 'drooping', but that was not my experience with any I've bought: read on), and overall I liked them so much I've deployed 12 of them now - 2 for my WFH office, 2 for my son's computer (gaming), 2  individually for my lab, and 6 in a business setting for graphic design and engraving.Seriously, the quality for the price makes the top 5 all time for products I've bought. At $120, this is also an ideal monitor to have around for a spare - just keep an eye on the family, because one of the two spares I had grew legs when I wasn't looking, but I'm not brave enough to go into the boy's room to see if it was him..."
    },
