        "review": "I’m a beginner at guitar, but with my limited knowledge I’ll just say the quality and beauty of this guitar is by far the best investment I’ve made in my journey in trying to teach myself. The sound is on the lighter end, but I like that. The look of the guitar is just beautiful and it’s much easier to play than my other Johnson guitar. Can’t beat this guitar for the money"
    },
    {
        "review": "I appreciated the price for this guitar.  Seems solidly made and electronics are well done - no glitches so far.  I like the composition of woods and the resulting sound is clear, can be loud if you want it that way.  The imbedded tuner is handy as heck, and the action on the fret board is remarkably low making it fairly easy to play, bend notes, and move quickly.  HOWEVER - I do not like the finish on the sounding board.  It looks like it is dusty or dirty all the time as a result.  Because it comes without a pick guard - the finish is getting scratched.  The tuners do not move smoothly and so tuning becomes an issue when needed.  And when playing through and amp - it is easy to over power the amp and get some pretty crappy sound as a result.  This guitar plays beautifully as an acoustic - just use some caution when plugging in as the sound is easily distorted.  Major drawback - comes without a case!  I am glad I bought it - love the cutaway - good guitar."
    },
    {
        "review": "Beautiful guitar, plays wonderfully and sounds great"
    },
    {
        "review": "I bought this guitar to learn to play and it was a great choice. It is well made and stays in tune. It came well packaged and ready to play with only minor tuning adjustments needed out of the box. I have since upgraded to another guitar as my learning has progressed, but this one is my first and will always be special to me. Is is the perfect guitar for a beginner or an advanced player. I highly recommend this guitar."
    },
    {
        "review": "The gtr is very nice, the sound is very nice, I connect with a clean and powerful sound, all this but the seller failed me, the pickup of the mechanism that tunes the guitar is useless, it does not show values ​​of anything so it is a gtr only electroacoustic without the ability to be able to tune if you do not have pedals, personally a very good guitar but it needs to be all good."
    },
    {
        "review": "I read a review of this guitar that said it looked like a $1000. guitar. True.  Absolutely stunning.  I can't  believe I bought an acoustic guitar, sight unseen, online. Am I ever glad I did.  This git is a real beauty.  The tuners are smooth and hold tune.  The action is superb.  It sounds beautiful.  The inlay, which runs the length of the neck save for one fret, has to be seen to really be appreciated.  It is really something.  It deserves more stars.  I hope I'm  not laying this on too thick, but this guitar is amazing for the price.  Maybe I just got lucky and they sent me the best one they had.  I dunno but all I can say is the one they sent me ROCKS!!!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Beautiful Guitar, online pictures can not show exactly how good this guitar is. In person it looks awesome..."
    },
    {
        "review": "The photos do not do justice to the beauty of this guitar.  It arrived in a simple cardboard box.  Now I have to find a case for it.  It came ready to tune and play, even with a 9V battery in the tuner.  It plays easily, and has a good, balanced tone. My only complaint is the quality of the strings.  They are not bad, but they are not good either.  I think I will change them very soon for better ones.  But without a doubt, it is an excellent guitar."
    },
    {
        "review": "It arrived very well in time and form!! It would only be good if they gave them some maintenance before sending them (cleaning and hydrating the fretboard since the wood was very dusty and seemed dry!! But everything was very good, thank you!!!"
    },
    {
        "review": "The guitar arrived packaged well and has no damage whatsoever. The finish is lovely and the fretboard inlay is stunning. It's a bit hit or miss buying import guitars online sight unseen and unplayed, but this was a good specimen for me. The frets are well dressed, though the action is a bit on the high side. I don't mind a higher action, so that's not a problem for me. The strings on it, especially the higher three, are kind of dead sounding but will do for now. I'll replace them as needed.The guitar only arrived with the truss wrench, there was no warranty or inspection cards or promotional material whatsoever. This could be good or bad depending on your point of view, but it was worth mentioning.The guitar only took about a day to play in to reach tuning stability."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "Been playing for 30 years and always wanted a 12 string. With prices this low figured why not. Ordered this and it showed up quickly. Very nice right out the box. The small G string popped on me and took an .010 string from a pile of them I have and tried that, popped that one too. Tried it again and it came right in tune.  After that it took a few days for the strings to stop drifting out of tune and are now pretty solid. I play it daily and its a great player. This is one of those guitars you can just get lost in noodling. Plugged it up to my amp and it sounded great. No fret buzz and the neck plays easy. The built in tuner is a nice feature too.If you always wanted a 12 string acoustic/electric then this one is worth considering. It really is a beautiful guitar."
    },
    {
        "review": "I bought this guitar for my son to replace my twenty year old Takamine 12 string that he had been playing for the last five years.From my son's point of view, the guitar is easy to play for a 12 string.  The onboard tuner is an upgrade from the older guitar because it also shows the battery life.  It's also easy to read.  The sound quality is bright and has a higher register.  It is smaller than the Takamine.  He said the fretboard was high quality.As for looks, it's a beautiful guitar.  It's sleek and elegant.We're both happy with the purchase."
    },
    {
        "review": "This brings plays very nicely. I bought the Vangoa 12 string from Amazon and was very disappointed so I replaced it with this Ibanez. The Vangoa did have a nice matte black finish but the tuners were garbage. This Ibanez has a satin neck and glossy body finish. Does have some sharp fret ends that can be fixed with some minor sanding. Tuners hold time nicely even though 12 strings are notoriously difficult to keep in tune. For estethic reasons I changed the tuners to bronze. The action after was great up to the 12th fret but too high afterwards so I did take out bridge saddle and sand it from the bottom end (approximately 1mm) to lower the overall action.  There is a spare saddle included incase you need it. Worked out well afterwards. Overall would highly recommend if you’re looking play Pink Floyd Wish You Were Here or Bon Jovi songs lol at home but if you something more professional for recording then I would recommend something better."
    },
    {
        "review": "The build quality.. the tone.. the attention to detail... the playability This guitar has everything I was looing for, and then  delivered more than I expected"
    },
    {
        "review": "Guitar player for 20 years. I always wanted a 12-string guitar. Ibanez never fails to impress! Even though this price point might be more \"entry-level\", it feels and sounds incredible. Electronic preamp/mic hardware is solid, and onboard tuner works great. Action is low to and strings very playable. I will be using this for a long time!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I love this guitar.  I needed it for one song in my Cinderella tribute band, and it sounds and plays amazing."
    },
    {
        "review": "Came out of the box very badly out of tune. I'm talking wildly out of tune, 1.5-2 steps up or down in tuning. Not only this, but when I was able to get it in tune, almost every single string had fret buzz across the entire neck... I'm not sure how this was built, but I would not pay $50 for it.Action was good. The neck was flat; it should have been fine. I wasn't trying to fix this straight out of the box; I'm sending it back for a refund.I suggest you look at something else or risk having high action on a 12-string cutaway by adjusting the truss rod. NOPE"
    },
    {
        "review": "Took a chance, but sent it back.  Ordered a Fender for just a little bit more..no comparison."
    },
    {
        "review": "I was surprised by the quality of the guitar case. Very good notes. The finish is of very good quality."
    },
    {
        "review": "It arrived today, in great condition and I played it for an hour, heavily,(after tuning) and it held its tuning very well. I had left the sunburst model in my wish list too long and that color had run out, but I settled for a black one and it looks and plays great. I have small hands and to save my sciatic hip, I stand up to play, most of the time but it puts pressure on my neck so I need a lighter guitar. It has a nice balance (the neck is surprisingly on the heavy side but it balances the body very well, IMO. So it arrived by DHL and they kept me well informed of delivery. Love the guitar. I got a good one. ;-) I plugged it into the amp -- no problem. The guitar has a nice tone -- not too deep and not too bright when played acoustically. The price was right and the guitar was excellent, IMO"
    },
    {
        "review": "This guitar is honestly incredible! I needed a 12 string suitable for gigs around the city, not to expensive, not too cheap. Amazing sound, nice small body, extremely playable right out the box.  What's not too like. Similar size to the \"CE\" body Taylor's, so if you're looking for an alternative, here it is!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I wanted a 12 string for a long time and have been looking at a number of different ones. I'm very happy with my desicion to buy this one, it has a nice tone both plugged in and unplugged. I've been playing it for about two weeks now and I am very impressed. If you want a smaller 12 string that sounds great and is comfortable to play I recommend giving this one a try. It is a bit thinner than a 6 string and the neck is very playable. I have no complaints and I am very happy to have this guitar in my collection. I haven't been playing any of my other guitars since I got it. I managed to get it at a discount but even at the full price this guitar is a steel."
    },
    {
        "review": "I like the product"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "Wow, really impressed with this guitar, for the price it's a sleek and handsome instrument, doesn't sound as deep and bassy as a conventional acoustic, the tone is different, more bright, and it's still full of natural volume and I like the stylish soundhole that looks like a flame. My 3 year old has a penchant for putting objects into the soundholes of guitars and I hope this will prevent that behavior! I haven't tried the electronics on this yet. It's relatively lightweight and of a manageable medium size, thin body, easy to play and tune and seems to stay in tune well. I'm really going to enjoy using this.Oh, all the accessories too!!(In my photo I have a strap on it purchased separately, it's not the one that came with it).The beautiful color choices were tempting but I ended up going with clean bright white for maximum visual impact.Also the travel case it comes with is good quality and comfortable to wear on your back so the guitar is easily portable for the roving troubador. This is my first time owning a carbon fiber guitar, so it feels like a new adventure. I think this would be a great guitar for people who live in humid environments because the inside won't get that musty smell like wooden acoustics do."
    },
    {
        "review": "I bought this 4lb guitar when I learned I was supposed to have surgery and would not be able to lift more than 5 pounds for a couple of months. I’ve had it for about a month or so, and I’m still getting used to it, but it has become my main road guitar. It plays as acoustic or amplified and I’m loving that it’s carbon fiber so I can leave it in the hot truck and not worry about it. The sound isn’t amazing, but it’s good enough for jams and open mics. It’s super light, super cute and the price was easy on the pocketbook. I still have not figured out all of the effects or the app, but I really haven’t tried. Definitely recommended."
    },
    {
        "review": "A great guitar, especially for the price. Tuning machines keep in tune. The sound is great. There's one thing need to be aware of is the electric one, the saddle was cut to be mass produced for every guitar however the adjustment was not made to accommodate the extra (2mm) height of the pickup wire under the saddle. Caused the action to be too high. I tried cutting/sanding down the saddle, didn't do it exactly correct and needed another saddle. Contacted Enya, and the shipped another saddle out to me, completely free of charge. Great customer service.Other than that, great guitar, great sound, comes with a nice extra set of string. And certainly durable. Dropped it and scratched it a little and still works great. Light weight, great guitar on the go, and great for a first/beginner guitar. The price and value, can't go wrong."
    },
    {
        "review": "I've had this guitar for awhile now and I'm not sure if I would purchase it again.For the price I think putting the money toward a Tonewood amp may have been a better choice for me.I still enjoy this guitar and it does get played.The good points:It has good build quality, the frets are smooth and level, tuners are decent, strings are good, gig bag is nice.Sound is full for such a small acoustic and it holds tune well.You can connect your phone to the guitar and play backing tracks through the internal amp.Guitar also sounds good connected to an amp.The guitar feels durable and is built for travel, I have no fear of damaging it or having a problem due to environmental changes.The areas needing improvement:The action was extremely high, there is an inspection ticket stating the action at less than half of what it actually measured. The saddle had to be sanded to lower the action to the correct height, after that the guitar plays great.The electronics need the app to be usable, out of the box settings you can hardly here any effects coming from the guitar. I had to increase the volume using the app to set the level, not a big deal but should be set higher from the factory as you can adjust the volume down with the knob.Effects are not that great, app adjustability is limited and you are stuck with only the 4 effects. They should have added the ability to choose different effects and create your own patches. Digital processors are cheap and some more usable effects would have been nice.Overall it's a nice travel guitar (after adjusting the action) it sounds bigger than it is.The internal amp is nice even with the limited effects (after adjusting levels with the app).The App connects well and is easy to use, the ability to play backing tracks through the guitar adds to the fun and is a good tool for practice.Keep in mind the guitar may need some setup to play nice like mine did, if you cannot do this yourself it will be an added cost."
    },
    {
        "review": "I have 3 Enya caron fiber guitars and I love all of them. Most recently I bought the NOVA GO SP1 acoustic which I will say I had reservations with, I was a little hesitant about its durability, size and how it would sound, but all for naught as it doesn’t dissapoint in build quality, appearance or sound characteristics. I had been learning to play, on and off for quite a few years, sometimes ardently commited to it, other times half heartedly, but always loving the instrument and loving to “play”. Regardless of if I was any good at it, after all, the only one I had to please or entertain was myself. I was finally getting to a point where I was feeling confident, like I was finally “getting it” when unfortunately, at about that same time, I developed a health condition which caused me chronic pain and to become fatigued very quickly. The weight and size of my guitars was intolerable, so I begrudginly had to give it up completely. I am so grateful to have recently seen these carbon fiber guitars on Amazon, I was intrigued by the positive reviews and all the comments regarding their weight and convenience. I figured I would give them a try even with my low stamina and back pain issues. I started with the XMari and loved it, then the Go Sonic, loved that too, and now the SP1. Enya hit the trifecta on these, the light weight and convenience have allowed me to pick up the thing I loved so much again and the excellent quality of the looks, construction, functionallity and sound is the icing on the cake. I would reccomend these to anyone, from beginner or hobbyist to seasoned pro. I can’t say enough good about all of the Enya products, the kind of quality and value that is a rarity in todays world."
    },
    {
        "review": "Good starter guitar. Does not need an amp for playing, however when playing with an amp I hear a lot of static."
    },
    {
        "review": "Quality: Good quality materialsDurability: Quite sturdyAppearance: Premium look, minimalist & gothicAction: Low action, and still adjustableNeck: Neck is nice, a bit small for me but manageableSound: Not that impressive when speaker is off. But quite nice when speaker is on and with effects.Overall: Very nice product. Very durable, portable, weather proof, and has premium look. Easy 4.5⭐️ to 5⭐️"
    },
    {
        "review": "This guitar is an amazing addition to my collection of budget guitars that hit way above their price point! With it's all fiber carbon body, it's resistant to temp changes, it's extremely lightweight, and very portable!I love the case it came with, and even the strap is perfectly fine here! I love the built in speaker with the 4 different effects! You can Bluetooth to it, so you can actually play to a back track if you wanted to. There's an app for it I haven't checked out yet, but I'm sure it is full of extra goodies for this 21rst century guitar! You get a pretty full, deep sound out of this little guitar, and I dare say it is amazing when plugged into an amp! I got this on sale for 224.99, and it is worth every penny! My only concern is the inevitable day the batterie inside it dies, and how I go about having that replaced, but that should be a good 5 years away or so, and I'll cross that bridge when I get there. For me, this was a self-bought birthday present I picked up and added to my personal collection that does not disappoint! If you've been on the fence about buying one, my advise is to take the plunge and you wont want to look back! A truly exceptional guitar!"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "This is so much more than a travel guitar. The looks, feel, playability and sound are superb for this instrument. Worth every penny. Well made and a guitar you don't want to put down. I was pleasantly surprised. I got it for a trip where I didn't want to bring my main guitar and it is a keeper."
    },
    {
        "review": "Excellent guitar that is easy to play and maintain. The body size makes it much easier to hold than some travel guitars. I like its tone and resonance. The intonation is as close as can get with a standard acoustic design. It stays in tune very well. Considering the price it can’t be beat!I love to modify many of my instruments; every thing from changing string type to installing pickups, etc.. This guitar will be an excellent platform for modifications. However, I like it the way it is, so I will start with non-permanent mods. First experiment will be to convert it into a jazz box by changing to flat wound strings and adding pickups. (A thin jazz pickup would be ideal if I can find away to fit it but other types can be more easily placed.) This guitar has strong, but nice resonance; however, I might add foam internally to tame the resonance and make it softer.Don’t forget the great weather resistance, I will definitely take it camping.What fun!!!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I bought this guitar to take with me on camping trips where I'll be leaving it in a hot car, or possibly exposing it to moisture and the elements.  I took it with me on a car camping weekend and it worked very well for my purposes.  After the initial tuning it took a few days of adjusting, but it more or less kept in tune after a while.  It sat in a car that got well over 100 degrees, sat out in the wood, probably got a little damp at some point, and kept playing as if it hadn't.All that said, this guitar does not sound very good.  It's not awful sounding, but it won't replace even a cheaper wooden guitar.  This is a cheap beater instrument that is small, portable, and probably indestructible.  I don't recommend it as a first guitar or for performing, but for taking for a hike or sitting around in hot or cold weather it's great."
    },
    {
        "review": "I’ve been playing guitar for over 15 years and had no idea what to expect since I’ve never played a carbon fiber guitar before (didn’t even know it was a thing till now).  What made me try it was the price (terrifyingly reasonable), seeing all the glowing reviews, and because I teach guitar lessons to adults/kids and wanted to see what my students’ experience with it is. The low price made me skeptical primarily about how well this holds its tune which is the problem with most beginner, cheap guitars.If you’re serious about learning guitar, I fully recommend investing in this guitar as a better beginner guitar. Mind you I really enjoy playing this and I’ve been playing for awhile as I mentioned before. If you go too cheap, like the adm brand for kids (which we have for my 3 year old), you will find they can’t hold their tune past 5 mins of playing which is critical for beginners to develop their ear. That’s because the quality of materials just isn’t there and where they skimp on quality affects playability. Thats not the case here. I can tell this thing is thoughtfully designed. The video I took of me playing is same day it was delivered. It literally was ready to play, all I had to do was tune it a couple times to get the strings used to the tension and then it held its tune which was the real test for me!This guitar is heavier than I expected for the size but is still lightweight overall. The carbon fiber means this thing is as durable as it gets for a musical instrument, you can feel the this guitar is built solid. I was also hesitant about the sound hole difference with this one as they essentially moved it right next to the player in a side hole and it projects surprisingly well. I was surprised how loud I was able to get this thing. Not the same as a typical guitar but that’s not a knock, it’s just designed differently.Overall, absolutely worth it for the price. Excellent beginner guitar or if you want something very portable to play out."
    },
    {
        "review": "This little guitar packs quite the sound for such a small instrument and at an affordable price point. I purchased it as a gift for my son who is quite picky and tends to stick to his expensive Martin. However, he opened up this guitar, quickly tuned it and played for almost an hour. He was surprised at the sound, and his only complaint was that the frets needed to be sanded a bit. It is very well made, sturdy and tuned quickly and held it's tune. He is a pretty extreme traveler, from hiking in Alaska to California road trips and I wanted him to be able to have a sturdy, dependable guitar that would stand up to his travels. It is easy to set up, has a nice fit when holding , and best of all has a beautiful tone. The one piece carbon fiber body is solid and has a super cool design. Highly recommend!!"
    },
    {
        "review": "More like a toy thana real guitar. Sounds like the plastic guitar my Dad got me when I was 4 years old. Would be good for a child starting to learn to play perhaps, but there are much better options for less money.(Leo Jaymez)"
    },
    {
        "review": "So, my expectations were not high at this price point, but the reviews made me decide to give it a try.  My advice - click the buy button now!  This little guitar plays well above its weight.  I had tried the guitar version from another company that specializes in outdoor carbon fiber Ukes, and did not keep that instrument - strings broke when you brought them up to tune, and disappointing sound, to say the least.  This one - my wife is fighting me to get to play it!  It arrived VERY well set up, sounds great, holds tune, a nice well padded case, accessories included, and a very cool little guitar!!"
    },
    {
        "review": "New to trying to learn guitar this guitar feels great in the hands light weight and so far is staying in tune . My guitar teacher said it sounds decent for it’s size was really impressed how well it actually plays . All I did was tune it right out of the box so no paying for setup or tweaking was needed so I highly recommend this guitar ."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "This thing is amazing. It was smaller thanI thought it would be, but that wasn’t the only thing that surprised me. The sound is impressive. For 10 watts, the depth and richness is very impressive. The amp only operates at 5 watts when on battery power but the sound is still great. I love the portability of being able to play on battery power. It’s very fun when using to practice or playing for a few friends. Also the Cubase software it comes with is amazing. It is very powerful and is a quality DAW for hat is very capable if you are recording. This amp does one thing and does it well - it’s the best amp for acoustics and if you want a natural sound that’s just louder, than this is for you."
    },
    {
        "review": "Gotta say this unit is UNIQUE, the way it models microphone's for Acoustic play is really 2nd to none. Along with true Mic modeling that is pretty true to the microphones emulated, it also has a great selection of other features like delay, reverb  and compression and chorus. I have purchased other \"portable amps\" for acoustic play. They had larger speakers and might have been a little louder but there they lacked the ability to enhance the tone, they were just louder. Some I purchased for much more money, but wound up in a sound test within 5 minutes returning them. For a portable unit that really gives you the best acoustic modeling and tone, this unit cannot be beat, especially for its size."
    },
    {
        "review": "I love this Yamaha amp.  It sounds great.  I use it primarily for practice with my GS mini but I’ve also used it for small get togethers.  It has a convenient handle on top making it easy to carry and it just looks really cool.  Bonus you can also use batteries to power it."
    },
    {
        "review": "Quite impressed with this tiny \"acoustic\" amp! If you simply want to loop tracks you create, for practice, as I do, and amplify your studies with a bit of reverb or delay, this 12\" long amp is a great buy! Well made, lovely sound, and with a number of acoustic settings and tone controls. You won't be performing with this, except for a few friends in a quiet context, or just practicing at home, but it is more than adequate for this, and provides sufficient range and a nice variety of tone colors. Haven't used the Cubase software yet, so I won't offer any thoughts there."
    },
    {
        "review": "Remember this is 5 watts. However, its a very clean, sound isn't distorted, it's easy to transport, it has built in features like reverb, chorus etc. that do not require you to carry pedals. Its a parlor practice amp that will allow you to busk on the corner if necessary with it battery power feature. If you have AC power nearby it will work there too. Having it plugged in will not charge your batteries. It's great for travel, in the park, or assisting your writing music. You can also play mp3 music through it with the aux out. If you need to practice in silence use the headphone jack or send music out to a mixer in a bigger environment."
    },
    {
        "review": "awesome!!!"
    },
    {
        "review": "It's not only for acoustics -- I use the THR5A as a home/practice amp for my electric guitar.  Since I play only in a jazz style and hate overdrive distortion, the amp works really well.  I don't use the recommended \"EG Clean\" setting; I use the \"Condenser Mic\" setting, which gives just the right tone for jazz guitar by helping to preserve the acoustic sonorities of my archtop while at the same time amplifying them.  And when I play with a Stratocaster it gives roughly the same effect, creating the illusion that the Strat is a hollow-body -- great for guitaring with headphones while others are sleeping.  (When you set it to \"EG Clean\" the \"Blend\" knob becomes a gain knob, so you can get a bit of overdrive that way. Works okay; I simply don't play in that style.)The Hall Reverb is excellent (though you don't get the variability you'd get with a pedal), and the Compression and Chorus effects are serviceable.  I don't use the Delay, but it works well enough.Portability is another boon.  I use it with rechargeable AA batteries and carry it to wherever I can find some peace in the house.My only complaint is that when I put the amp on the wood floor and play with a backing track, it sometimes distorts (perhaps with feedback from the floor vibrations?).  Generally, you can't expect great sound from this amp at high volume.  But then that's not what it's for.If you're looking for a case, try the Neewer Large Size SLR DSLR Camera and Video Bag with Shoulder Strap.  Perfect fit, and much cheaper than the Yamaha case."
    },
    {
        "review": "I'm using this primarily for ukulele.  And wow *THIS* is it.  I'd tried a few before arriving at this little gem of an amp.  What I was looking for was a decent acoustic practice amp for home or small groups that was portable and could take batteries.  I tried the Blackstar Fly3 but it was just not as clean sounding - I could get close just using my electric guitar Vox 10 amp if I tweaked the settings enough.  So I came across the THR series of amps and decided to give the THR10C a try which is geared towards Jazz thinking the cleaner output would lend itself well to nylon.  It's a really nice amp but still too electric sounding.  That's when I discovered they made a acoustic specific amp, this THR5A.  Not only does it have a \"nylon\" specific setting (which I actually do not play in all of the time), but it has mic models rather than amp models.  There is a mixer that blends straight pickup with the mic model.  And if you wanna get a little funky, it also has some reverb and hall effect.  The compression effect is great to tighten up the sound on strumming so it sounds cleaner.  The big thing missing from its THR10 cousins is the ability to memorize settings but I'm ok with that.  Also, I understand that the THR5 series puts out the same power as the THR10 series which I was afraid coming from the 10C I would give up some volume but not the case.Definitely consider the case from Yamaha made for this series of amps.  It was designed to fit the longer THR10 series but the extra space with the THR5A just gives you more room for gear.  Also grab 2 sets of 8x AA batteries.  A single set of 8x AA lasts quite a while actually but its always nice to have a backup because charging them can take a while.  I use Eneloop as recommended but I use those batteries for everything anyway (beware of defects being sold on ebay for cheap).Why the missing star?  On BOTH my 10C which I returned for the 5A, NEITHER included this Cubase AI recording software DVD ROM.  I thought maybe it was a download but the only thing on the Yamaha site was the editor software (to edit models) and USB drivers."
    },
    {
        "review": "Excellent product, the sound it produces is incredible for such a small thing, the digital effects are also excellent, perhaps it would have been worth adding something for the thr 10 but all in all the settings are more than sufficient, I consider myself extremely satisfied and I recommend it to all those who want excellent effects for home use"
    },
    {
        "review": "In the description it said that it did not have an eliminator but it arrived complete."
    },
    {
        "review": "I fot this for my Yamaha YEV-104 electric violin and it's pretty good for me because I do not intend to be playing outside, so it's plenty loud for my needs. I like the choice of amplifier types (condenser, tube, dynamic and nylon) and the effects are nice (Reverb, etc.) I wish it would be bluetooth, but I bought a cable to plug my iphone and play backing tracks, and it sound great. The sound with the headphone is also great.I was quite shocked when I first saw it, because it's so tiny, but it deliver great results. It's cute too, with the retro look and orange light inside. At first I was disapointed that it does not have an internal battery, but I when an internal battery fail, it's very hard to have a replacement, so I think I prefer it that way. I will buy rechargeable battery. It's so small that it's very portable. Should be cool to take anywhere.The warranty is only one year and it's made in China, so I hope it will last more than one year."
    },
    {
        "review": "Sounds, setting options, appearance. I would buy it again immediately. Top!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Exceptional quality. It goes very well with an acoustic guitar, with its great features. Robust manufacturing, easy to handle and transport."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "Awesome guitar! Easy to play, has a built in tuner is beautifully finished and sounds amazing through a matched Fender Acoustisonic amp. Was priced like it was stolen!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I ordered this model Fender Guitar for my 86 year old husband as his Christmas present.  Giving him this beautiful surprise present at Christmas brought joy to our family.  My husband had admired our son's guitar that is this same model and said how much he would love to have one just like it.  At Christmas when my husband first received the guitar I wrote an \"outstanding rave\" review about the guitar.  Less than 2 months later I am back with very bad news.  My husband complained to our son that he was having difficulty tuning his guitar.  Our son checked it out and discovered the problem!  The 6th string (E), 2nd, 3rd and 4th frets have sunk into the keyboard there by making the guitar untunable.  The guitar sounds horrible!  My husband is very disappointed and his Christmas gift has turned into a joyless, sad and expensive mistake.  Fender Company can you help us?July 16, 2023-  Since I wrote my original evaluation about the guitar I bought my Husband, the Fender Guitar Company has come to my rescue.  They have made everything right.  I found them very responsive to my problem and more than willing to help.  My husband now loves playing his beautiful guitar with the fabulous tone."
    },
    {
        "review": "I was not disappointed in any way with this guitar. It's my northern guitar for when we're home in Massachusetts.  I leave the Gibson in Florida. The fended is way less expensive, but the quality is there, and the sound is excellent - after a couple of tweaks.When the guitar arrived, I knew it'd need SOME work, so I was ready. I'd ordered a set of BONE pins, nut, and saddle. When I immediately replaced the strings upon the guitar's arrival, I fitted the bone components. If you have an inexpensive guitar, it's likely that these pieces are composite. Replace them with bone! Replace them with bone! Replace them with bone. This (https://www.amazon.com/gp/product/B07CQSZQZL/ref=ppx_yo_dt_b_search_asin_title?ie=UTF8&psc=1) is the one I used. I can guarantee (from other guitars I've owned) the proper nut and saddle increase both volume and sustain.If you don't feel up to the job, take it somewhere and have it done. The neck guitar that I received needed no adjustment, and the new saddle matched the original one perfectly.By the way, the built-in tuner and pickups are worth the extra expense."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is my 3rd Fender in my collection.  I originally played a Les Paul Jr in the 60s. I loved that one, the stratocaster is my choice now. The other is a 3/4 Fender Acoustic guitar,  nylon strings.  Great practice one for me.The CD 60 is farthest in the photo, the built in tuner works great. After initial tuning, the guitar stayed tuned first couple days.  Seems to have a good setup, the tonal quality is excellent and with amp really nice. Kinda warm, not twangy."
    },
    {
        "review": "A significant improvement over my beater, Hohner acoustic. Easy on the hands. Seems to be settling in after practicing on it for about a half hour. Strings definitely were not pre-stretched. Nice sound projection and the body resonates nicely. Sustains a note/chord for long time. It rewards you well sonically when you hit the chords and notes just right.I can't say much regarding the \"setup\" because I am not skilled enough to determine if the neck needs adjustment or anything like that. Looks fine to me when I hold it up closely and look down the neck/frets. The saddle and nut also seem fine, but I will likely replace the saddle and nut as well as the pins with those made of bone for both tone and cool factor. Head stock and tuning knobs all seem fine on close inspection and operation.Sorry to report not much useful info from me on the electronics because I don't have an amp. But one thing of note... Be aware that the built in tuner will not work unless you have it plugged into an amp. I confirmed this with the included Fishman instructions. I thought the tuner would come on anytime I wanted so I could tune it. Nope. Must be plugged in to an amp. Thinking back, it makes sense, because the sound is transduced into an electric signal by the Fishman pickup on its way to the amp.Docked one star in craftsmanship for the sloppy application of stain you see in the pic as well as some minor finish aberrations. The builder(s) just let the stain run down into the sound hole; they didn't actually put it there with intent. It would have only taken 3 seconds to correct this during the staining process. I will fix it with one of those stain pens you can get at any DIY store because my eye automatically goes directly to that spot when I pick it up. Leaving it raw wood would have even looked better. Also noticed some aberrations on the body if I hold it at specific angles. You can detect them as distortions of the glare, but not when looking at it \"normally.\" I cannot feel them when I run my finger over those areas though. Maybe just the clearcoat.All told, this seems like a decent guitar for the price. I am not disappointed, even with the small cosmetic flaws. Hopefully one day I will be able to play good enough to get all the potential out of this guitar.Good luck to everyone learning. Anything worth learning requires effort. At least, that is what I keep telling myself."
    },
    {
        "review": "I love this guitar. At first, I was going to return it because it looked damaged on the pick board. That was just a VERY thin plastic. The guitar is beautiful and sounds and plays beautifully for its price point."
    },
    {
        "review": "I bought the Fender CD-60-SCE. It came in good condition and on time. The action is great right out of the box. And the built in pickup sounds good so far. The built in tuner is also very accurate. About 16 months ago I bought the Fender CD-60S (which is the same thing but without the pickup) it is holding up very well and still sounds great. The only sign of wear is the finish on the neck has worn off a little from my fret hand but it is barely noticeable and not at all an issue. They also stay in tune well. overall REALLY RECOMMEND THESE GUITARS!!!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Even though this carries Fender's name, it's a very cheap and poorly constructed guitar. The high e and the b string buzz. The neck doesn't adjust easily. I waited past the return date so now I'm stuck with it. Now I have to decide if I want to sink more money into it to set it up properly. In the electronics are subpar. The low e tone overwhelms the other five strings. No matter how I adjust my settings. So for example, when you play an e major chord, All you hear is the overwhelming e bass note from the low e string. The other five strings don't ring out. Cleanly. I'm not happy with this purchase."
    },
    {
        "review": "Excellent guitar to get back into it. Easier than an Ovation Celebrity whose neck is thinner (42 mm compared to 45 for the Fender) with my big fingers it's a joy :o). It arrived today. Well done Northerners for sending it to me so quickly (they really are the best ;o))"
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a beautiful guitar and I'm really happy with it. The finish looks fantastic, it feels well made, and it sounds great whether you're practising at home or plugging it into an amp.One thing I noticed straight away compared to cheaper guitars is how much more comfortable it is to play. The strings don't feel as harsh on my fingers, and the guitar was easy to adjust to my preferred setup. That made learning and practising much more enjoyable.I've also had quite a few compliments on how it looks. Both friends and strangers have commented that it's a great-looking guitar, and apparently it makes me look pretty good holding it too, which is a nice bonus.Overall, it's an excellent instrument with great build quality, comfortable playability, and a fantastic appearance. I'd definitely recommend it."
    },
    {
        "review": "For that price you can have a good quality sounding acoustic guitar. Comes with built in tuner so its really a big plus. Build quality is top notch. Looks good overall and feels good as well. Perfect for beginners"
    },
    {
        "review": "The d,b and e strings buzz irritably after few months of usage and you can’t help( maybe try using capo on other frets!)Have watched tons of YouTube videos to try correct this but in vain.Just don’t buy this if you look forward to having a guitar that your kids will play (if you’re 25!) coz it won’t last even half a year.Now, instead of sinking the repair costs I’ll have to look for a new one in the nearby store 🥲"
    },
    {
        "review": "Going from a Segovia to this Fender didn't pose any problems. For a beginner with its easy handle, it allows rapid progression. For me, after all these years of practice, a sound and pleasure always renewed. One thing, the set of strings will have to be changed. This is fine for a start but after that it has to ring. Have fun."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "Amazing quality and performance! I played around with one of these Escape Mark III guitars a while back but didn't have the need for a travel guitar at that time. Now that I have retired, I like to travel a bit more. This guitar fits perfectly into that lifestyle. Well-built with great sound whether you are using the headphone jack for silent playing or through an amp. The two-stage built in amp adjusts the sound to your ears and offers a nice tuner that lines up perfectly with other tuners I have tried. You can also add your phone or tablet for backing tracks, music, etc. No Bluetooth but I like the simpler way anyway. Bluetooth tends to have latency issues, and I have none with this set-up.I use some pretty good headphones, but I have also used the older style Apple earbuds and they sound great. I travel with these in the case but use the better headphones at home.The neck is solid and very comfortable. It's hard to believe you are playing a travel size guitar! I do use a strap for my personal comfort, but the larger body does well in other positions.I have yet to fly with this guitar, but this is really no bigger than my baritone ukulele that has thousands of miles on it. The silent feature is good for me when we are in a hotel and my wife sleeps in. I can still practice without disturbing her. The ukulele was not good for that..."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is the ultimate practice guitar.  At first, I overlooked this guitar because the neck didn't detach and fold like other travel guitars.  Then I realized how perfect this design is and how it doesn't need that feature, which I now think is gimmicky.  I'm shocked this isn't way more popular.1) it's tiny.  My wife and mother-in-law watched me open it and they had to each ask me twice \"THAT's a guitar?!?!\"  You will most definitely have no traveling issues and certainly fit in a carry on, overhead, flight bag.  in fact, it's the same length as my Baritone Ukulele (and hence fit in their cases).  I carry it everywhere: in my bed, in my car, at the park, on a hike, in a boat, with a goat, in a box, with a fox, in a house, with a mouse, here and there, I carry it anywhere!  I do so like this guitar!  Thank you!  Thank you, Traveler!2) despite it's size, it is a 100% full sized 25.5\" scale.  This was very important for me because, as a beginner, I did not want to train on a short scale just to suffer when I picked up a full scale.  Trust me, you want to get used to a full scale and then if you pick up a smaller guitar, it will be easy.  It kind of makes me wonder, why aren't all solid body electric guitars this size??  Seriously, why do they need to be any bigger??3) it's a solid body.  I like this because it's QUIET.  If you want to get better at guitar, you have to practice a lot, but nobody wants to hear a noob practice on a dreadnought all day.  Also, the solid body makes it smaller and more durable for traveling.4) it's an \"acoustic\" style.  This means it uses acoustic strings, which are thicker (including a wound G-string) and at a higher tension than electric strings.  Read: harder to play.  I liked this from a practice perspective.  Again, I think it's important I get accustomed to acoustic strings, so when I play an electric, it's like child's play and a confidence booster.  If you disagree with my theory here, well Traveler also makes electric (EG-1 and EC-1), and nylon classical, versions of this guitar, so the choice is yours.  Note, it uses completely standard guitar strings, so you can replace them with whatever is your favorite.5) it has a built in tuner.  nobody ever talks about this, but I love built in tuners.  One less thing to carry around - especially important when we're talking about travel guitars.  The electric version EG-1 has one too, but not the EC-1.6) it has a built in headphone amp.  Sounds good unplugged, but if you want to jam a little harder by yourself, just plug headphones directly in.  Of course, it has a standard amp output too.7) some have expressed concern about the in-body tuners.  I think they are fabulous and have great tuning stability.  In fact, I think it's even better than headstock tuners that are prone to getting shifted when it brushes up against a wall, your body, or you have young curious kids who turn them when you're not looking.  Why aren't all guitars headless these days??  The tuners are nicely protected in the body.  I find there is ample space to fit your fingers to tune them and the holes do not get in my way, even when I'm playing fingerstyle.  It's slightly awkward crossing your arms to pick a string and tune at the same time, but really not an issue.  The actual tuners seem pretty standard, so I'm sure you can replace with one of your choice, and I noticed Traveler sells a locking tuners upgrade, but I don't think they need them.8) I think the mahogany version looks amazing.  The tales are true that if you are attracted to your guitar, you will want to pick it up and play more.Conclusion:  This guitar will make you play more than any other guitar = will make you better.  IMO, as a beginner, you should be less concerned about tone nuances and more concerned about getting your hours in.  I would definitely consider the EG-1 or EC-1 electric versions in the future.Some minor criticisms:1) now the sound sucks.  But what do you expect with a solid body \"acoustic\"?  You're not going to be gigging in front of a huge audience with this thing.  The sound is solely created from a piezo pickup under the saddle.  From what I've read, piezo pickups on normal acoustic guitars are one of the least desirable ways to record your instrument.  So this is piezo without any acoustic sound to help out.  It sounds weird plugged in (headphones or amp), fake sounding acoustic sound, and it's got some gain/sustain, which is weird for an \"acoustic\".  But sound quality is not why you should be buying this.  Again, if this turns you off, check out their humbucked electric versions, which I assume sound like normal electric guitars.2) the top edge of the body is razor sharp and very uncomfortable on the forearm.  Also note, the black \"binding\" I believe is just a strip of paint for looks.  The back has a rounder edge, so they should really round the top for the next generation.3) there's no pick guard, and it definitely needs it.  I was very light on it in the beginning and it already has tiny scratch marks.  I've seen this happen to others too.  I made my own pick guard with clear packing tape, which works very well.  I highly recommend everyone make a pick guard before they start playing."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a great guitar (my first travel electric). It plays nice. The body is big enough to rest my forearm on it. Its not neck-heavy, yet light and balanced. Only 4 stars on \"thickness category\" because the neck could be a little thinner but no complaints here. A sales point for me was that I could plug my phone into it and play Ultimate Guitar app, YouTube or whatever to play through the amp. The 3 dot tuner doesn't compare to a Snark (which clamps nicely on the stubby head). I coupled this with a Blackstar 3W amp. Everything fits nicely in my van."
    },
    {
        "review": "I don’t write a lot of reviews, but I am so impressed with this guitar I feel I owe it to Traveler to comment on it. You really need to buy this guitar if you are an acoustic guitar player.  It has a full size acoustic neck, but sounds like a non-amplified electric, so it doesn’t annoy others when practicing the same licks over and over again.  I find I practice so much more because of this. Of course you can plug it into an amp or plug in earbuds if you want to hear a full sound. I bought it for travel because I am always short on cargo space in my vehicle.  This takes up about a 1/4 of the space my other acoustics take up with a hardshell case.  Didn’t expect the quality of this guitar.  Very solid and well crafted.  The engineering to make it compact is genius."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a well crafted guitar. Light, great action, built in tuner, very light and portable."
    },
    {
        "review": "I'm a beginner/intermediate guitar player and wanted a low profile guitar for traveling. I was a bit skeptical and watched a lot of YouTube and read a lot of reviews and finally decided on this guitar. I have to say that I was pleasantly surprised and 100% satisfied when I received it.  For it's small size, it's a heavy instrument. But this is a solid body, beautiful work of art and engineering. It's not much of an acoustic guitar, but with the built-in tuner, electronics, and headphone jack, it makes a great electric sound. With the treble and base adjustments, and phase shifting, there's so much sounds to play with.  The neck and the body are ascetically perfect and clean. I love to hold it in my hands, even if I'm not playing. And that's saying a lot. This is a beautiful instrument, very functional, and a lot of fun. If I had to complain, I would say it's a bit heavy, but that solid wood body is a great trade off. I'll take the extra weight and still give it five stars. Weather you're looking for a traveling guitar or just an enthusiasist, you need one of these. Highly recommend. And 100 percent real deal."
    },
    {
        "review": "I have owned many piezo pickup acoustics and this one sounded really thin and bad even after working with an EQ pedal for a while.  It looks great and like it is well made.  It was almost perfectly in tunes out of the box, I just thought it sounded week especially with single note flatpicking.  It might work pretty well if all you are looking to use it for is strumming chords."
    },
    {
        "review": "I am planning a trip and I have my guitar, as I will be playing for a family gathering outside.  So, I need to travel on an airplane with it.  I need portability to the outside venue and I need a good sound.  After reading so many reviews, and looking at options for several hours, I finally decided on the Mark III.  I received it yesterday.  I have been playing it for several hours and I have to say, I love this guitar.  If you plug into a decent amp, the sound is great.  It does not have the full sound of my Fender.  It's a solid body, and it's better than I expected.  Also, it feels the same. With headphones, it is very quiet. I highly recommend the Mark III.  I am also using the Donner Mini Guitar Amp for portability."
    },
    {
        "review": "A marvellous gem. Sitting nicely beside my recliner just to pick up. Very light and comfortable to play. Connected to my phone with a bluetooth adapter for backing tracks and learning.Yes, been mentioned, the sound in the headphones could been a tad louder, but as is it is gentle to the ears,and headphone amps have been invented if needed. With somewhat decent headphones the sound is good enough for rehearsing, but of course it is better thru an amp.Maybe two small things I'd liked. 1, an ambience setting for the headphones. 2. A tad shorter scale length.I measured it towards my Gibson J-15 and the J-15 is nearly 2 frets shorter. But that is probably nit picking.It doesn't stop me from giving it 5 star rating. Love this thing."
    },
    {
        "review": "love this guitar. Perfect for travel and playing in hotels with out disturbing others"
    },
    {
        "review": "Super delighted with this guitar.  I bought it “deaf” without having heard it before.  Just for some videos on YouTube.  Full scale, don't be fooled by the size, already connected it sounds decent.  I never eat a box one but it has its own thing.  Good merchandise."
    },
    {
        "review": "Very aesthetic, light, piezo sound and playability ok. Does the expected job and can be taken anywhere. Plugged into the boss katana, we play folk as well as metal since there is no resonance box. Diverted use of course but it works."
    },
    {
        "review": "Jed"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "Well I don't know much about guitars but this is a very good guitar for ur toddler to learn to strum, it was super cheap, great color, just needed to tune it, sits good on the lap, light weight material,sits good on the shoulder, easy set up, came with a case"
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a really nice starter guitar for beginners, especially for younger players or anyone wanting something smaller and easier to handle. We got this for our 3 year old grandson and he loves whamming away on it trying to play.  The 30-inch size feels more comfortable than a full-size guitar for him. It has a clean look and comes with the basic accessories needed to get started. The sound is really good for a beginner setup and works well for practice. The strings and tuning may need a little adjustment at first, but that's pretty normal. It’s lightweight and easy to carry around. The bundle setup makes it convenient without needing separate purchases. I feel like this is a super good entry-level option to learn on."
    },
    {
        "review": "I got this as a gift for my little brother and he loves it. Remember, you do get what you pay for. This is not as good quality as a higher-end guitar, but it's not bad quality either. The ONLY complaint is that the strings it comes with are very cheap and don't sound good. We swapped them for D'Addario strings and it played so much better. It's made of wood, but I don't know if the neck was sanded down because the edge feels a bit rough. It's a good guitar for beginners, though, and a really good starting point. I would definitely recommend having some better strings ready for it, but other than that it's a great starting point!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Its okayyy i think i just suck at the guitar but i've learned that its pretty easy to tune. my brother got the hang of it so its good for a beginner. very comfortable to hold and i love the black."
    },
    {
        "review": "I have to say that I am impressed. While I must admit that I'm a beginner at guitar (I'm a former drummer that can no longer play due to health issues), I learned a lot about guitars from an old band mate. This beginner guitar sounds nice, and is of a better size for me to learn on. I have an old acoustic, but the body is far too large for me.This guitar arrived a little over an hour ago. No nicks, dings, or cracks on it. Checked the screws on the tuning machines, everything was good. Since the headstock is more of a classical style than a regular acoustic, I took off the steel strings that came on the guitar and replaced them with classical strings (nylon - medium tension). While it's going to take at least a few days for the nylon strings to stretch out enough before they stay in tune, I did get it tuned up for a bit and played a few chords. The guitar sounds wonderful, with a good bit of volume. I think I'll enjoy learning on this guitar.If you decide to get this guitar and go with nylon strings (which you should), unless you go with 'ball end' strings, you might have to look up how to 'tie' the nylon strings onto the guitar, as some nylon strings that you can get will not come with a ball end. I had helped do this on a guitar decades ago, but needed a refresher course, and found the information online easily enough.The reason I say you should use nylon strings is because they require less tension than the normal steel strings. Since this guitar does *not* have a truss rod, you want to go with strings that do not need as much tension. Using steel strings can cause problems with the bridge piece (breaking or coming off), the neck (warping), and the tuning pegs. You might be able to get away with using light or super light tension steel strings, but that's your choice. I'm sticking with the nylon strings. They bring out the sound that I want from this guitar. They're also easier to learn with as a beginner, as they don't hurt your fingertips as much. Yes, it will take longer to build up the callouses on the fingertips, but hey...less pain when learning can be a good thing.But again, for an inexpensive guitar, this one is a good deal. As with any instrument, if you're not sure if you, or the person you get it for, are going to maintain interest in learning the instrument...going with an inexpensive instrument like this is always the best idea.As for the other bits that come in this package...the picks are good, the case is good enough to keep dust off the guitar, the strap is not all that great, and the tuner is pretty much garbage because the battery cover does not want to stay on (I have another one just like it, and it has the same problem), and the pitch pipe is 'meh'. The main thing you want in this package is the guitar. Just get some better strings, a good tuner (I have a 'Snark' that wasn't expensive), and a decent guitar strap...and you're set."
    },
    {
        "review": "Great starter guitar but a half size of a regular one. Smile 😃 kind of a mandolin, ukulele size but still 6 string and tunable.  A great deal for price for a serious beginner or hobbyist."
    },
    {
        "review": "This guitar is good for deciding if you want to commit to learning guitar. The strings and everything work just fine, so one can learn fingerstyle playing and chords, however, the strings are rather buzzy, and in addition with a high action guitar such as this, it is harder on the fingers to play.I used this guitar frequently for about 7 months before deciding that I would like to upgrade to an actual name brand guitar (like Fender, Ibanez, or Rogue). When I would put a regular capo on any fret of the guitar, the low E bass note would become flat, however, with no capo, the string would perform just fine. I went to a guitar center to ask about it, and they hadn't even heard of this brand of guitar.Final Verdict:Yes, the guitar is good value of money if you are looking to see if a guitar would be the right pursuit for you. Looks-wise, the guitar looks nice with a mix of basic and elegant. However, I'd only recommend buying this guitar if you are looking to see if guitar is the right fit for you. If you are looking to buy a guitar that will last you a while, you are better off looking elsewhere as this guitar is better for a trial run rather than a long-term commitment."
    },
    {
        "review": "We just got this Guitar today.  We opened the box and were very impressed with the appearance!  This is a gift for our granddaughter and she loves purple.  This guitar is the perfect color with a beautiful gloss finish.  There are other colors a person can choose from. The guitar has wire strings which is what we were looking for. It comes with a strap, a cover, extra strings, pics, and tuner.  It sounds great and arrived in perfect condition.  We can't wait to give it to our granddaughter. This guitar was priced very reasonable, is very durable, and comes with materials that will help any beginner get started.  I would suggest that you purchase a solid case to protect this beautiful guitar."
    },
    {
        "review": "Had to return as I had ordered it for myself (an adult) and because it’s not well listed, it’s actually a child sized guitar.Was very disappointed, as I really wanted a pink guitar."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "At 63, I decided to try and teach myself guitar to play in church. Got some lessons and a cheap $150 acoustic. It was difficult going. A friend suggested if I was serious, investing in a quality instrument would keep me from getting discouraged. My Ibanez AEG70L arrived today. I was amazed at the quality of craftsmanship. It is well made and beautiful. To my novice ear it sounds great and the ease of playability is wonderful. Tuned it up fresh out of the box, and was ready to go. Well worth the mild investment. I am enjoying lessons and practice time immensely now!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Beautiful guitar, beautiful sound. I was iffy getting a guitar from Amazon because I couldn't hold it and play it first but this is a good size, the wood on the neck feels really nice, and it has a beautiful sound"
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a good guitar and would recommend it for a beginner / novice level player but you probably have that figured out if you are looking at this review ;-)"
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a beautiful guitar with a beautiful sound! This guitar is very easy to tune and does not require constant adjustments during use."
    },
    {
        "review": "Great little guitar! Sounds amazing! It rings like a much more expensive guitar. Very clear plugged in. No static.On-board tuner is a great feature."
    },
    {
        "review": "This guitar is beautiful instrument.  The action is really nice and the neck is super playable.  It is thinner so you do not get deep base tones, definitely on a bit on the \"tin\" side.  But the playability and built in preamp more than make up for it.  The built in tuner is really nice too.  When you turn on the tuner, it turns off the pickups.  A really nice guitar for gigs."
